1900:
** ἐπέκεινα**,
_adverb_, for ἐπ᾽ ἐκεῖνα, opposed to ἐπὶ τάδε {{Refs|(Pl. “Phd.” 112b)}}, **on yonder side, beyond,** with _genitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Hecat. cited in Str. 12.3.23; τοῦ Ἡρακλείου ἐ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.1.10; οἱ ἐ. Τίγριδος καὶ Εὐφράτου 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.8.8; ἐ. ἐλθεῖν Διονύσου _farther than..,_ 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.1: _metaphorically_, ἐ. τῆς οὐσίας ὑπερέχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 509b; ἐλπίδος ἐ. 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 9.5.}}
**2** with _Article_, τὸ ἐ., _Attic dialect_ τοὐπ., or τὰ ἐ., _Attic dialect_ τἀπ., the **part beyond,** the **far side,** τὰ ἐ. τῆς Εὐρώπης {{Refs|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.115, cf. Th. 6.63, etc.}}; τοὐπέκεινα τῆσδε γῆς **beyond** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|E. “Hipp.” 1199; Πίνδου τε τἀπ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 257, cf. X. “HG” 5.1.10; _absolutely_, οἱ ἐκ τοῦ ἐ. [prev. author] “An.” 5.4.3; ἐν τῷ ἐ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.58; τῶν νόθων [ἡδονῶν] εἰς τὸ ἐ. ὑπερβάς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 587c; τὸ ἐ. τοῦ νοῦ 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Sententiae ad intelligibilia ducentes” 25, Jul. “Or.” 4.132c.}}
**II** of past Time, οἱ ἐ. χρόνοι the times **beyond** or **before, earlier** times, {{Refs|Isoc. 6.41}}; οἱ ἐ. (i.e. τῶν Τρωϊκῶν γενόμενοι) {{Refs|[prev. author] 9.6.}}
**II.2** of future Time, **henceforth,** {{LXX|LXX.1Mac.10.30, Thd. “Su.” 64.}}

1901:
** ἐπεκ-τείνω**,
**stretch,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.10 (_passive_)}}, +others; **extend,** [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|τὸ αὔταρκες] ἐπὶ τοὺς ἀπογόνους 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1097b12}}:— _passive_, **to be extended, extend,** {{Refs|[prev\. author] “Ph.” 217b9, etc.}}
**2** _intransitive_, **extend,** {{Refs|ἐπὶ πλέον [prev\. author] “APo.” 96a24}}; of a people or country, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|μέχρι.. 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 8.3.11}}: with _dative_, **extend over,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus “in Mete.” 75.12.}}
**3** _passive_, **extend beyond,** [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|τῆς οἰκείας ὥρας Theophrastus Philosophus [{4-3rd c.BC}] “Historia Plantarum” 6.8.4}}]; **reach out towards,** {{Refs|τοῖς ἔμπροσθεν “Epistle to the Philippians” 3.13}}.
**4** Tact., **extend,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|τοὺς ἱππέας 1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 10.20}}.
**5** **expand,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|ἕν νόημα 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ εὑρέσεως” 4.4}}.
**II** **lengthen, prolong,** [{{Refs 1st c.AD+|λόγους Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus [{1-2nd c.AD}] 2.1147}}].
**II.2** **pronounce** a syllable **as long,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphorical” 1014b17.}}
**II.3** **lengthen** a word, by inserting a vowel or otherwise (as πόληος for πόλεως), ἐπεκτεταμένον, opposed to ἀφῃρημένον, {{Refs|[prev\. author] “Po.” 1457b35; also by adding a syllable, in}}_passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “Pron.” 34.5, +others}}
**III** **make more burdensome,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|τὰς προσόδους 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.1.15}}.

1904:
** ἐπέρχομαι**,
_imperfect_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἐπηρχόμην 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.120}} (unless from ἐπάρχομαι: _Attic dialect_ _imperfect_ is ἐπῇα (but see at {ἔρχομαι}) and _future_ ἔπειμι): _aorist 2_ ἐπῆλθον, _Epic dialect_ -ήλῠθον: _perfect_ -ελήλυθα:
**I** **come upon**:
**I.1** of persons, **approach,** with _dative_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.200, 218}}, etc.; especially **come suddenly upon,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.155, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.95: with _accusative_, ἐ. πόλιν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 593}} codices (nisi to be read ἐσ-); **come to for advice,** μάντεις, μοῦσαν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev\. author] “Supp.” 155, “Hel.” 165, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lg.” 772d: with Preps., ἐ. ἐς ποταμόν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.280, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Aj.” 438: _metaphorically_, ἐ. ἐς λόγου στάσιν [prev\. author] “Tr.” 1180; ἐ. ἐς πόλεμον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.47; ἐ. ἐνθάδε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.651}}.
**I.1.b** frequently in hostile sense, **go** or **come against, attack,** _absolutely_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|12.136, +others, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.90, etc.: with _dative_, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.91, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ba.” 736, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.34, etc.: rarely with _accusative_, τμήδην αὐχέν᾽ ἐπῆλθε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.262}}; τὴν τῶν πέλας ἐ. **invade** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.39}}: hence, **visit, reprove,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ταῦτα μέν σε πρῶτ᾽ ἐπῆλθον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 349, compare “Andr.” 688: with a _preposition_}}, **invade,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|ἐπὶ τὴν οἰκίαν “PFay.” 12.12 (2nd c.BC).}}
**I.1.c** **come forward to speak,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Or.” 931, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.119, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lg.” 850c; ἐ. ἐπὶ τὸν δῆμον, ἐπὶ τοὺς ἐφόρους, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.97, 9.7; ἐπὶ τὸ κοινόν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.90}}; τοῖς Αακεδαιμονίοις {{Refs|[prev. work] 91.}}
**I.1.d** in Law, **proceed against,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|ἐπί τινα “PEleph.” 3.3 (3rd c.BC); ἐπί τινα περί τινος “PAmh.” 2.96.8 (3rd c.AD); τινὶ περί τινος “POxy.” 489.11 (2nd c.AD); ἐπὶ πιττάκιον}} **impugn**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1167.14 (1st c.BC)}}: also in _aorist 1_ {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|ἐπελεύσασθαι “PStrassb.” 35.25 (2nd c.BC), etc. (ἐπιπορεύομαι (which see)}} is more common in the _present_ in the Hellenistic period.)
**I.2** of events, conditions, etc., **come upon,** especially **come suddenly upon,** with _accusative_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|μιν.. ἐπήλυθε νήδυμος ὕπνος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.793, +others, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.141; ἔρως γὰρ ἄνδρας οὐ μόνους ἐπέρχεται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “fragment” 431}}: with _dative_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|τοῖσιν ἐπήλυθε νήδυμος ὕπνος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.311, compare 5.472; μοι νοῦσος ἐπήλυθεν 11.200}}; βροτοῖσιν.. [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|ὅταν κλύδων κακῶν ἐπέλθῃ Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Persae” 600, compare “Ag.” 1256; ἐπῆλθέ μοι πάθος Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Leges” 811d}}], etc.
**I.3** with _dative_ _person_, **come into** one's **head, occur to** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἵμερος ἐπειρέσθαι μοι ἐπῆλθε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.30}}; ὅ τι ἂν ἐπέλθῃ Latin _quicquid in buccam venerit,_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.24: _impersonal_}} with _infinitive_, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|καί οἱ ἐπῆλθε πταρεῖν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.107, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Tr.” 134 (Lyric poetry); ἐμοὶ τοιαῦτ᾽ ἄττα ἐ. λέγειν Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Gorgias” 485e, etc.; also ἐπέρχεταί με λέγειν [prev\. author] “Phd.” 88d}}].
**I.4** **come in,** of revenues, etc., ἐπερχόμενοι τόκοι, ἐπιβολαί, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 155.11 (2nd c.AD), 1049.16 (4th c.AD).}}
**II** of Time, **come on,** ἐπήλυθον ὧραι the season **came round again,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.107}}, etc.; also, **come on, be at hand,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|νὺξ δ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ἐπῆλθε 14.457; γῆρας ἐ. 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 528, 728; ἕκαθεν ἐπελθὼν ὁ μέλλων χρόνος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).7}}; τὸ παρὸν τό τ᾽ ἐπερχόμενον πῆμα and **that which is coming, the future,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Pr.” 98.}}
**II.2** **come in after** or **over** another, of a second wife, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.41.}}
**III** **go over** or **on** a space, **traverse,** mostly of persons, with _accusative_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|πολλὴν γαῖαν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.268; ἀγρόν 16.27; ἄγκεα πολλά 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.321, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.139, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.30}}; **go the round of, visit,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|δόμους 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1297; ναοὺς χοροῖς [prev\. author] “Ant.” 153}} (Lyric poetry); πόλιν, of a god, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD(?): Macedonius Epigrammaticus “Pac.” 29}}; of an officer, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|πύλας φυλακάς τ᾽ ἐπῆλθον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 699; τὰς ξυνωμοσίας ἐπελθών 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.54}}; **walk on** ice, {{Refs|[prev\. author] 3.23; also of water, ἐπέρχεται ὁ Νεῖλος τὸ Δέλτα}} **overflows** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.19, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supp.” 559 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.89.}}
**III.2** **go through** or **over, discuss, recount,** with _accusative_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “fragment” 160.4 codices 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Eq.” 618}}; **review,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|τὰ εἰρημένα περί τινος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1172b8; also ἐ. περί τινος [prev\. author] “Ph.” 189b31}}, +others; followed by an _interrogative_, {{Refs|πειρατέον ἐπελθεῖν τίνες.. [prev\. author] “Pol.” 1289b24}}; πῶς δεῖ.. ἐπέλθωμεν συντόμως {{Refs|[prev. work] 1317a15.}}
**III.3** **accomplish,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|πολέμῳ καὶ διαχειρίσει πραγμάτων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.97}}.
**IV** **come up to, imitate,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|πάτρῳ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 6.46}}.

1905:
** ἐπερωτ-άω**,
_Ionic dialect_ **ἐπειρ-**, **consult, inquire of,** with _accusative_ _person_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|τὸ χρηστήριον, τὸν θεόν, εἰ.. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.53, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.118, etc.; τινὰ περί τινος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32}}, cf. Oracle texts cited in {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 43.66; later, ἐν τῷ θεῷ Vetus Testamentum Graece redditum LXX.Judg.18.5}}:—_passive_, **to be questioned, asked** a **question,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.45, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sph.” 250a.}}
**2** with _accusative_ _of things_, **ask** a question, ταῦτα, τάδε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.30, 55, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.10}}; also, **ask about** a thing, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|τὰς ναῦς] καὶ τὸν πεζόν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.100}}; σμικρόν τι τῶν ῥηθέντων **call** it **in question,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Prt.” 329a; ἐ. θυσίαις καὶ οἰωνοῖς ὅ τι χρὴ ποιεῖν}} **inquire** what.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oec.” 5.19; ἐ. ἐς..}} **inquire** about, {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.11.7:—}}_passive_, τὸ ἐπερωτηθέν **the question asked,** variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Tht.” 146e.}}
**3** with _accusative_ _person_ et _of things_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἐ. τοὺς προφήτας τὸ αἴτιον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.93 codices; ἐπηρώτα ὑμᾶς τὸ ἐκ τοῦ νόμου κήρυγμα 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.79}}.
**4** _absolutely_, **put a question,** especially of a chairman putting a question to the vote, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.9, “SIG” 898.17 (Chalcis, 3rd c.AD), +others}}
**5** in Roman Law, **put a formal question** in **stipulatio,** most frequently in _passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 905.19 (2nd c.AD), etc.: also in}}_active_, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|[prev. work] 1273.41 (3rd c.AD).}}
**5.b** hence later, **guarantee,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|PIand. 48.9 (6th c.AD).}}
**6** **ask a further question**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 953.49 (Calymna, 2nd c.BC), +others}}

1906:
** ἐπερώτ-ημα**,
_Ionic dialect_ **ἐπειρ-**, ατος, τό, **question,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.67, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.53, 68, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sent.Vat.” 71.}}
**2** **answer to inquiry** put to higher authority: hence, **sanction,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|κατὰ τὸ ἐ. τῶν Ἀρεοπαγιτῶν “SIG” 856.6 (2nd c.AD), compare 1008.4 (3rd c.AD).}}
**3** = Latin _stipulatio_, **PCair. Preis.**1.16 {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|(2nd c.AD), “Codex Just.” 8.10.12.3 (_plural_)}}: hence probably, **pledge,** {{Refs|συνειδήσεως ἀγαθῆς ἐ. εἰς θεόν “1st Epistle of Peter” 3.21}}.

1907:
** ἐπέχω**,
_future_ ἐφέξω (see. infr. IV. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1, 2) and ἐπισχήσω 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 160, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.88}}: _aorist_ ἐπέσχον, _imperative_ ἐπίσχες, _infinitive_ ἐπισχεῖ; _poetry_ [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἐπισχέθοι Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Septem contra Thebas” 453 (Lyric poetry), ἐπέσχεθον 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1622}}]: _perfect_ {{Refs|ἐπέσχηκα “Supp.epigram” 1.362.12}} (Samos, 4th c.BC):—**have** or **hold upon,** θρῆνυν.., τῷ κεν ἐπισχοίης (variant{ἐπίσχοιας}) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|λιπαροὺς πόδας 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.241, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.410; ποτῷ κρωσσὸν ἐ.}} **hold** it **to** or **for..,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 13.46; λόγον ζωῆς ἐπέχοντες (i.e. κόσμῳ)}} **holding it out** like a torch, {{NT|NT.Phil.2.16:—}}_middle_, **hold by,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|χειρός 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.751}}.
**II** **hold out to, present, offer,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|οἶνον ἐπισχών 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.489; ἐπέσχε τε οἶνον ἐρυθρόν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.444; κοτύλην.. ἐπέσχε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.494}}; εἴ ποτέ τοι.. μαζὸν ἐπέσχον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 83, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andr.” 225; also γάλακτι δ᾽ οὐκ ἐπέσχον οὐδὲ μαστῷ τροφεῖα ματρός}} I **offered** not mother's food **with** my breast, {{Refs|[prev\. author] “Ion” 1492}}: with _infinitive_, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|πιεῖν ἐπέσχον Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Nubes” 1382}}]: _absolutely_, {{Refs|[prev\. author] “Pax” 1167:}} —_middle_, ἐπισχόμενος (i.e. τὴν κύλικα) ἐξέπιεν **having put** it **to his lips,** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phd.” 117c, compare 7th-6th c.BC: Stesichorus Lyricus 7, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.472, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tox.” 37; ἐπὶ χείλεσι.. μαστὸν ἐπισχομένη 3rd c.BC: Euphorio Epicus 92}}; **present** a sum of money, τῇ πόλει {{Refs|“Supp.epigram” [prev. cited]}}
**II.2** **extend, spread out,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|τὴν πλεκτάνην 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 550b6}}.
**II.3** simply, **hold,** {{Refs|σκῆπτρα “IPE” 2.37 (from Panticapaeum)}}; of writings, **contain,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “VS” 2.24.2, compare 2.9.1.}}
**II.4** **enjoin, impose** a task, with _dative_ _person_, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “Arc.” 17, “Vand.” 1.8.}}
**III** **hold** or **direct towards,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἔπεχε τόξον σκοπῷ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.89; ἄλλῳ ἐπεῖχε τόξα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 984}}:— _middle_, _absolutely_, ἐπισχόμενος βάλεν ἰῷ **having aimed at him** he hit him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.15.}}
**III.b** _intransitive_, **aim at, attack,** τί μοι ὧδ᾽ ἐπέχει; why thus **launch out against** me? [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|19.71; in tmesi, ἐπὶ αὐτῷ πάντες ἔχωμεν 22.75; ἀλλήλοις ἐ. {8-7th c.BC}: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 711; ἄνδρα ἐπέχοντα τῷ Πύρρῳ Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus [{1-2nd c.AD}] “Pyrrhus” 16; ἐπέχειν ἐπί τινα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.59; τὰς ἐπὶ σφίσι ναῦς ἐπεχούσας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.105; πρός τι Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus [{1-2nd c.AD}] “Antonius” 66}}]: with _dative_, ἀκτῇσιν ἐπέσχεθον **held straight for** the beach, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1766: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ba.” 1131.}}
**III.2** ἐπέχειν τὴν διάνοιαν ἐπί τινι **direct** one's mind to a thing, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lg.” 926b; τῷ πολέμῳ τὴν γνώμην Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus [{1-2nd c.AD}] “Aemilius Paulus” 8}}], etc.; also ἐ. ἑαυτόν τινι **attend** to him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “R.” 399b codices}}
**III.2.b** _absolutely_, ἐπέχειν (i.e. τὸν νοῦν) **intend, purpose,** with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἐπεῖχε ἐλλάμψεσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.80, compare 153, 6.96:}} with _dative_ _of things_, **to be intent upon,** ταῖς ἀρχαῖς, διαβάσει, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lys.” 490, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.43.2, etc.}}
**III.3** **stand facing, face** in a line of battle, οὗτοι (i.e. οἱ Μῆδοι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἐπέσχον Κορινθίους 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.31}}.
**IV** **hold back, keep in check,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἐπέσχε δὲ καλὰ ῥέεθρα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.244; καὶ πῶς ἐπέσχε χεῖρα μαιμῶσαν φόνο; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 50}}; ἐπισχὼν ἡνίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 847; ἐπίσχωμεν τὸ πλεῖν [prev\. author] “Ph.” 881; ὀργάς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1642; οὐκ ἐφέξετε στόμ; [prev\. author] “Hec.” 1283}}; χρησμοὺς ἐ. **withhold** them, {{Refs|[prev\. author] “Ph.” 866; ἐπέχειν τινὰ τῷ ξύλῳ}} **keep** him **down** with the stick, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1121; τὸ εὐθέως ἐπιχειρεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.33}}; **confine,** as the earth a corpse, [{{Refs 1st c.BC+|“AP” 7.461 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus); ἐ. τῇ χειρὶ τὸ στόμα _cover,_ Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus [{1-2nd c.AD}] “Cat.Mi.” 28; ἐπέχομεν τὴν ἐκπνοήν 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.172; τὰς διαχωρήσεις ἐ. [prev\. author] Vict. _Attic dialect_ 12}}]:—_middle_, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|ἐπισχόμενος τὰ ὦτα Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Symposium” 216a}}]:—_passive_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|τοῦ βάθους ἐπεσχημένου 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 5.1.3}}; **to be prevented, hindered,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|ὑπό τινος “PFreib.” 11.13 (4th c.AD)}}; of the menses, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.184.}}
**IV.b** **stay** or **adjourn** proceedings, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|τὰ πρὸς Ἀργείους 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.46}}; τὴν ζημίαν καὶ τὴν κατασκαφήν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 63; τὴν δίαιταν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.84}}; **suspend** payments, in _passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PTeb.” 337.4 (2nd-3rd c.AD), compare “PGiss.” 48.11 (3rd c.AD).}}
**IV.c** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἐ. τινά τινος _stop, hinder from,?? 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 160, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lys.” 742, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.87}}: with _infinitive_, σε μήτε νὺξ μήτε ἡμέρα ἐπισχέτω ὥστε ἀνεῖναι.. **let** them not **stop** thee so that thou neglect.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.129; ἐ. τινὰ μὴ πράσσειν τι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 517, “Ph.” 349; κλαυθμυρίζον τὸ βρέφος ἐπισχεῖν μὴ δυνάμεναι 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.88}}:—_passive_, μηδενὸς ἐπεχομένου no **objection being taken,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PTeb. 327.37 (2nd c.AD).}}
**IV.d** _impersonal_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|ἐπέχει _there is a hindrance,?? 2nd c.AD: Astrampsychus Onirocriticus “Oracle texts” 97.3}}.
**IV.2** _absolutely_, **stay, pause,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Ἀντίνοος δ᾽ ἔτ᾽ ἐπεῖχε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.186}}; **refrain,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32, 5.51, 7.139; εἰ δ᾽ ἐφέξετον}} if you **tarry,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “El.” 1369, etc.: followed by a _conjunction_, especially in _imperative_, ἐπίσχες ἢν..}} **wait and see** whether.., [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supp.” 397; ἐπίσχες ἔστ᾽ ἂν.. προσμάθῃς Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Prometheus Vinctus” 697; ἐ. ἕως.. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.1; μέχρι τοσούτου ἔως.. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.90}}]; ἐπίσχες, _absolutely_, **hold! stop!** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Ch.” 896, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “OC” 856, etc.; ἐπίσχετε, μηδὲ συρίξητε 4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 2.6D.; ἐπίσχετον, μάθωμεν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 539, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hipp.” 567}}; in _participle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἐπισχὼν ὀλίγον χρόνον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.132}}, +others; τὸ ἐπισχεῖν, opposed to τὸ παραχρῆμα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.73; οὐ πολὺν χρόνον ἐπισχὼν ἧκεν}} came **after a** short **interval,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phd.” 59e; μικρὸν ἐπισχόντα διεφθείροντο}} they very **shortly** died, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “HP” 4.4.13, cf. Diocl.fragment 43; in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.81 οὐκ ἐπέσχον τὸ στρατόπεδον καταλαβεῖν}} **did** not **halt** for the purpose of occupying a camp (unless it, = '_had_ no _intention_ of occupying').
**IV.2.b** with _genitive_ _of things_, **stop** or **cease from,** [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|ἐπίσχες τοῦ δρόμου Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Aves” 1200; τῆς πορείας Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.12; τούτου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.31; also ἐ. περί τινος [prev\. author] 5.32, compare 8.5}}]: so with _infinitive_, **leave off, cease** to do, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Mem.” 3.6.10}}: with _participle_, **cease** doing, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|ἀναλῶν οὐκ ἐφέξεις Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Equites” 915 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ph.” 449.}}]
**IV.2.c** as technical term of the Sceptics, **suspend judgement, doubt,** [{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.1.11, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.387, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “P.” 1.196; ἐ. ἐν τοῖς ἀδήλοις Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus [{1-2nd c.AD}] 2.955c; τὰ ἄδηλα 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.7.5}}].
**IV.3** _middle_, **maintain reserve,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|ἐπείχετο [ἡ σύγκλητος] κατὰ τοὺς Ἀθηναίους 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 30.19.17}} (assuming variant).
**V** **reach** or **extend over** a space, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἐπτὰ δ᾽ ἐπέσχε πέλεθρα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.407}}; ὁπόσσον ἐπέσχε πυρὸς μένος so far as the fire **reached,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|23.238, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.19, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.77, _falsa lectio_ in 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus}} “Aër.” 5, etc.: _aorist_ _middle_, ἐπέσχετο he **lay outstretched,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Th.” 177; βούβρωστις ἐπέσχετο κόσμον _prevailed over..,?? epigram Gr. 793.5 (from Apollonia); ἀφορία ἐ. τὸν βίον 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 44.1}}.
**VI** **have power over, occupy** a country, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|οἱ Σκύθαι τὴν Ἀσίην πᾶσαν ἐπέσχον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.104, compare 108, 8.32, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.101, 7.62}}, etc.; of things, ἐπ᾽ ὀκτὼ μῆνας Κυρηναίους ὀπώρη ἐ. **occupies** or **engages** them, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.199; τὴν πόλιν ἐπεῖχε κλαυθμός Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus [{1-2nd c.AD}] “Otho” 17}}]; ὧν τὰς χρόας τὸ [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|ἡμερινὸν φῶς ἐ. _overspreads,_ Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Respublica” 508c: κραυγῆς ἐπεχούσης τὴν ἐκκλησίαν 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.87; πρὸ τοῦ τὰ σώματα τὰς ὠδῖνας ἐπισχεῖν 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.53}}]: generally, **occupy,** τὴν κρατίστην μοῖραν ἐ. **hold** the foremost place, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 9.1, compare 44.12; ὕλης ἐ. τάξιν “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 3.27; τὴν γῆν κέντρου λόγον ἐπέχουσαν 3rd c.AD: Diogenes Laertius 7.155, compare “Placit.” 3. “Praef.”; τὸν τέλειον ἐ. λόγον 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.160}}; δίκην ἐπέχειν ἡμᾶς φυτῶν we **are like** plants, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Meno Medicus “Iatr.” 6.18.}}
**VI.2** _absolutely_, **prevail, predominate,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἢν μὴ λαμπρὸς ἄνεμος ἐπέχῃ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.96; σεισμοὶ ἐπέσχον ἐπὶ πλεῖστον μέρος γῆς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.23}}; πάντῃ ἐπεῖχε γαλήνη Timo {{Refs 5th c.BC+|63; [τῶν νεῶν] ἐπὶ πολὺ τῆς θαλάσσης ἐπεχουσῶν _being spread over... 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.50; τὴν [τύχην].., ἣ νῦν ἐπέχει 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.253; ἐτησίων ἐπεχόντων 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.5.6}}.
**VI.2.b** of Time, **continue,** [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|τὴν θύραν ἐπεῖχε κρούων Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Ecclesiazusae” 317; ἐπέχων καὶ οὐκ ἀνιείς _continuously,_ Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Theaetetus” 165e; ἐπὶ πλείους ἡμέρας ὁ σεισμὸς ἐπεῖχεν Dio Cassius Historicus [{2-3rd c.AD}] 68.25}}]; σκότος, νὺξ ἐπέσχε, **came on,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Mar.” 20, “Crass.” 30, etc.}}
**VII** _middle_, _future_ ἐπιέξομαι, **meet,** Oracle texts in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Michel” 855.39 (5th c.BC: Magnes Comicus Mae.).}}

1908:
** ἐπηρε-άζω**,
**threaten abusively,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|λέγειν ἐπηρεάζοντες 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.9}}: with _accusative_, **speak disparagingly of,** {{Refs|τὴν ἀγαθὴν ἀναστροφήν “1st Epistle of Peter” 3.16}}.
**II** **deal despitefully with, act despitefully towards,** with _dative_ _person_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Mem.” 1.2.31; ἐ. μοι συνεχῶς καὶ μικρὰ καὶ μείζω 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.14}}, etc.; ἐ. ψηφίσμασι καὶ νόμοις **oppose** them **insolently,** {{NT+4th c.BC+|[prev\. author] 18.320; τινός 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nav.” 27; τινά 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1311a37, NT.Luke.6.28, etc.; εἰ δ᾽ ἄν τις.. ἐπηρειάζεν δέατοι ἰν τὰ ἔργα “IG” 5(2).6.46}} (Tegea, 4th c.BC): _absolutely_, **to be insolent,** [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.8; ὑψηλὴ ῥὶς ὥσπερ -άζουσα διατετείχικε τὰ ὄμματα Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Symposium” 5.6}}]:— _passive_, **to be insulted,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 29.7, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.15, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 36.11, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.52, “PGen.” 31.18 (2nd c.AD):}} —later _middle_ in _active_ sense, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|τινί “PLond.” 3.846.6 (2nd c.AD).}}
**III** of the action of disease, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|διάφορα ἐ. μόρια 7th c.AD: Stephanus Medicus “in Hp.” 1.204 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}

1909:
** ἐπί**,
  Thess. (before τ) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|ἐτ “IG” 9(2).517.14 (3rd c.BC), _preposition_ with _genitive_, _dative_, and _accusative_}}, to denote the **being upon** or **supported upon a surface** or **point.**
**A** WITH GEN.:
**A.I** of Place,
**A.I.1** with Verbs of Rest, **upon,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|καθέζετ᾽ ἐ. θρόνου 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.536; ἧστο.. ὑψοῦ ἐπ᾽ ἀκροτάτης κορυφῆς 13.12; ἐ. πύργου ἔστη 16.700; κεῖται ἐ. χθονός 20.345}}: without a Verb expressed, ἔγχεα ὄρθ᾽ ἐ. σαυρωτῆρος (i.e. σταθέντα) {{Refs|ἐλήλατο 10.153}}; ἔκλαγξαν ὀϊστοὶ ἐπ᾽ ὤμων the arrows **on** his shoulders, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.46; ἐ. γῆς, opposed to ὑπὸ γῆς, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lg.” 728a}}: also with Verbs of Motion, where the subject rests **upon** something, as **on** a chariot, a horse, a ship, φεύγωμεν ἐφ᾽ ἵππων **on** our chariot, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.356; οὐκ ἂν ἐφ᾽ ὑμετέρων ὀχέων.. ἵκεσθον 8.455; ἄγαγε.. δῶρ᾽ ἐπ᾽ ἀπήνης 24.447; ἐπὶ τῆς ἁμάξης.. ὠχέετο 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.31; ἐπὶ τῶν ἵππων ὀχεῖσθαι Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.58; οὓς κῆρες φορέουσι.. ἐ. νηῶν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.528; πέμπειν τινὰς ἐ. τριήροιν Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.56, etc.; ἐπ᾽ ὤμου.. φέρειν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.170}}]; τὴν κλεῖδα περιφέρειν ἐφ᾽ ἑαυτοῦ to carry the key about **on** his person, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Numenius Apamensis Platonicus cited in 4th c.AD: Eusebius Caesariensis Scriptor Ecclesiasticus “PE” 14.7; βαδιοῦνται ἐ. δυοῖν σκελοῖν, ἐφ᾽ ἑνὸς πορεύσονται σκέλους, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Smp.” 190d; ἐπ᾽ ἄκρων ὁδοιπορεῖν}} walk **on** tiptoe, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Aj.” 1230}}; of places, **upon,** if the place is an actual **support,** {{Refs|νέρθε κἀπὶ γῆς ἄνω [prev\. author] “OT” 416}}; ἐ. τοῦ εὐωνύμον **on** the left, ἐ. τῶν πλευρῶν **on** the flanks, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “An.” 1.8.9, 3.2.36}}; but most frequently, **in,** rarely in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ἐπ᾽ ἀγροῦ}} **in** the country. [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.190; γᾶς ἐ. ξένας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1705 (Lyric poetry); νήσου τῆσδ᾽ ἐφ᾽ ἧς ναίει [prev\. author] “Ph.” 613; ἐ. ξένας δμωῒς ἐπ᾽ ἀλλοτρίας πόλεος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 137 (Lyric poetry); οἱ ἐ. Θρᾴκης σύμμαχοι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.35; τοὺς ἐ. τῆς Ἀσίας κατοικοῦντας Isocrates Orator [{5-6th c.BC}] 12.103}}]; ἐπ᾽ οἰκήματος κατίσαι, καθῆσθαι, **in** a brothel, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121.έ, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Chrm.” 163b; τοὺς ἐ. τῶν οἰκημάτων καθεζομένους 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.74; ἐ. τῶν ἐργαστηρίων καθίζειν Isocrates Orator [{5-6th c.BC}] 7.15}}]; μένειν ἐ. τῆς αὐτῶν (i.e. χώρας) remain **in** statu quo, Indut. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.118; οἱ ἐπ᾽ ἐρημίας λῃστεύοντες 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 7.210a}}; later of towns, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|ἐπ᾽ Ἀλεξανδρείας “BGU” 908.16 (2nd c.AD)}}, etc.; sometimes also, **at** or **near,** ἐπ᾽ αὐτάων (i.e. τῶν πηγῶν) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.153; κόλπος ὁ ἐ. Ποσιδηΐου 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.115}}; αἱ ἐ. Λήμνου ἐπικείμεναι νῆσοι **off** Lemnos, {{Refs|[prev. work] 6 codices; τὰ ἐ. Θρᾴκης}} the Thrace-**ward** region, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.59, compare “IG” 12.45.17, etc.; ποταμοὶ ἐφ᾽ ὧν ἔξεστιν ἡμῖν ταμιεύεσθαι..}} **on,** i.e. **near** which.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “An.” 2.5.18; ἐ. τῶν τραπεζῶν}} **at** the money-changers' tables, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ap.” 17c; in Geometry texts, αἱ ἐφ᾽ ὧν AA BB [γραμμαί] the lines AA BB, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “EN” 1132b6, etc.; ἕλιξ ἐφ᾽ ἇς τὰ ΑΒΓΔ a spiral ABCD, 3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “Spir.” 13 (compare 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1.1k); also ἐ. τοῦ βάτου}} **in the passage concerning** the bush, {{NT|“NT.Mark.12.26”.}}
**A.I.2** in various relations not strictly local, μένειν ἐ. τῆς ἀρχῆς remain **in** the command, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Ages.” 1.37; μένειν ἐ. τινος}} abide **by** it, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.9; ἐ. τῶν πραγμάτων, ἐ. τοῦ πολεμεῖν εἶναι}} to be engaged **in..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev\. author] 15.11, “Prooem.” 1; ἐ. ὀνόματος εἶναι bear a name, [prev\. author] 39.21; ἔχεται πόλις ἐ. νόσου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1141}} (Lyric poetry).
**A.I.2.b** of ships, ὁρμεῖν ἐπ᾽ ἀγκύρας ride **at** (i.e. _in dependence upon an_) anchor, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.188; ἐ. προσπόλου μιᾶς χωρεῖν}} **dependent upon** an attendant, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “OC” 746.}}
**A.I.2.c** with the personal and reflexive _pronoun_, once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, εὔχεσθε.. σιγῇ ἐφ᾽ ὑμείων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.195}}; later mostly with _3rd pers._, ἐπ᾽ ἑωυτῶν κεῖσθαι **by** themselves, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.2, compare 8.32; οἰκέειν κώμην [prev\. author] 5.98; ἐ. σφῶν αὐτῶν αὐτόνομοι οἰκεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.63; ἵζεσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.17; ἐφ᾽ ἑαυτῶν πλεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.8}}; ἐπ᾽ ὑμέων αὐτῶν βαλέσθαι consider it **by** yourselves, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.71, etc.; αὐτὴ ἐφ᾽ αὑτῆς σκοποῦσα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.40; ἐφ᾽ ἡμῶν αὐτῶν τὸν ἐξετασμὸν ποιεῖσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.16}}; ἐπ᾽ ἑωυτῶν διαλέγονται speak **in** a dialect of their own, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.142; also αὐτοὶ ἐφ᾽ ἑαυτῶν χωρεῖν Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Anabasis” 2.4.10; πράττειν Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Protagoras” 326d, compare “Sph.” 217c; τὸ ἐφ᾽ ἑαυτῶν μόνον προορώμενοι}}] considering their own **interest** only, th.{{Refs|1.17.}}
**A.I.2.d** with numerals, to denote the **depth** of a body of soldiers, ἐ. τεττάρων ταχφῆναι to be drawn up four **deep,** four **in file,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “An.” 1.2.15, etc.; ἐ. πεντήκοντα ἀσπίδων συνεστραμμένοι, of the Thebansat Leuctra, [prev\. author] “HG” 6.4.12; ἐπ᾽ ὀλίγων τεταγμένοι}} i.e. **in** a long thin **line,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev\. author] “An.” 4.8.11; οὐκ ἐπ᾽ ὀλίγων ἀσπίδων στρατιὰν παρατεταγμένην 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.79}}; ἐφ᾽ ἑνὸς ἄγειν **in** single **file,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cyr.” 2.4.2, compare “An.” 5.2.6}}; rarely of the **length** of the line, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἐ. τεσσάρων ταξάμενοι τὰς ναῦς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.90; in 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus, ἐγένοντο τὸ μέτωπον ἐ. τριακοσίων.. τὸ δὲ βάθος ἐφ᾽ ἑκατόν “Cyr.” 2.4.2}}; πλεῖν ἐ. κέρως, ἐ. κέρας, see below c.{{Refs|1.3; ἐ. φάλαγγος γίγνεται τὸ στράτευμα}} is formed **in** column, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“An.” 4.6.6, etc. (but in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ph.” 1467, ἀσπίδων ἔπι is merely _in_ or _under_ arms): hence, generally, ἐ. ὀκτὼ πλίνθων τὸ εὖρος}} eight bricks **wide,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “An.” 7.8.14.}}
**A.I.2.e** with _genitive_ _person_, **before, in presence of,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἐ. μαρτύρων.. πράσσεταί τι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.3.8; ἐξελέγχεσθαι ἐ. πάντων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.36}}; so, **before** a magistrate or official, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|ἐ. τοῦ στρατηγοῦ “POxy.” 38.11 (1st c.AD), compare “UPZ” 71.15 (2nd c.BC), “NT.Mat.28.14”; γράψομαί σε ἐ. Ῥαδαμάνθυος 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cat.” 18; τινὰ εἰς δίκην καὶ κρίσιν ἐ. τῶν στρατοπέδων προκαλεῖν 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 1.30d; πίστεις δοῦναι ἐ. θεῶν 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.29}}; but ἐπὶ δικασταῖς is _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.243 (to be read ἔπη).}}
**A.I.2.f** with Verbs of perceiving, observing, judging, etc., **in the case of,** [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|ἐπὶ νούσων παντοίων ἐπύθοντο 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 112.10; ὁρᾶν τι ἐ. τινος Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Memorabilia” 3.9.3; αἰσθάνεσθαί τι ἐ. τινος Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Respublica” 406c, etc.; τὴν γνώμην ἔχειν ἐ. τινος 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 32; τὰ συμβόλαια ἐ. τῶν νόμων σκοπεῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.210}}]; ἐπ᾽ αὐτῶν τῶν ἔργων ἂν ἐσκόπει [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|[prev. work]233, compare 25.2 (variant); ἐφ᾽ ἑνός τι παριδεῖν 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 64; τὰς ἐναντιώσεις ἐ. μὲν τῶν λόγων τηροῦντες, ἐ. δὲ τῶν ἔργων μὴ καθορῶντες Isocrates Orator [{5-6th c.BC}] 13.7; οὐδεὶς ἐφ᾽ αὑτοῦ τὰ κακὰ συνορᾷ Menander Comicus [{4-3rd c.BC}] 631; ἀγνοεῖν τι ἐ. τινος Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Memorabilia” 2.3.2}}]; also with Verbs of speaking, **on** a subject, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|λέγειν ἐ. τινος Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Charmides” 155d, “R.” 524e, etc.; ἐπιδεῖξαί τι ἐ. τινος Isocrates Orator [{5-6th c.BC}] 8.109; ἵνα τοὺς ἐπαίνους ἐπ᾽ αὐτῶν κοινοὺς ποιήσωμαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 60.12}}].
**A.I.3** implying Motion:
**A.I.3.a** where the sense of motion is lost in the sense of being supported, ὀρθωθεὶς.. ἐπ᾽ ἀγκῶνος having raised himself **upon** his elbow, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.80; ἐ. μελίης.. ἐρεισθείς 22.225; τὴν μὲν.. καθεῖσεν ἐ. θρόνου 18.389}}.
**A.I.3.b** in a pregnant sense, denoting the goal of motion (compare [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|εἰς Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] 1.2, ἐν Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] 1.8), νῆα.. ἐπ᾽ ἠπείροιο ἔρυσσαν}}] drew the ship **upon** the land **and left it there,** 1.485; περάαν νήσων ἔπι carry **to** the islands **and leave there,** 21.454, compare22.45; [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|ἐ. τῆς γῆς καταπίπτειν Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.54}}]; ἀναβῆναι ἐ. τῶν πύργων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 7.1.39; ἐπ᾽ Ἀβύδου ἀφικομέναις 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.79}} (variant); frequently of motion **towards** or (in a military sense) **upon** a place, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|προτρέποντο μελαινάων ἐ. νηῶν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.700; τρέσσε.. ἐφ᾽ ὁμίλου 11.546}} (but νήσου ἔ. Ψυρίης νέεσθαι to go _near_ Psyria, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.171); ἐπ᾽ οἴκου ἀπελαύνειν, ἀναχωρεῖν, ἀποχωρεῖν}} home**wards,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121.δ, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.30, 87, etc.; also with names of places, ἰέναι ἐ. Κυζίκου 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.14; πλεῖν ἐ. Χίου [prev\. author] 1.164, compare 168; ἀποπλεῖν ἐπ᾽ αἰγύπτου [prev. work] 1; ἀπαλλάσσεσθαι ἐ. Θεσσαλίης [prev\. author] 5.64}}; ὁ κόλπος ὁ ἐ. Παγασέων φέρων the bay that leads **to** Pagasae, {{Refs|[prev\. author] 7.193; ἡ ἐ. βαβυλῶνος ὁδός}} the road **leading to** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cyr.” 5.3.45, compare “An.” 6.3.24.}}
**A.I.3.c** _metaphorically_, ἐ. γνώμης τινὸς γίγνεσθαι come **to** an opinion, [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.7; ἐπ᾽ ἐλπίδος γενέσθαι Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus [{1-2nd c.AD}] “Solon” 14}}]; ὡς ἐ. κινδύνου as if **to meet** danger, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.34; ἐ. τοῦ ἀλύπως ζῆν _with a view to... Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Protagoras” 358b; compare below 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 111.2.}}]
**A.II** of Time, **in the time of,** [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἐ. προτέρων ἀνθρώπων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.637, 23.332; ἐ. Κρόνου {8-7th c.BC}: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 111}}]; ἐ. Κέκροπος, ἐ. Δαρείου, etc., [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.44, 6.98, etc.; ἐ. τῶν τριάκοντα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.2; ὀλιγαρχία ἡ ἐ. τῶν τετρακοσίων καταστᾶσα Isocrates Orator [{5-6th c.BC}] 8.108}}]; ἐ. τούτου τυραννεύοντος, ἐ. Λέοντος βασιλεύοντος, ἐ. Μήδων ἀρχόντων, etc., [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.15, 65, 134, etc.; ἐ. τῆς ἐμῆς βασιλείας Isocrates Orator [{5-6th c.BC}] 3.32}}]; ἐπ᾽ ἐμεῦ **in** my **time,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.5, 2.46, etc.; ἡ εἰρήνη ἡ ἐπ᾽ Ἀνταλκίδου 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.54, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “HG” 5.1.36; αἱ ἐπ᾽ Ἀσδρούβα γενόμεναι ὁμολογίαι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.15.5}}; ἐπ᾽ εἰρήνης **in time of** peace, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.797, 9.403; ἐπ᾽ ἐμῆς νεότητος Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Acharnenses” 211 (Lyric poetry); ἐ. Λάχητος καὶ τοῦ προτέρου πολέμου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.6}}]; ἐπ᾽ ἡμέρης ἑκάστης variant for{-ῃ -τῃ} in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.117.}}
**A.II.b** later ἐ. δείπνου **at** dinner, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 3; ἐ. τῆς τραπέζης, ἐφ᾽ ἑκάστης κύλικος, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alex.” 23; ἐ. τῆς κύλικος, ἐ. τοῦ ποτηρίου, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pisc.” 34, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alex.” 53.}}
**A.III** in various causal senses:
**A.III.1** **over,** of persons in authority, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἐπ᾽ οὗ ἐτάχθημεν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.109}}; οἱ ἐ. τῶν πραγμάτων the public officers, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.247; frequently in forged decrees, ὁ ἐ. τῶν ὅπλων στρατηγός [prev. work]38; ὁ ἐ. τῶν ὁπλιτῶν, τῶν ἱππέων, [prev. work]116; ὁ ἐ. τῆς διοικήσεως [prev. work]38 (but compare with 111.3); τοῦ ἐ. τῶν ὁπλιτῶν is _falsa lectio_ in 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.5; ὁ ἐ. τῆς χώρας στρατηγός Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus [{1-2nd c.AD}] “Phocion” 32; οἱ ἐ. τῶν σιτοποιῶν καὶ μαγείρων [prev\. author] “Alex.” 23; ὁ ἐ. τοῦ οἴνου [prev\. author] “Pyrrh.” 5}}]; ὁ ἐ. τῶν ἐπιστολῶν τοῦ Ὄθωνος, = Latin _ab epistulis,_ his secretary, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev\. author] “Oth.” 9; compare 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 111.6.}}
**A.III.2** κεκλῆσθαι ἐ. τινος to be called **after** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.45; ἐ. τινος μετονομασθῆναι [prev\. author] 1.94: ἐ. τινος τὰς ἐπωνυμίας ἔχειν [prev\. author] 4.107}}; ἐ. τινος ἐπώνυμος γίγνεσθαι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. work] 184; also ἐπ᾽ ὀνόματος καλεῖν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.35.2}}.
**A.III.3** of occasions, circumstances, and conditions, οὐκ ἐ. τούτου μόνον, ἀλλ᾽ ἐ. πάντων, **on** all occasions, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.38, compare 183; ἐφ᾽ ἑκάστων Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Philebus” 25e; ἐφ᾽ ἑκατέρου [prev\. author] “Tht.” 159c; ἐφ᾽ ἑκάστης μαντείας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.54}}]; ἐπ᾽ ἐξουσίας καὶ πλούτου πονηρὸν εἶναι **in..** [{{NT+4th c.BC+|[prev. work]138; ἐ. τῆς ἀληθείας καὶ τοῦ πράγματος [prev. work]72, compare 18.17; τὴν ἐ. τῆς πομπῆς καὶ τοῦ μεθύειν πρόφασιν λαβών [prev\. author] 21.180; ἐ. σχολῆς 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.191; ἐπ᾽ ἀδείας Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus [{1-2nd c.AD}] “Solon” 22; ἐπ᾽ ἀληθείας NT.Mark.12.14, “POxy.” 255.16 (1st c.AD): hence in adverbial phrases, ἐπ᾽ ἴσας (i.e. μοίρας)}}] **equally,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “El.” 1062 (Lyric poetry); ἐ. καιροῦ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.90}}; ἐπ᾽ ἐσχάτων **at** the last, [{{LXX|LXX.Deu.17.7 (variant{ἐσχάτῳ}); ἐπὶ τοῦ παρόντος}}] **for** the present, {{Refs|“SIG” 543.6 (Epist. Philipp.).}}
**A.III.4** **in respect of,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|ἐ. τῶν πραγμάτων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1280a17, compare “EN” 1131b18}}; **concerning,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|τὰ ἐπ᾽ αὐτῶν ἐνεστηκότα “PTeb.” 7.6 (2nd c.BC).}}
**B** WITH DAT.:
**B.I** of Place, **upon,** just like the _genitive_ (hence Poets use whichever case suits the metre, whereas in Prose the _dative_ is more frequently):
**B.I.1** with Verbs of Rest, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἕζεο τῷδ᾽ ἐ. δίφρῳ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.354; ἧντ᾽ ἐ. πύργῳ 3.153; στῆ δ᾽ ἐ... νηΐ 8.222; κεῖσθαι ἐ. τινι Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Anabasis” 1.8.27}}]; καίειν ἐ. πᾶσι (i.e. βωμοῖς) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.240; ἔβραχε χαλκὸς ἐ. στήθεσσι 4.420; ἐ. χθονὶ δέρκεσθαι 1.88}}, etc.: also with Verbs of Motion, where the subject rests **upon** something, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|νηυσὶν ἐπ᾽ ὠκυπόροισιν ἔβαινον 2.351 (variant for{ἐν}); ἐπ᾽ ὤμοις φέρειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1131}}] (but ἐφ᾽ ἵππῳ, ἐφ᾽ ἵπποις and the like are never used for ἐφ᾽ ἵππου, etc.); of places, mostly **in,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἐ. τῇ χώρῃ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.77; τἀπὶ Τροίᾳ πέργαμα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 353; ἐπ᾽ ἐσχάτοις τόποις [prev\. author] “Tr.” 1100; ἐ. τῇ ψυχῇ δάκνομαι [prev\. author] “Ant.” 317}}; also, **at** or **near,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἐ. κρήνῃ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.408; ἐ. θύρῃσι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.788}}, etc.; of rivers, etc., **by, beside,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἐ. ὠκυρόῳ Κελάδοντι.. 7.133, etc.; ἐπ᾽ ἐσχάρῃ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.160; ἐ. νηυσί 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.559}}, etc.; of persons, οὐ τἀπὶ Λυδοῖς οὐδ᾽ ἐπ᾽ Ὀμφάλῃ λατρεύματα **in** Lydia, **in the power of** O., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Tr.” 356.}}
**B.I.1.b** **on** or **over,** ἐπ᾽ Ἰφιδάμαντι **over** the body of Iphidamas, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.261, compare 4.470; τοιόνδ᾽ ἐπ᾽ ἀνδρὶ κομπάζεις λόγον Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Agamemnon” 1400}}]; also, **over** or **in honour of,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἐ. σοὶ κατέθηκε.. ἄεθλα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.91; [βοῦς] ἐ. Πατρόκλῳ πέφνεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.776; κειράμενοι χαίτας ἐπ᾽ Ἀδώνιδι 2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus 1.81, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.80; in _Doric dialect_ and _Aeolic dialect_ sepulchral Inscrr., “Schwyzer” 348, +others}}
**B.I.1.c** in hostile sense, **against,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.61, 6.74, 88, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ph.” 1139 (Lyric poetry)}}, etc.; **as a check upon,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|οἱ πρόβουλοι καθεστᾶσιν ἐ. τοῖς βουλευταῖς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1299b37, compare 1271a39}}; also, **towards, in reference to,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἐ. πᾶσι χόλον τελέσαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.178; ἐπ᾽ ἔργοις πᾶσι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1268; δικαιότερος καὶ ἐπ᾽ ἄλλῳ ἔσσεαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.181, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Tr.” 994 (_anapaest meter_), etc.; ἐ. τοῖς δυνατοῖς ἔχειν τὴν γνώμην 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 191}}; τὸ ἐ. πᾶσιν τοῖς σώμασι κάλλος **extending over** all bodies, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Smp.” 210b; ἡ [παιδεία] ἡ ἐ. σώμασι, ἐ. ψυχῇ, [prev\. author] “R.” 376e; τἀπὶ σοὶ κακά}} the ills which lie **upon** thee, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ph.” 806: in _Attic dialect_ also, νόμον τίθεσθαι, θεῖναι ἐ. τινι}} make a law **for his case,** whether **for** or **against,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Grg.” 488d, Legal cited in 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.87; νόμους ἀναγράψαι ἐ. τοῖς ἀδικοῦσι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.5}}; νόμος κεῖται ἐ. τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work]70; τἀπὶ τῷ πλήθει νενομοθετημένα [prev. work]123, compare 142; τί θεσμοποιεῖς ἐ. ταλαιπώρῳ νεκρ; 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ph.” 1645.}}
**B.I.1.d**. of accumulation, **upon, after**, ὄγχνη ἐπ᾽ ὄγχνῃ one pear **after** another, pear **on** pear, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.120; ἐ. κέρδεϊ κέρδος {8-7th c.BC}: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 644; ἄτη ἑτέρα ἐπ᾽ ἄτῃ Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Choephori” 404}}] (Lyric poetry); πήματα ἐ. πήμασι, ἐ. νόσῳ νόσος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ant.” 595, “OC” 544 (both Lyric poetry).}}
**B.I.1.e**. **in addition to, over and above, besides**, οὐκ ἄρα σοί γ᾽ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἐ. εἴδεϊ καὶ φρένες ἦσαν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.454, compare 308; ἄλλα τε πόλλ᾽ ἐ. τῇσι παρίσχομεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.639, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.264; ἐ. τοῖσι}} **besides**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|24.277; ἐ. τούτοις 4th c.AD: Himerius Sophista “Orationes” 14.10}}; so of Numerals, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|τρισχιλίους ἐ. μυρίοις Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus [{1-2nd c.AD}] “Publicola” 20, compare 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Or.” 4.148c, etc.; γυναῖκ᾽ ἐφ᾽ ἡμῖν.. ἔχει 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Med.” 694}}]: with Verbs of eating and drinking, **with**, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|ἐ. τῷ σίτῳ πίνειν ὕδωρ Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.27}}]; νέκταρ [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|ποτίσαι ἐπ᾽ ἀμβροσίᾳ Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Phaedrus” 247e}}]; especially of a relish, κάρδαμον μόνον [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|ἐ. τῷ σίτῳ ἔχειν Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.11; παίειν ἐφ᾽ ἁλὶ τὰν μᾶδδαν Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Acharnenses” 835}}]: _metaphorically_, ἐ. τῷ φάγοις ἥδιστ᾽ ἄ; ἐ. βαλλαντί; {{Refs|[prev\. author] “Eq.” 707; later ἐ. γογγυλίσι διαβιῶναι}} live **on** turnips, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 10.419a.}}
**B.I.1.f**. of position, **after**,**behind**, of soldiers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cyr.” 8.3.16 - 18.}}
**B.I.1.g**. **in dependence upon, in the** **power of**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|τὰ δ᾽ οὐκ ἐπ᾽ ἀνδράσι κεῖται 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.76}}; ἐ. τινί ἐστι it is **in** his **power** to do, c._infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.29, etc.; ἐ. σοί ἐστιν ἀναζωπυρεῖν 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 7.2; ἐ. ἑτέροις γίγνεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.22}}; ἐ. τῷ πλήθει **in** their **hands**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “OC” 66, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.84; τὸ ἐπ᾽ ἐμοί, τὸ ἐ. ἐκείνῳ}}, etc., as far as is **in** my **power**, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cyr.” 5.4.11, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.142, etc.; τὸ ἐ. τούτοις εἶναι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 28.14}}; ἐ. τοῖς υἱάσι their **property,** {{Refs|Leges Gortynensium 4.37.}}
**B.I.1.h**. **according to**, ἐ. τοῖς νόμοις Legal cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.56; ἐ. πᾶσι δικαίοις ποιούμεθα τοὺς λόγους [prev\. author] 20.88; ἐ. προφάσει θηρός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 662}} codices (Lyric poetry).
**B.I.1.i**. of condition or circumstances **in** which one is, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἀτελευτήτῳ ἐ. ἔργῳ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.175, etc.; ἐπ᾽ ἀρρήτοις λόγοις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 556; ἐπ᾽ ἀσφάκτοις μήλοισι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 228 (Lyric poetry); ταύταις ἐ. συντυχίαις 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.36; ἐπ᾽ εὐπραξίᾳ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1554; ἐ. τῷ παρόντι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.36}}; ἐπ᾽ αὐτοφώρῳ λαβεῖν, see at {αὐτόφωρος}; also ἐ. τῷ δείπνῳ **at** dinner, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cyr.” 1.3.12, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Char.” 3.2; ἐ. τῇ κύλικι Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Symposium” 214b; ἐ. θαλίαις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Med.” 192}}] (_anapaest meter_).
**B.I.1.k**. Geometry texts, of the point, etc., **at** which letters are written, κέντρον ἐφ᾽ ᾧ K Hippocr. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Ph.” 64.14; ἡ [γραμμὴ] ἐφ᾽ ᾗ HK the line HK, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mete.” 375b22.}}
**B.I.2**. with Verbs of Motion:
**B.I.2.a**. where the sense of motion merges in that of support, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἐ. χθονὶ βαίνει 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.443; θεῖναι ἐ. γούνασιν 6.92; καταθέσθαι ἐ. γαίῃ 3.114}}; ἱστὸν ἔστησεν ἐ. {{Refs|ψαμάθοις 23.853; ἐ. φρεσὶ θῆκε 1.55}}; δυσφόρους ἐπ᾽ ὄμμασι γνώμας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|βαλεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 51}}, etc.
**B.I.2.b**. in pregnant construction, πέτονται ἐπ᾽ ἄνθεσιν fly **on to** the flowers **and settle there**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.89; ἐκ.. βαῖνον ἐ. ῥηγμῖνι θαλάσσης 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.499; καθεῖσεν ἐ. Σκαμάνδρῳ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.36}}; ἦλθε δ᾽ {{Refs|ἐ. Κρήτεσσι 4.251, compare 273; νῆες εἰρύατ᾽.. ἐ. θινὶ θαλάσσης 4.248}}.
**B.I.2.c**. rarely for εἰς c._accusative_, {{Refs|νηυσὶν ἔ. γλαφυρῇσιν ἐλαυνέμεν 5.327, 11.274}}.
**B.I.2.d**. in hostile sense, **upon** or **against**, ἐ. τινι ἔχειν, ἰθύνειν ἵππους, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5.240, 8.110; ἐ. τινι ἱέναι βέλος, ἰθύνεσθαι ὀϊστόν, 1.382, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.8; ἐ. τοι Ἀκράγαντι τανύσαις 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.91; ἐ. Τυδεΐδῃ ἐτιταίνετο.. τόξα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.97; ἐφ᾽ Ἕκτορι.. ἀκοντίσσαι 16.358; κύνας.. σεύῃ ἐπ᾽ ἀγροτέρῳ συΐ 11.293; ὡρμήθησαν ἐπ᾽ ἀνδράσιν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.214, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ph.” 1379, etc.: also ἐ. τινι τετάχθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.70, 3.13; ὅστις φάρμακα δηλητήρια ποιοῖ ἐ. Τηΐοισιν “SIG” 37.2}} (Teos, 5th c.BC).
**B.II**. of Time, rarely, and never in good _Attic dialect_, except in sense of succession (below 2), ἐ. νυκτί **by** night, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.529; ἐφ᾽ ἡμέρῃ, αἱ δ᾽ ἐ. νυκτί {8-7th c.BC}: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 102}}]; ἐπ᾽ ἤματι τῷδε **on** this very day, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.234; ἐπ᾽ ἤματι}} **for** to-day, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|19.229, 10.48, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.284; αἰεὶ ἐπ᾽ ἤματι every day, 14.105; ἐπ᾽ ἡμέρῃ ἑκάστῃ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.112, 5.53, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 34 / 5.2.1; ὁ ἥλιος νέος ἐφ᾽ ἡμέρῃ ἐστίν 1st c.AD: Heraclitus 6; ἐ. τρίς “Act.Ap.” 10.16, “PHolm.” 1.18.}}
**B.II.2**. of succession, **after**, ἕκτῃ ἐ. δέκα on the {{Refs 4th c.BC+|16th of the month, Chron. cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.155, Decrees [prev. work]181 (δεκάτῃ codices); τετράδι ἐ. δέκα “IG” 12.304.62}}; πρὸ τῆς ἕκτης ἐ. δέκα {{Refs|[prev. work]22.1361.19; ἐπ᾽ ἐξεργασμένοισι, =}} Latin _re peracta_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.164, etc.; ἐ. τινι ἀγορεύειν, ἀνίστασθαι, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Or.” 898, 902, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cyr.” 2.3.7, etc.; ἐ. διεφθαρμένοισι Ἴωσι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.170}}, τὰ ἐ. τούτοισι, = Latin _quod superest_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev\. author] 9.78, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.65, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Ag.” 255, etc.; τοὐπὶ τῷδε πῆμα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 855}} (Lyric poetry), etc.
**B.II.3**. **in the time of** (compare {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11) only in 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus, ἐπὶ Χαιριάδαι “Schwyzer” 665 A 21, compare 666 (Orchom.).}}
**B.III**. in various causal senses:
**B.III.1**. of the occasion or cause, τετεύξεται ἄλγε᾽ ἐπ᾽ αὐτῇ **for** her, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.585; ἐ. σοὶ μάλα πόλλ᾽ ἔπαθον}} **for** thee, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|9.492: frequently with Verbs expressing some mental affection, ἐπὶ παντὶ λόγῳ ἐπτοῆσθαι 1st c.AD: Heraclitus 87}}; μέγα φρονεῖν ἐ. τινι to be proud **at** or **of** a thing, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Prt.” 342d, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “HG” 3.4.11, etc.; χλιδᾶν ἐ.τινι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “El.” 360; ἀγάλλεσθαι, ἀγανακτεῖν ἐ. τοῖς παροῦσι, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “An.” 2.6.26, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.122; ὀνομαστὸς ἐ. τινι γεγονέναι Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Memorabilia” 1.2.61}}]; also ἐφ᾽ αἵματι φεύγειν to be tried **on** a capital charge, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.105; πληγὰς λαμβάνειν ἐ. τινι Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.16; ζημιοῦσθαι ἐ. τινι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.122}}], etc.: in adverbial phrases [δικάσσαι] ἐπ᾽ ἀρωγῇ **with** favour, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.574; δολίῃ ἐ. τέχνῃ {8-7th c.BC}: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 540; ἐ. μιῇ αἰτίῃ ἀνήκεστον πάθος ἔρδειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.137}}], etc.; ἐ.κακουργίᾳ καὶ οὐκ ἀρετῇ **for** malice, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.37; ἐπ᾽ εὐνοία, ἐπ᾽ ἔχθρα, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.273, 21.55; ἐπ᾽ ἀγαθῇ ἐλπίδι **with.. **, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus Mem. 2.1.18, cf. NT.Rom.4.18; ἐφ᾽ ἑκατέροις}} **in** both cases, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Tht.” 158d, compare 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 34.4; ἐ. δάκρυσί τινα καταστένειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 315}} (Lyric poetry); ἐ. τῇ πάσῃ συκοφαντίᾳ καὶ διασεισμῷ Mitteis {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Chr.” 31 vI (2nd c.BC), etc.}}
**B.III.2**. of an end or purpose, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|υἱὸν ἐ. κτεάτεσσι λιπέσθαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.154, compare 9.482; ἐ. δόρπῳ}} **for** supper, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.44; ἐ. κακῷ ἀνθρώπου σίδηρος ἀνεύρηται 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.68; ἐ. διαφθορῇ [prev\. author] 4.164; ἐ. σῷ καιρῷ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 151 (Lyric poetry); ἐ. τῷ κέρδει Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Memorabilia” 1.2.56}}]; δῆσαι ἐ. θανάτῳ or τὴν ἐ. θανάτῳ, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.37, 3.119, compare 1.109, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “An.” 1.6.10; ἐ. θανάτῳ συλλαβεῖν Isocrates Orator [{5-6th c.BC}] 4.154}}]; ἐπ᾽ ἐξαγωγῇ **for** exportation, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.6; χρηστηριάζεσθαι ἐ. τῇ χώρῃ}} **with a view to gaining.. **, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|[prev\. author] 1.66; ἐ. τούτοις ἐθύσαντο Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Anabasis” 3.5.18; ἐ. τῷ ὑβρίζεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.38, compare 34, etc.; τι κακοτεχνεῖν ἐ. αἰσχύνῃ τοῦ ἀνδρός “PEleph.” 1.6 (4th c.BC).}}]
**B.III.3**. of the condition **upon** which a thing is done, ἐ. τούτοισι **on** these terms, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.60, etc.; ἐ. τοῖσδε, ὥστε.. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.114}}; ἐ. τούτῳ, ἐπ᾽ ᾧτε **on condition that.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.83, compare 7.158: in indirect speech, ἐπ᾽ or ἐφ᾽ ᾧτε}} followed by _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev\. author] 1.22, 7.154, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “HG” 2.2.20; ἐφ᾽ ᾧ μηδὲν κακὸν ποιήσουσιν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.126}} (but ἐφ᾽ ᾧ = _wherefore,_ NT.Rom.5.12); ἐπ᾽ οὐδενί **on** no condition, **on** no account, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.38}}; but, **for** no adequate **reason**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.132; ἐπ᾽ ἴσῃ τε καὶ ὁμοίῃ, ἐπὶ τῇ ἴσῃ καὶ ὁμοίᾳ}}, **on** fair and equal **terms**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.7, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.27; ἐ. ῥητοῖς, see at {ῥητός}; also of a woman's dowry, τὴν μητέρα ἐγγυᾶν ἐ. ταῖς ὀγδοήκοντα μναῖς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 28.16}}]; γῆμαί [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|τινα ἐ. δέκα ταλάντοις Andocides Orator [{5-6th c.BC}] 4.13; τὴν θυγατέρα ἔχειν γυναῖκα ἐ. τῇ τυραννίδι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.60}}]; **on the principle of.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἐ. τῷ μὴ λυπεῖν ἀλλήλους 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.71}}.
**B.III.4**. of the price **for** which.., [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἔργον τελέσαι δώρῳ ἔ. μεγάλῳ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.304, compare 21.445; ἐ. τίνι χρήματ; 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.38; ἐ. πόσ; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ap.” 41a; ἐ. ταλάντῳ χρυσίου Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Aves” 154}}]; ἐπ᾽ ἀργυρίῳ λέγειν, πράττειν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.182, 24.200; ἐ. χρήμασι λυμαίνεσθαι [prev\. author] 19.332; ἐ. πολλῷ ἐρρᾳθυμηκότες [prev\. author] 1.15}}; also of money lent **at** interest, δανείζεσθαι ἐ. τοῖς μεγάλοις τόκοις {{Refs|[prev. passage]; ἐ. δραχμῇ δανείζειν}} lend **at** 12 per cent., {{Refs|[prev\. author] 27.9; ἐπ᾽ ὀκτὼ ὀβολοῖς τὴν μνᾶν τοῦ μηνὸς ἑκάστου δανείζειν}}, i.e. **at** 16 per cent., {{Refs|[prev\. author] 53.13; ἐ. διακοσίαις εἴκοσι πέντε τὰς χιλίας _for_ 225 per mille, i.e. 22.5 per cent., Syngr. cited in [same work] 35.10}}; also of the security **on** which money is borrowed, {{Refs|δανείζειν ἐ. ἀνδραπόδοις [prev\. author] 27.27}}; ἐπ᾽ οἴνου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|κεραμίοις τρισχιλίοις [prev\. author] 35.18; ἐ. νηΐ [prev\. author] 56.3; δανείζειν ἐ. τοῖς σώμασιν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 9.1, compare 2.2, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.9.}}
**B.III.5**. of names, φάος καὶ νὺξ ὀνόμασται.. [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|ἐ. τοῖσί τε καὶ τοῖς Parmenides Poeta Philosophus [{4-5th c.BC}] 9.2; ἐ. τῇ τοῦ οἰκείου ἔχθρᾳ στάσις κέκληται Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Respublica” 470b; so ὄνομα κεῖται ἐ. τινι Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.12}}]; ὄνομα καλεῖν [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|ἐ. τινι Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Sophista” 218c, compare 244b; πότερον ταῦτα, πέντε ὀνόματα ὄντα, ἐ. ἑνὶ πράγματί ἐστι [prev\. author] “Prt.” 349b (v. above 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 111.2).}}]
**B.III.6**. of persons in authority, ὅς μ᾽ ἐ. βουσὶν εἷσεν who set me **over** the kine, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.209, compare 221; ποιμαίνειν ἐπ᾽ ὄεσσι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.25; οὖρον κατέλειπον ἐ. κτεάτεσσιν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.89; σημαίνειν ἐ. δμῳῇσι 22.427}}; πέμπειν ἐ. τοσούτῳ [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|στρατεύματι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.29; ἐ. ταῖς ναυσίν Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.5.11; οἱ ἐ. ταῖς μηχαναῖς [prev\. author] “Cyr.” 6.3.28}}]; οἱ ἐ. ταῖς καμήλοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 33; οἱ ἐ. τοῖς πράγμασιν ὄντες 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.2; ἐ. θυγατρὶ.. γαμεῖν ἄλλην γυναῖκα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.154}}.
**B.III.7**. **in possession** **of, possessing**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἐ. τοῖς ἑαυτοῦ μένειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.105, compare 8.86; ζῆν ἐ. παιδίοις, τελευτᾶν ἐ. παιδὶ γνησίῳ, 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.3, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “VS” 2.12.2; ἐ. παισὶ διαδόχοις 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 4.2.1; ἀποθανεῖν ἐ. κληρονόμοις ταῖς θυγατράσι 2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 1.78, compare “PMeyer” 6.22 (2nd c.AD); ἐ. μόνῳ παιδὶ σαλεύειν 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 1.9}}.
**C**. WITH Acc.:
**C.I**. of Place, **upon** or **on to** a height, with Verbs of Motion, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἐ. πύργον ἔβη 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.386, compare 12.375; ἐ. τὰ ὑψηλότατα τῶν ὀρέων ἀναβαίνειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.131; προελθεῖν ἐ. βῆμα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.34}}; ἀναβιβαστέον τινά, ἀναβαίνειν ἐ. τὸν ἵππον, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “R.” 467e, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “An.” 3.4.35; also ἐξ ἵππων ἀποβάντες ἐ. χθόνα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.265}}; ἐξεκυλίσθη πρηνὴς ἐ. στόμα **upon** his face, {{Refs|6.43; ἐ. θρόνον.. ἕζετο 8.442}}; ὤμω.. ἐ. στῆθος συνοχωκότε drawn together **upon** his breast, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|2.218; Ὀδυσσῆ᾽ εἷσαν ἐ. σκέπας 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.212; θέσθαι ἐ. τὰ γόνατα Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Anabasis” 7.3.23; ἐπ᾽ ἀμφότερα τὰ ὦτα καθεύδειν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Socraticus Philosophus 54}}]; ἐ. κεφαλήν head-**foremost**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “R.” 553b, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pisc.” 48 (v. κεφαλή): less frequently than ἐπί with _genitive_ or _dative_}}
**C.I.b**. Geometry texts, αἱ ἐ. τὰς ἁφὰς ἐπιζευγνύμεναι εὐθεῖαι **joining** the points of contact, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “Sph.Cyl.” 1.8; κάθετος ἐ}}. perpendicular **to** (v. κάθετος).
**C.I.2**. **to**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἦλθε θοὰς ἐ. νῆας 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.12}}, etc.; ἐ. βωμὸν ἄγων [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|[prev. work] 440; ἴθυσαν δ᾽ ἐ. τεῖχος 12.443; ἐ. τέρμ᾽ ἀφίκετο 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 48; ἡ [ὁδὸς] ἐ. Σοῦσα φέρει Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Anabasis” 3.5.15; ἡ ὁδὸς ἡ ἀπὸ τῶν Πυλῶν ἐ. τὸ Ποσειδώνιον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.118}}]; ἐ. {{Refs|τὸ αὐτὸ αἱ γνῶμαι ἔφερον [prev\. author] 1.79}}: c._accusative_ _person_, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|βῆ δ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ἐπ᾽ Ἀτρεΐδην 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.18, compare 10.18, 85, 150, etc.: sometimes in _pregnant_ _construction_ with Verbs of Rest, ἐπιστῆναι ἐ. τὰς θύρας Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Symposium” 212d; παρεῖναι ἐ. τὸν τάφον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.34, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus Cyr. 3.3.12.}}]
**C.I.2.b**. _metaphorically_, ἐ. ἔργα τρέπεσθαι, ἰέναι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.422, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.127; ἰέναι ἐ. τὸν ἔπαινον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.36; ἐ. συμφορὴν ἐμπεσεῖν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.88}} codices; also ἐ. τὴν τράπεζαν ἀποδιδόναι, ὀφείλειν, pay, owe **to** the bank, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 33.12, Docum. cited in [same work] 45.31; ἡ ἐγγύη ἡ ἐ. τὴν τράπεζαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 33.10}}; τὸ ἐ. τὴν τράπεζαν χρέως {{Refs|[prev. work]24; also εἰσποιηθῆναι ἐ. τὸ ὄνομά τινος}} to be entered **under** his name, {{Refs|[prev\. author] 44.36.}}
**C.I.2.c**. **up** **to, as far as** (μέχρι ἐ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “An.” 5.1. [1]), παρατείνειν ἐπ᾽ Ἡρακλέας στήλας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.181; ἐ. θάλασσαν καθήκειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.27, 97: _metaphorically_, ἐ. πείρατ᾽ ἀέθλων ἤλθομεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.248}}; ἐ. διηκόσια ἀποδιδόναι yield {{Refs|200}}-**fold**, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.193; in measurements, πλέον ἢ ἐ. δύο στάδια Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.8, “An.” 6.2.2; ὅσον ἐ. εἴκοσι σταδίους [prev. work] 6.4.5, compare 1.7.15: frequently with a _neuter_}}] _adjective_ or _pronoun_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|τόσσον τίς τ᾽ ἐπιλεύσσει ὅσον τ᾽ ἐ. λᾶαν ἵησιν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.12}}; ὅσσον {{Refs|ἔφ᾽ 2.616, compare 15.358; ἐ. τοσοῦτό γε φρονέω,.. ταύτην μηδὲν σίνεσθαι}} I am prudent **enough**, not to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.97; ἐ. ὅσον δεῖ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.66}}; ἐ. [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|πάντ᾽ ἀφίξομαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 265; ἐ. πᾶν ἐλθεῖν Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Anabasis” 3.1.18}}]; ἐ. τὸ ἔσχατον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἀγῶνος ἐλθεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.92}}; ἐ. μεῖζον χωρεῖν, ἔρχεσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 117, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ph.” 259; ἐ. μέγα χωρεῖν δυνάμεως 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.118}}; ἐ. μακρότερον, ἐ. μακρότατον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev\. author] 4.41, 1.1, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.16, 192; ἐ. σμικρόν, ἐ. βραχύ}}, a little **way**, a little, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “El.” 414, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.118; ἐπ᾽ ἔλαττον, ἐπ᾽ ἐλάχιστον, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phd.” 93b, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.70; ἐπ᾽ ὀλίγον, ἐ. πολλά, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sph.” 254b; ἐ. πλέον}} **still** more, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.171, 5.51, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.51; rarely with Advs., ἐ. μᾶλλον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.94, 4.181}}.
**C.I.2.d**. **before, into the presence of** (compare {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 1.2e), ἦγον δή μιν ἐ. τὰ κοινά [prev\. author] 3.156}} (but στὰς ἐ. τὸ συνέδριον standing _at the door of_ the council, {{NT|[prev\. author] 8.79); ἐ. ἡγεμόνας καὶ βασιλεῖς ἀχθήσεσθε NT.Matt.10.18}}.
**C.I.2.e**. in Military phrases (compare {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 1.2d), ἐπ᾽ ἀσπίδας πέντε καὶ εἴκοσιν ἐτάξαντο}}, i.e. twenty-five **in file**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.93; uncertain in 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus, as ἐ. πολλοὺς τεταγμένοι}} many _in file, _{{Refs|“An.” 4.8.11 codices; ἐπ᾽ ὀλίγον τὸ βάθος γίγνεσθαι “Cyr.” 7.5.2}} codices; for ἐ. κέρας see below{{Refs|3.}}
**C.I.3**. of the quarter or direction **towards** or **in** which a thing takes place, ἐ. δεξιά, ἐπ᾽ ἀριστερά, **to** the right or left, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.238, 12.240, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.171, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.33, etc.; ἐ. τὰ ἕτερα or ἐ. θάτερα, [prev\. author] 5.74, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.87, etc.; ἐ. τὰ μακρότερα,βραχύτερα}}, **on** the longer, shorter **side**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.50; ἐπ᾽ ἀμφότερα νοέων}} both **ways**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev\. author] 8.22; ἐπ᾽ ἀμφότερα μαχᾶν τάμνειν τέλος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.57}}, etc.; ἐ. τάδε Φασήλιδος **on** this **side**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.80; ἐ. ἐκεῖνα, see at {ἐπέκεινα}; ἐφ᾽ ἕν, ἐ. δύο, ἐ. τρία}}], of space, **in** one, two, three **dimensions**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de An.” 404b23, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.3.13; in Military phrases, ἐ. δόρυ ἀναστρέψαι,ἐ. ἀσπίδα μεταβαλέσθαι}}, **to** the spear or shield **side**, i.e. **to** right or left, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “An.” 4.3.29, “Cyr.” 7.5.6; ἐ. πόδα ἀναχωρεῖν}}, etc., retire **on** the foot, i.e. facing the enemy, {{Refs|[prev\. author] “An.” 5.2.32; so ἐ. κέρας or ἐ. κέρως πλεῖν}}, etc., sail **towards** or **on** the wing, i.e. **in** column (v. {{Refs|κέρας v11): _metaphorically_, ἐ. τὸ μεῖζον κοσμῆσαι, δεινῶσαι}}, etc., **with** exaggeration, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.10, 8.74, etc.; ἐ. τὸ πλέον ἀγγέλλεσθαι [prev\. author] 6.34}}; ἐ. τὸ φοβερώτερον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 83; ἐ. τὰ γελοιότερα ἐπαινέσαι so as to provoke laughter, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Smp.” 214e; ἐ. τὰ καλλίω, ἐ. τὰ αἰσχίονα, [prev\. author] “Plt.” 293e; ἐ. τὸ βέλτιον καὶ κάλλιον, ἐ. τὸ χεῖρον καὶ τὸ αἴσχιον, [prev\. author] “R.” 381b; ἐ. τὸ ἄμεινον Oracle texts cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 43.66.}}
**C.I.4**. in hostile sense, **against**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἰέναι ἐ. νέας 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.101; ὦρτο δ᾽ ἐπ᾽ αὐτούς 5.590}}; στρατεύεσθαι or -εύειν ἐ. τινα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.71, 77, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.26, etc.; ἰέναι ἐ. φάτιν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 495}} (Lyric poetry); πλεῖν ἐ. τοὺσ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Ἀθηναίους 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.90; πέμπειν στρατηγὸν ἐ. τινας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.153}}; θύεσθαι ἐ. τινα offer sacrifice **on going against.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “An.” 7.8.21; ἐφ᾽ ὑμᾶς}} **to** your **prejudice**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 6.33, 10.57.}}
**C.I.5**. of extension **over** a space, πουλὺν ἐφ᾽ ὑγρὴν ἤλυθον **over** much water, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.27: ἐπ᾽ εὐρέα νῶτα θαλάσσης 2.159; ἐ. κύματα 13.27; ὁρόων ἐπ᾽ ἀπείρονα πόντον 1.350}}; πλέων, λεύσσων ἐ. οἴνοπα πόντον, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|7.88, 5.771; ἐ. πολλὰ δ᾽ ἀλήθην 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.120; ἄγοισι.. Ἀνδρομάχαν.. ἐπ᾽ ἄλμυρον πόντον Sappho Lyrica [{7-6th c.BC}] “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 20a. 7: also with Verbs of Rest, ἐπ᾽ ἐννέα κεῖτο πέλεθρα}}] **over** nine acres he lay stretched, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.577; τόσσον ἔπ᾽}} **over** so much, {{Refs|5.251, compare 13.114; διώκοντες ἐ. πολύ}} **over** a large space, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.50, compare 62, etc.; ἐ. πλεῖστον [prev. work] 4; ὡς ἐ. πλεῖστον 2.34}}, etc.; frequently to be rendered **on**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|δράκων ἐ. νῶτα δαφοινός 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.308}}; ἵππους.. ἐ. νῶτον ἐΐσας {{Refs|[prev. work] 765; ὅσσα τε γαῖαν ἔπι πνείει 17.447}}; ἐ. γαῖαν εἰσὶ δύω [γένη] [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Op.” 11; ἀοιδοὶ ἔασιν ἐ. χθόνα “Th.” 95; ἐ. γᾶν μέλαιναν ἔμμεναι κάλλιστον Sappho Lyrica [{7-6th c.BC}] “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 5.2}}]; also, **among**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|κλέος πάντας ἐπ᾽ ἀνθρώπους 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.213, compare 24.202, 535; δασσάμενοι [κτήματ᾽] ἐφ᾽ ἡμέας 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.385, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Prt.” 322d.}}
**C.II**. of Time, **for** or **during** a certain time, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἐ. χρόνον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.299, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.193: πολλὸν ἐ. χρόνον 12.407; παυρίδιον.. ἐ. χρόνον {8-7th c.BC}: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 133; ἐ. δηρόν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.415; ἐ. πολὺν χρόνον Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Phaedo” 84c}}], etc.; ἐπ᾽ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|ὀλίγον χρόνον 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 7}}; ἐ. χρόνον τινά, ἐ. τινα χρόνον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Prt.” 344b, “Grg.” 524d; γῆν ἀπεμίσθωσαν ἐ. δέκα ἔτη 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.68}}; ἐ. διετές Legal cited in [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 46.20; ἐ. τρεῖς ἡμέρας Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Anabasis” 6.6.36}}]; τὸ ἐφ᾽ ἡμέραν ἀρκέσον enough **for** the day, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev\. author] “Cyr.” 6.2.34, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 50.23, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32; ἐ. πολύ}} **for** a long time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.6, etc.}}
**C.II.2**. **up to, until** a certain time, εὗδον παννύχιος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|καὶ ἐπ᾽ ἠῶ καὶ μέσον ἦμαρ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.288; οὐδ᾽ ἐ. γῆρας ἵκετ᾽ 8.226}}.
**C.III**. in various causal senses:
**C.III.1**. of the object or purpose **for** which one goes, ἀγγελίην ἔπι Τυδῆ στεῖλαν sent him **for** (i.e. _to bring_) tidings of.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.384 (uncertain); ἐ. βοῦν ἴτω}} let him go **for** an ox, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.421; ἐ. τεύχεα δ᾽ ἐσσεύοντο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.808; ἐλθεῖν πρός τινα ἐπ᾽ ἀργύριον Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.12}}]; πέμπειν εἴς τινα ἐ. στράτευμα {{Refs|[prev. work] 4.5.31; ἴτω τις ἐφ᾽ ὕδωρ [prev. work] 5.3.49; ἥκειν ἐ. τοὺς τόκους}} **for** (i.e. _to demand_) the interest, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 50.61: less frequently with _accusative_ _person_, ἐπ᾽ Ὀδυσσῆα ἤϊε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.149, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “OT” 555; κατῆλθον ἐ. ποιητήν Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Ranae” 1418; κατέρχονται ἐ. τὸν Ἀγόρατον 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.23}}]: with _accusative_ of a Noun of Action, ἐξιέναι ἐ. θήραν go out hunting, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cyr.” 1.2.9; ἔπλεον οὐχ ὡς ἐ. ναυμαχίαν (variant for{-μαχίᾳ}) 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.83; ἐ. μάχην ἰέναι Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Anabasis” 1.4.12}}]; ἔρχεσθαι, ἵζειν ἐ. δεῖπνον, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.381, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.394; ἐ. δόρπον ἀνέστη 12.439; κληθεὶς ἐ. δεῖπνον Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Symposium” 174e, etc.; καλεῖν ἐ. ξείνια 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.107, 5.18; ἐ. τὴν θεωρίαν}}] **to see** the sight, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|“NT.Luke.23.48”, compare “PTeb.” 33.6 (2nd c.BC): frequently with _neuter_ _pronoun_}} or _adjective_, ἐ. τοῦτο ἐλθεῖν **for** this **purpose**, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “An.” 2.5.22, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.87; ἐπ᾽ αὐτὸ τοῦτο Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Gorgias” 447b}}], etc.; ἐ. τ; **to** what **end**? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nu.” 256; ἐφ᾽ ὅ τι [prev\. author] “Lys.” 22, 481; ἐφ᾽ ἃ ἤλθομεν}} **for** which **purpose**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.15, etc.; ἐπὶ ἴσα}} **for** like **ends**, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.5 (but ἐ. ἶσα μάχη τέτατο, ={ἴσως}, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.436); ἐ. τὸ βέλτιον}}] **to** a better **result**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “An.” 7.8.4; ἀναστῆσαί τινα ἐ. χριστὸν Θεοῦ}} set up **as** God's anointed, {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.23.1}}: after an _adjective_, ἄριστοι πᾶσαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἐπ᾽ ἰθύν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.79, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.434; ἄπορος ἐ. φρόνιμα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 691}} (Lyric poetry); χρήσιμος [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|ἐ... οὐδέν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.31: after a Noun, ὁδὸς ἐ. τι Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.21}}]; ὄργανα ἐ. τι {{Refs|[prev. work] 6.2.34.}}
**C.III.2**. **so far as regards**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|τοὐπὶ τήνδε τὴν κόρην 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 889; ὅσον γε τοὐπ᾽ ἐμέ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1345}}; τοὐπί σε, τὸ ἐ. σέ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev\. author] “Hec.” 514, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cyr.” 1.4.12; τὸ ἐ. σφᾶς εἶναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.28}}; ὡς ἐ. τὸ πολύ **for** the most part, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Top.” 100b29, etc.; ἐ. πᾶν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.51}}; τὸ πρὸς ἅπαν {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|ξυνετὸν ἐ. πᾶν ἀργόν [prev\. author] 3.82; κρείσσων ἐπ᾽ ἀρετήν 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 181}}; ἐ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|μέγα 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Dianam” 55}}.
**C.III.3**. of persons set **over** others, ἐ. τοὺς πεζοὺς [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|καθιστάναι ἄρχοντα Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.58, compare “HG” 3.4.20; στρατηγὸς ἐ. τοὺς ὁπλίτας, ἐ. τὴν χώραν, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ath.” 61.1, “IG” 22.682.24; ἐ. τὸν Πειραιέα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ath.” [same place]; ἐ. Ῥαμνοῦντα “IG” 2.1206b (compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 111.1); οἱ θεσμοθέται οἱ ἐ. τοὺς νόμους κληρούμενοι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.90}}].
**C.III.4**. **according to, by**, ἐ. στάθμην **by** the rule, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.245, 21.44, etc.}}
**D**. POSITION:—ἐπί may suffer anastrophe (ἔπι) and follow its case, as in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.162; it may like wise follow its Verb, ἤλυθ᾽ ἔπι ψυχή 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.20, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.539.}}
**D.II**. in Poets it is sometimes put with the second of two Nouns, though in sense it also governs the first, ἢ ἁλὸς ἢ ἐ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|γῆς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.27, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “OT” 761, “Ant.” 367 (Lyric poetry).}}
**E**. AB{{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, used adverbially, without anastrophe, καὶ ἐ. σκέπας ἦν ἀνέμοιο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.443}}; κτεῖνον δ᾽ ἐ. μηλοβοτῆρας **as well**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.529; especially ἐ. δέ..}} and **besides.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.65, 75, etc.; πολιαί τ᾽ ἐ. ματέρες 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 182}} (Lyric poetry).
**E.II**. ἔπι, for ἔπεστι, **there is**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.515, 3.45, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.315; οὐ γὰρ ἔπ᾽ ἀνήρ..}} **there is** no man.., [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|2.58; σοὶ δ᾽ ἔ. μὲν μορφὴ ἐπέων 11.367; ἔ. δέ μοι γέρας Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Eumenides” 393}}] codices (Lyric poetry).
**F**. PROSODY: in ἐπιόψομαι, ι is not elided before a vowel; also in some words where σ or ϝ has been lost, as ἐπιάλμενος, ἐπιείκελος, ἐπιεικής, ἐπιέξομαι (v. {{Refs|ἐπέχω v11). _Doric dialect_ ἐπιεργάζομαι (v. ἐπιεργάζομαι).}}
**G**. IN COMPOSITION:
**G.I**. of Place, denoting,
**G.I.1**. Support or Rest **upon**, ἔπειμι (A), ἐπίκειμαι, ἐπικαθίζω, ἐπαυχένιος, ἐπιβώμιος, etc.
**G.I.2**. Motion,
**G.I.2.a**. **upon** or **over**, ἐπιβαίνω, ἐπιτρέχω.
**G.I.2.b**. **to** or **towards**, ἐπέρχομαι, ἐπιστέλλω, ἐπαρίστερος, ἐπιδέξιος.
**G.I.2.c**. **against**, {{Refs|ἐπαΐσσω, ἐπιπλέω 11}}, ἐπιστρατεύω, ἐπιβουλεύω.
**G.I.2.d**. **up to** a point, ἐπιτελέω.
**G.I.2.e**. **over** a place, as in ἐπαιωρέομαι, ἐπαρτάω.
**G.I.2.f**. **over** or **beyond** boundaries, as in ἐπινέμομαι.
**G.I.2.g**. implying reciprocity, as in ἐπιγαμία.
**G.I.3**. Extension **over** a surface, as in ἐπαλείφω, ἐπανθίζω, {{Refs|ἐπιπέτομαι, ἐπιπλέω 1}}, ἐπάργυρος, ἐπίχρυσος.
**G.I.4**. Accumulation of one thing **over** or **besides** another, as in ἐπαγείρω, ἐπιμανθάνω, ἐπαυξάνω, ἐπιβάλλω, ἐπίκτητος.
**G.I.5**. Accompaniment, **to, with**, as in ἐπᾴδω, ἐπαυλέω, ἐπαγρυπνέω: hence of Addition, ἐπίτριτος one **and** 1/3 more, 1 + 1/3; so ἐπιτέταρτος, ἐπίπεμπτος, ἐπόγδοος, etc.
**G.I.6**. with _adjectives_, **somewhat, slightly**, as in ἐπίξανθος, ἐπίπικρος.
**G.II**. of Time and Sequence, **after**, as in ἐπιβιόω, ἐπιβλαστάνω, ἐπιγίγνομαι, {{Refs|ἐπακόλουθος, ἐπίγονος, ἐπιστάτης 1.2}}.
**G.III**. in causal senses:
**G.III.1**. Superiority felt **over** or **at**, as in ἐπιχαίρω, ἐπιγελάω, ἐπαισχύνομαι.
**G.III.2**. Authority **over**, as in ἐπικρατέω, ἔπαρχος, ἐπιβουκόλος, ἐπιποιμήν.
**G.III.3**. Motive **for**, as in ἐπιθυμέω, ἐπιζήμιος, ἐπιθάνατος.
**G.III.4**. to give force or intensity to the Verb, as in ἐπαινέω, ἐπιμέμφομαι, ἐπικείρω, ἐπικλάω.

1910:
** ἐπιβαίνω**,
rarely **ἐπηχ-βάω**, _imperative_ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|ἐπίβᾱ 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 847}}, _Doric dialect_ _infinitive_ ἐπιβῆν (belowIV): _future_ -βήσομαι: _perfect_ -βέβηκα: _aorist 2_ ἐπέβην: _aorist 1_ _middle_ ἐπεβησάμην (of which Hom.always uses the _Epic dialect_ form ἐπεβήσετο, _imperative_ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἐπιβήσεο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.105, +others; later ἐπεβήσατο 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.869, _Doric dialect_ -βάσατο 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Lavacrum Palladis” 65).}}
**A**. in these tenses, _intransitive_, **go upon**:
**I**. with _genitive_, **set foot on, tread, walk upon**, γαίης, ἠπείρου, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.83, “Hymnus in Cererem” 127; πόληος, πατρίδος αἴης, Τροίης, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.396, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.521, 14.229; ἀδύτων ἐπιβάς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1034}} (Lyric poetry); ἐ. τῶν οὔρων **set foot on** the confines, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.125, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.103, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lg.” 778e; τῆς Λακωνικῆς ἐπὶ πολέμῳ Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.4.6}}]; πυρῆς ἐπιβάντ᾽ ἀλεγεινῆς, of a corpse, **placed upon.. **, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.99; πλατείᾳ τῇ ῥινὶ ἐ. τοῦ χείλους Philostratus Sophista [{2-3rd c.AD}] “Philostrati majoris imagines” 2.18; also ἐ. ἐπί τινος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.107}}].
**I.2**. **get upon, mount on**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|πύργων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.165}}; νεῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 512; ἵππων 5.328, 10.513; δίφρου 23.379; εὐνῆς 9.133; τοῦ τείχεος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.70}}; λέκτρων ἐ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supp.” 39; also ἐ. ἐπὶ νεός 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.118: frequently in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, in _aorist_ _middle_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἐπεβήσετ᾽ ἀπήνης 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.78}}, +others
**I.2.b**. Architecture texts, **to be superposed**, {{Refs|τὰ ἐπιβαίνοντα πάντα ἐπὶ τοὺς κρατευτάς “IG” 7.3073.104}}, compare 111 (Lebad.).
**I.3**. of Time, **arrive at**, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|τετταράκοντα ἐ. ἐτῶν Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Leges” 666b}}]; δεκάτω (i.e. ἔτεος) ἐ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 26.29; δωδεκάτου ἐπιβάς “IG” 14.1728; τῆς μειρακίων ἡλικίας 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 1.3.1}}.
**I.4**. _metaphorically_, ἀναιδείης ἐπέβησαν **have trodden the path of** shamelessness, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.424; ἐϋφροσύνης ἐπιβῆτον}} **enter into** joy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|23.52; τέχνης ἐπιβήσομαι,-βήμεναι, “Hymnus ad Mamercus Elegiacus” 166, 465; ὁσίης [prev. work] 173; εὐσεβίας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 189}} (Lyric poetry); ἐ. δόξης **entertain** an expectation, {{Refs|[prev\. author] “Ph.” 1463 (_anapaest meter_); ἐ. σοφίας}} **undertake** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epin.” 981a; λόγου 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Astr.” 8}}; ἐ. τῆς ἀφορμῆς, τῆς προφάσεως, **seize upon** it, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Syr.” 2, “Sam.” 11}}, etc.; **preside over**, τῆς ἀνθρωπίνης {{Refs 4th c.AD+|ψυχῆς 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 9.8}}, +others
**II**. with _dative_, **get upon, board**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ναυσί 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.70}}; **land on**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|ἐ. τῇ Σικελίᾳ 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.66}}: _metaphorically_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἐ. ἀνορέαις 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.20}}; also, **make forcible entry into**, τινός οἰκίαις, γῇ, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PHamb.” 10.6 (2nd c.AD), “PAmh.” 2.142.7 (4th c.AD).}}
**II.b**. with a _preposition_, ἐπὶ πύργῳ ἄλλος πύργος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἐπιβέβηκε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.181}}.
**II.2**. with _dative_ _person_, **set upon, assault**, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|τινί Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.26, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cim.” 15}}], etc.; simply, **approach**, uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “fragment” 88.2.}}
**II.3**. **trample on**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|λὰξ ἐπίβα δήμῳ 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 847}}.
**III**. with _accusative_ loci, **light upon**, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} twice of gods **lighting upon** earth after their descent from Olympus, Πιερίην ἐπιβᾶσα, ἐπιβάς, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.226, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.50; so πολλῶν ἐ. καιρόν}} **light on** the fit time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.18}}; then simply, **go on to** a place, **enter** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|γῆν καὶ ἔθνος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.50; λειμῶν᾽ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 144}} (_anapaest meter_): with _preposition_, ἐ. ἐπὶ χώραν Decrees Amphict. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.154; εἰς Βοιωτίαν 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14. 84.}}
**III.2**. rarely with _accusative_ _person_, **attack**, only _poetry_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Aj.” 138 (_anapaest meter_): _metaphorically_, of passion or suffering, [prev\. author] “El.” 492 (Lyric poetry), “Ph.” 194 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**III.3**. **mount**, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|νῶθ᾽ ἵππων ἐπιβάντες {8-7th c.BC}: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 286}}]: more frequently with _preposition_, ἐπὶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|τὸν ἵππον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.22; ἐπὶνέα [prev\. author] 8.120, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.111; but ἐ. ἐπὶ τὸ θῆλυ}}, of made quadrupeds, **cover** a female, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “HA” 539b26; so _absolutely_, [prev. work] 574a20, +others: with _dative_, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 27: with _genitive_, 4th c.AD(?): Horapollo 1.46, 2.78.}}
**III.4**. ἐ. ἐπὶ τὸ σκέλος **use, put one's weight on**, a broken leg, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Fract.” 18.}}
**III.5**. with _accusative_ of the Instr. of Motion (compare [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|βαίνω Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] 11.4), ἐπιβῆναι τῷ ἀριστερῷ ἐκείνης τὸν ἐμὸν δεξιόν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMeretr.” 4.5, compare “Tox.” 48.}}]
**IV**. _absolutely_, **get a footing, stand on one's feet**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.666, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.434; μἠπιβῆν}} it is forbidden to **set foot** here, {{Refs|“IG” 12(3).1381 (from Thera).}}
**IV.2**. **step onwards**, **advance**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Τρώων δὲ πόλις ἐπὶ πᾶσα βέβηκε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.69, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Op.” 679, _falsa lectio_ in 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.43; ἐπίβαινε πόρσω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 179}} (assuming variant, Lyric poetry): me taph., **advance in one's demands**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.68.8.}}
**IV.3**. **mount on a** **chariot** or **on horseback, be mounted**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.84}}; **go** or **be on board ship**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.387, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Aj.” 358 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.90, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.90, etc.}}
**B**. Causal in _future_ **-βήσω** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 6.4}}, _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ **-βησέμεν** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.197, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Th.” 396}}, but usually in _aorist 1_ _active_ (ἐπιβιβάζω, ἐπιβάσκω serve as _present_):—**make** one **mount, set** him **upon**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ὅν ῥα τόθ᾽ ἵππων.. ἐπέβησε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.129; πολλοὺς δὲ πυρῆς ἐπέβησ᾽ ἀλεγεινῆς 9.546}}; ὥς κ᾽ ἐμὲ.. ἐμῆς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἐπιβήσετε πάτρης 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.223; ἐ. τινὰς σκάφεσιν 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 4.7.6}}; πλοίων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 11.5, cf. Luc.[same place]; ὁπλίτας ὁλκάσιν 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 5.92}}; τινὰς ἐπὶ τὰς ναῦς {{Refs|[prev. work] 2.59}}: also in _aorist 1_ _middle_, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|νιν ἑῶ ἐπεβάσατο δίφρω 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Lavacrum Palladis” 65}}.
**B.b**. of things, νευρὰν ἐπέβασε κορώνας **set** the string **on** his bow's tip, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 5.73.}}
**B.2**. _metaphorically_ (compare{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 1.4), ἐϋκλεΐης ἐπίβησον}} **bring** **to** great glory, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.285; τιμῆς καὶ γεράων {8-7th c.BC}: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 396}}]; χαλιφρονέοντα σαοφροσύνης ἐπέβησαν they **bring** him **to** sobriety, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.13; λιγυρῆς ἐπέβησαν ἀοιδῆς {8-7th c.BC}: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 659}}]; δουλοσύνας (probably) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hyps.fragment” 41(64).86; εἴ σε τύχη.. ἡλικίας ἐπέβησεν}} **had brought thee to** full age, {{Refs|“IG” 2.2263.}}
**B.3**. [ἠὼς] πολέας ἐπέβησε κελεύθου dawn **sets** them **on** their way, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Op.” 580.}}

1911:
** ἐπιβάλλω**:
**I**. _transitive_, **throw** or **cast upon**, θριξὶ.., ἃς ἐπέβαλλον (i.e. πυρί) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.135; ἐπὶ δὲ χλαῖναν βάλεν αὐτῷ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.520, compare 4.440; ἑωυτὸν ἐς τὸ πῦρ variant in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.107; φάρη κόραις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1221}} (Lyric poetry); ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|τινὰς ἐπὶ ἁμάξας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.48, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.75, 5.112; ἐπιβάλλοντας (i.e. χοῦν)}} **throwing on more and more**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.76.}}
**I.2**. **lay on**, [ἡμιόνοις] ἐπέβαλλεν [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἱμάσθλην 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.320; ἐ. πληγάς τινι Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 2.8}}]; Ζεὺς ἐπὶ χεῖρα [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|βάλοι Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Choephori” 395 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nu.” 933 (_anapaest meter_); ἐ.τὴν χεῖρά τινι [prev\. author] “Lys.” 440}}] (but τῷ καρπῷ τοῦ νοσοῦντος τὴν χεῖρα, of feeling the pulse, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).40; so τὴν ἁφήν [prev\. author] 8.821, 4th c.AD(?): Marcellinus Biographus “Puls.” 119); τὰς χεῖρας τοῖς κατ᾽ Αἴγαιον 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.2.8; Ῥωμαίοις [prev\. author] 18.51.8; ἐπί τινα NT.Matt.26.50}}; **impose** as a tax tribute, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|τινί τι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.106, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.108; as a fine or penalty, ζημίην, φυγὴν ἐ. τινί, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.92 (_passive_), 7.3; ἀργύριον 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 9.6; ἐπιβολάς [prev\. author] 20.14, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ath.” 61.2; λύτρα Vetus Testamentum Graece redditum LXX.Exo.21.30}} (_passive_); **inflict**, θνατοῖς ἐ. ἀνάλγητα, λύπην, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Tr.” 128 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1115 (_anapaest meter_), etc.}}
**I.3**. ἐ. σφρηγῖδα, δακτύλιον, **affix** a seal, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.128, 2.38; σφραγῖδ᾽ ἐπί τι Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Aves” 559}}]; σύμβολόν τινι {{Refs|[prev. work] 1215.}}
**I.4**. **add, contribute**, μικρὸν [ἀληθεία] [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphorical” 993b2; ἐ. ἐπὶ τὸ ὕδωρ Theophrastus Philosophus [{4-3rd c.BC}] “de Igne” 49}}]; νέον [φῶς] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cra.” 409b: _metaphorically_}}, **throw in, mention**, τι uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “El.” 1246 (Lyric poetry) (in _middle_, χαίρειν τεοῖς προθύροις ἐπιβάλλομαι 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 23.27); Φαῖστος.. ἐπιβάλλων φησί Scholia 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.28: _absolutely_}}, **bid higher**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Pol.” 1259a14.}}
**I.5**. **place next in order**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.26.15.}}
**I.6**. **let grow**, [{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|κλήματα Theophrastus Philosophus [{4-3rd c.BC}] “Historia Plantarum” 4.13.5}}]; βλαστούς {{Refs|[prev. work] 3.5.1.}}
**I.7**. **let loose**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|πρόβατα ἐπὶ κνῆκον “PRyl.” 69.6 (1st c.BC).}}
**I.8**. causal of [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἐπιβαίνω Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] 111.3, 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 7.134.}}]
**II**. **throw oneself upon, go** **straight towards**, with _accusative_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἡ δὲ Φεὰς ἐπέβαλλεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.297}}: later with _dative_ loci, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.18.3, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.30, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.7.12, etc.; νήσοις 3rd c.BC: Rhianus Epicus 39}}; εἰς Ιταλίαν, ἐπὶ τὸν τόπον, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.24.17, 5.6.6, compare “PAmh.” 2.31.5 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}
**II.2**. **fall upon**, ὅπου ἂν ὁ ἥλιος ἐ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “HA” 598a3}}; especially in hostile sense, **set upon**, c._dative_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. work] 623b1, etc.; τοῖσ᾽ Αρβήλοις 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.64}}: _absolutely_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|ἐ.ληστρικῷ τρόπῳ “PRyl.” 127.10 (1st c.AD); ἐπιβάλλουσαι}} **jostling, trampling**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phdr.” 248a; obscene meaning, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Av.” 1216.}}
**II.3**. (i.e. τὸν νοῦν) **set to** a thing, **devote oneself to** it, with _dative_, [{{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 10.30; τοῖς αὐλοῖς 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.59; τοῖς κοινοῖς πράγμασιν Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus [{1-2nd c.AD}] “Cicero” 4}}] (in full τὴν διάνοιαν ἐ. πρός τι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.43)}}: generally, **give one's attention to, think on,** NT.Mark.14.72.
**II.3.b**. **apprehend**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “fragment” 423}}; **attain by intuition**, c._dative_, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “Pr.” 54.}}
**II.4**. **fall in one's way**, ὅταν ἐπιβάλλῃ περὶ τῆς τοιαύτης {{Refs 4th c.BC+|πολιτείας ἡ σκέψις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1266a25; κατὰ τὸν ἐπιβάλλοντα λόγον [prev\. author] “GA” 716a3}}.
**II.5**. **follow, come next**, [{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 11.23.2; τισί Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus [{1-2nd c.AD}] “Aemilius Paulus” 33}}]; ἐφ᾽ ὃν ἐπιβαλὼν ἔφη said **thereupon**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.80.1}}; **interrupt**, [{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|ἀποκρινομένῳ Theophrastus Philosophus [{4-3rd c.BC}] “Characteres” 7.2}}].
**II.6**. **belong to, fall to the share of**, μόριον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ὅσον αὐτοῖσι ἐπέβαλλε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.23, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 43.16; εἰ μὴ τὸ ὅλον, μέρος γε, ἐπιβάλλει τῆς βλασφημίας ἅπασι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.272; ὅσον ἐπιβάλλει αὐτοῖς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1260a19}}; ἑκάστῳ τῆς εὐδαιμονίας ἐπιβάλλει τοσοῦτον ὅσονπερ ἀρετῆς {{Refs|[prev. work] 1323b21; τῶν κτημάτων τὸ ἐπιβάλλον (i.e. μέρος)}} **the** **portion that falls to one**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.115, cf. LXX.Tob.3.17, 6.12; so τὸ ἐ. ἐφ᾽ ἡμᾶς μέρος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.254; τὸ ἐ. μέρος τῆς οὐσίας NT.Luke.15.12, compare “PGrenf.” 1.33.33 (2nd c.BC)}}, etc.; **fall due**, of payments, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PLond.” 1.3.21 (2nd c.BC); τόκον ὃν ἔφη ἐπιβάλλειν αὑτῷ}} which **was payable by** him, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“BCH” 6.21 (Delos, 2nd c.BC).}}
**II.6.b**. _participle_ ἐπιβάλλων, in Law, **next-of-kin**, ὁ ἐ., οἱ ἐ., {{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 7.36, 11.42, +others}}
**II.7**. _impersonal_ with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, τοὺς Δελφοὺς δὴ ἐπέβαλλε.. παρασχεῖν **it concerned** them to provide, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.180: or with _dative_}} et _infinitive_, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|ἐπιβάλλει τινὶ ποιεῖν τι 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.39, +others, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.51.1; ἐπιβάλλοντος ἡμῖν εὐεργετικοῖς εἶναι 1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 15}}; κοινῇ πᾶσιν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|ἐπιβάλλει “UPZ” 112.10 (2nd c.BC); καθότι ἐπέβαλλεν ἀνδρὶ καλῷ καὶ ἀγαθῷ “IG” 12(7).231.5 (from Amorgos)}}: frequently in _participle_, ἐπιβάλλουσαν ἡγεῖσθαι τὴν στρατείαν τινί **incumbent** upon.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Teles Philosophus p.61 H.; τὸ ἐπιβάλλον 4th-3rd c.BC: Cleanthes Stoicus 1.128, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epict.” 2.11.3, etc.; τὰς -ούσας τάσεις τῆς φωνῆς 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.96}}; τὸ τῇ φύσει ἐ. {{Refs|Antip.Stoic. 3.255}}; **appropriate**, ὑποδοχαί Teles{{Refs 4th c.AD+|p.41 H.; ἰήματα “IG” 22.1121.15; ἁρμονία 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “Comm.” “in Mathematics texts” 30; ἡ στέρησις ἐπιβάλλοντός ἐστι παρεῖναι εἴδους τινός}} a specific form which **ought** to be present, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.8.11.}}
**II.8**. **shut to, close**, of the larynx, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “PA” 664b26.}}
**II.9**. in Logic, λόγοι ἐπιβάλλοντες, -όμενοι, **overlapping** and **overlapped**, of syllogisms in a sorites, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.85; so of Time, ἐπέβαλε τοῖς χρόνοις Ἰουλιανῷ Eunapius Historicus [{4-5th c.AD}] “Vitae Sophistarum” p.497 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus:}}] —_middle_, {{Refs|γηραιῷ τῷ Κυρηναίῳ ἐπεβάλετο Anonymus “Intr.Arat.” p.326M.}}
**II.10**. in Alchemy, **make a `projection'** (compare ἐπιβολή), Syn.Alch.p.68B.
**III**. _middle_, mostly like the _intransitive_ usages, but also:
**III.1**. with _genitive_, **throw oneself upon, desire eagerly**, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἐνάρων ἐπιβαλλόμενος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.68; παρθενίας ἐπιβάλλομαι Sappho Lyrica [{7-6th c.BC}] 102; τοῦ εὖ ζῆν ἐπιβάλλονται 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1258a3}}].
**III.2**. with _accusative_, **put upon oneself**, ἐπιβαλλομέναν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|.. πλόκον ἀνθέων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Med.” 840}}; ἐπιβάλλεσθαι **put on more** **wraps**, [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Char.” 2.10 (cf. IV. 1); ὕπνον ἡδὺν -όμενος Dio Chrysostomus Sophista [{1-2nd c.AD}] 12.51}}]: _metaphorically_, **take possession of**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|καὶ ἐπὶ κλήρους ἐβάλοντο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.209}}; αὐθαίρετον δουλείαν ἐπιβαλεῖται **will take upon himself**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.40.}}
**III.2.b**. of trees, **make fresh growth**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “HP” 3.5.1.}}
**III.3**. c._accusative_, also, **attempt, undertake**, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|ἔργον Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Sophista” 264b, “Ti.” 48c; μέθοδον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1260b36}}]: c._infinitive_, Decrees cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.164, 4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.68, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.43.2, etc.: _absolutely_, πολλῶν -ημένων}} though many have **made the attempt**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Agatharchides Geographus 76.}}
**III.4**. with _dative_, **put one's hand to**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|ἐχέτλῃ “AP” 7.650 (3rd c.BC: Phalaecus Epigrammaticus (?)): _metaphorically_}}, **apply** or **devote oneself to**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|τόλμῃ καὶ πράξει 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.81.1; ἐγχειρήματι μεγάλῳ 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.25}}, etc.
**III.5**. **arrive at**, [πολίεσσι] {{Refs 1st c.BC+|Callias Epigrammaticus “Del.” 68; ὅταν ἐπὶ τοὺς χρόνους ἐπιβαλώμεθα 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.55}}.
**III.6**. ἐπὶ πᾶσι -εβάλοντο **brought up the rear**, {{Refs|[prev\. author] 18.33.}}
**IV**. in _passive_, **lie upon, be put upon**, ἐπιβεβλημένοι τοξόται archers **with their** **arrows on the string**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “An.” 4.3.28, compare 5.2.12; λάσιον ἐπιβεβλημένος}} **having** a rough cloak **on**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Theopompus Comicus 36}}; τὸ ἐν ψύχει κεῖσθαι **-ημένον** [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 2.3.1, compare 6.4.14; διφθέραν -ημένη Dio Chrysostomus Sophista [{1-2nd c.AD}] 5.25}}].
**IV.2**. **to be set over**, [{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|ὁ τελώνης ὁ ἐπιβεβλημένος τῷ Ζεύγματι Philostratus Sophista [{2-3rd c.AD}] “Vita Apollonii” 1.20}}].
**IV.3**. Rhet., **ornate** (see. ἐπιβολή), ἰδέα λόγων οὔτ᾽ ἐπιβεβλημένη {{Refs|οὔτ᾽ αὖος [prev\. author] “VS” 1.20.2}}.

1912:
** ἐπιβαρ-έω**,
**weigh down**: hence, **be a burden to**, {{Refs|τινά “1st Epistle to the Thessalonians” 2.9}}; **overload**, of food, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|τὸν ὄγκον 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.108}}: with _dative_, **press** **heavily upon**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|τοῖς ἠτυχηκόσι 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 4.31, compare 15, 5.107:}} —_middle_, **lay** **a burden on oneself, trouble oneself**, c._infinitive_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1481.12 (2nd c.AD):}} — _passive_, with _future_ _middle_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἐπιβαρήσομαι 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.9, 8.73; ὑπό τινος “SIG” 807.16 (5th c.BC: Magnes Comicus Mae., 1st c.AD); ὑπὸ τῶν δανείων “IG” 12(5).860.9}} (Te nos).
**2**. _passive_, of a bandage, **to be found irksome,** {{Refs|PMed.Lond. 3.39. (Cf.ἐπιζαρέω.)}}

1914:
** ἐπι-βλέπω**,
_future_ -ψομαι, later **-ψω** {{LXX|Vetus Testamentum Graece redditum LXX.Lev.26.9}}:—**look upon, look attentively**, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|εἴς τινα Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Phaedo” 63a; ἐπὶ πόλιν Dinarchus Orator [{4-3rd c.BC}] 1.72}}]; ἐφ᾽ ἑαυτόν variant in {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “EN” 1120b6; πρός τινα Vetus Testamentum Graece redditum LXX.Hos.11.4; τινί 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Astr.” 20}}.
**2**. c._accusative_, **look well at**, **observe**, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|λόγους ἁθρόους Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Leges” 811d; αἰτίαν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1147a24; κοινωνίαν [prev\. author] “Metaphorical” 991a8}}]: c._accusative_ _double_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|ἄφθαρτον τὸν θεὸν ἐπεβλέψαμεν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3 “fragment” 39; τῆς κατὰ τὴν γαστέρα ταραχῆς -ομένης}} **being taken** **into consideration**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.673.}}
**3**. **face** upwards or downwards, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.120.}}
**II**. **eye with envy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|τύχαις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1526}}.
**III**. Astrology texts, **to be in aspect with**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Hephaestio Astrologus 3.20.}}

1915:
** ἐπί-βλημα**, ατος, τό,
**that which is thrown over, covering**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Nicostratus Philosophus Comedy texts 15}}; **coverlet, bedspread,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12(5).593.4 (Iulis, 5th c.BC), 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.638, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.85}}; **head-covering**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “UP” 11.12.}}
**2**. **tapestry, hangings**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Ma.” 4, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “An.” 6.29.5.}}
**II**. **that which is put on**, **piece of embroidery**, {{Refs|ἐ. ποικίλον “IG” 12.387.28}}, 22.1514.31; **mantle**, {{LXX|LXX.Isa.3.22.}}
**II.2**. **patch,** NT.Mat.9.16, etc.
**II.3**. **outer bandage**, Paul. Aeg.6.92.

1916:
** ἐπιβο-άω**,
_future_ -βοήσομαι, _Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ -βώσομαι (see.below):— **call** **upon** or **to, cry out to**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἐ. τινὶ ὅτι.. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.65}}; ἐ. τινί, with _infinitive_, **call on** one to do.., {{Refs|[prev\. author] 4.28, 7.70: with _accusative_}}, **invoke**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|θεόν “AP” 9.334 (4th c.AD: Perses Epigrammaticus): _absolutely_}}, of hounds, **give tongue**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cyn.” 6.19}}; **cry out**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epict.” 4.1.14}}; of **calling** **upon** the dead at funerals, {{Refs|“BMus.Inscription” 791, +others (from Cnidus).}}
**2**. **utter** or **sing aloud over**, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|τινί τι, μέλος χέρνιβι ἐπιβοᾶν Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Aves” 898}}]; **shriek out besides**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|στέρν᾽ ἄρασσε κἀπιβόα τὸ Μύσιον Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Persae” 1054 codices (Lyric poetry); ἔγχει, κἀπιβόα τρίτον παιῶνα 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 131.5}}].
**3**. _middle_, **cry** **out against**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMeretr.” 12.1:}} —_passive_, τὰ ἴδια ἐπιβοώμενος **cried out** **against** in regard to private matters, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.16.}}
**4**. **applaud, acclaim**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epict.” 3.23.10, 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 10.34.}}
**II**. _middle_, **invoke, call upon**, θεοὺς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἐπιβώσομαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.378}}; σὲ γὰρ πρώτην.. ἐπιβωσόμεθ᾽ (variant for ἐπιδωσόμεθ᾽) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.463; τὸν Ἀπόλλωνα ἐπιβώσασθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.87}}; ἐπιβοᾶται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Θέμιν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Med.” 168}} (Lyric poetry); θεοὺς.. ἐπιβοώμενοι, πατέρων τάφους ἐ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.59, 67; ὅρκους καὶ πίστεις 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 11.49}}; **call to aid**, τὴν ἄλλην στρατιὴν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἐπεβώσαντο 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.23, compare 5.1}}: c._infinitive_, ἐ. [τινὰ] μὴ ἀπολέσαι τὴν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|πατρίδα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.92}}.
**II.2**. c._accusative_ _of things_, **call out**, {{Refs|[prev\. author] 7.69.}}

1918:
** ἐπιγαμβρ-εύω**,
**become son-in-law**, {{LXX+6th c.AD+|τῷ βασιλεῖ Vetus Testamentum Graece redditum LXX.1Ki.18.22, compare 6th c.AD: Lydus, Joannes Laurentius Historicus “Mens.” 1.13.}}
**2**. **become father-in-law**, {{LXX|τινί Vetus Testamentum Graece redditum LXX.1Mac.10.54, 56.}}
**II**. ἐ. γυναῖκα **take** a woman **to wife as her husband's next of kin**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Gen.38.8 (variant), “NT.Mat.22.24”.}}
**III**. _middle_, **intermarry with**, {{LXX|LXX.Gen.34.9, LXX.2Ch.18.1.}}

1919:
** ἐπίγειος**, ον,
**on** or **of the earth, terrestrial**, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|ζῷα Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Respublica” 546a; βροτοί “IG” 14.1571}}]; opposed to ὑπόγειος, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PMag.Par.” 1.3043 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}
**2**. **creeping**, of plants, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “HP” 3.18.6, 6.2.2}}, +others; but **land**-plants, opposed to water-plants, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “PA” 681a21}}; **living on the ground**, [ὄρνιθες], τετράποδα, {{Refs|[prev\. author] “HA” 633b1, “PA” 657b24.}}
**3**. _neuter_ _plural_, ἐπίγεια **ground-floor**, opposed to πύργος διώρυφος, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.20 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II**. _substantive_ ἐπίγειον, τό, misspelling of ἐπίγυον, variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “fragment” 80, 426. (Cf. ἐπίγαιος.)}}

1920:
** ἐπιγίγνομαι**,
_Ionic dialect_ and later **ἐπιγενν-γίνομαι** [γῑ], _future_ ἐπιγενήσομαι: _aorist 2_ ἐπεγενόμην: _perfect_ ἐπιγέγονα:
**I**. of Time, **to be born after, come** **into being after**, ἔαρος δ᾽ ἐπιγίγνεται ὥρῃ (i.e. φύλλα, nisi to be read ὥρη) {{Refs|11.6.148}}; of persons, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.2; οἱ ἐπιγινόμενοι ἄνθρωποι}} **posterity**, {{Refs|[prev\. author] 9.85; οἱ ἐπιγενόμενοι τούτῳ σοφισταί}} **who came after** him, {{Refs|[prev\. author] 2.49; ἀνάγκη τὰ -όμενα κρατεῖν}} the **new** must prevail over the old, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.71, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “R.” 574a; ἀντὶ τῶν ἀποθανόντων ἕτεροι.. [ἐπιγενήσονται] 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cyr.” 6.1.12, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.26; τῇ ἐπιγιγνομένῃ ἡμέρᾳ}} the **following**, the **next**.., {{Refs|[prev\. author] 3.75; τοῦ ἐπιγιγνομένου θέρους [prev\. author] 4.52}}; χρόνου ἐπιγενομένου as time **went on**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.28, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.126 (variant); χρόνος.. παρὰ λόγον ἐπιγιγνόμενος [prev\. author] 4.26}}; τὰ ἐπὶ τούτῳ ἐπιγενόμενα **that happened after**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.37.}}
**I.2**. **follow**, of a fleet, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.77.}}
**II**. of things, **come** **at the end, come as fulfilment**, with _dative_, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|βουλαῖς οὐκ ἐπέγεντο τέλος 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 640}}; τὸ τέλος, ἡ τελευτὴ ἐ. τινί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.65, 7.157; especially of sudden changes of weather and the like}}, **supervene**, καί σφι.. ἅμα τῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|βροντῇ σεισμὸν ἐπιγενέσθαι [prev\. author] 5.85; πλέουσι αὐτοῖσι χειμὼν.. ἐπεγίνετο [prev\. author] 8.13, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.3; ἐ. σφι τέρεα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.37}}; νὺξ ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 70; νὺξ ἐ. τῷ ἔργῳ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.25}}; ἄνεμος ἐπεγένετο τῇ φλογί **seconded** the flame, {{Refs|[prev\. author] 3.74: _absolutely_, τοσαύτη ἡ ξυμφορὰ ἐπεγεγένητο [prev\. author] 8.96, compare 1.16; ἐπιγενομένου νότου “Act.Ap.” 28.13}}; τὴν ἐπιγινομένην ἡδονὴν..τοῖς ἔργοις the **supervening** pleasure, [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “EN” 1104b4; μέμψις καὶ μετάνοια ἐ. πράξεσι Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus [{1-2nd c.AD}] “Timoleon” 36}}].
**II.2**. **come in after**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἐπὶ τῇ ναυμαχίῃ ἐ. Ἱστιαῖος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.27, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Eq.” 136}}; **come upon, assault, attack**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|τινί 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.30, 4.93}}; ἀφυλάκτοις καὶ ἐξαίφνης ἐ. {{Refs|[prev\. author] 7.32, compare 3.108}}; of disease, ἀρρωστία ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 36.7: frequently in 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus}} of additional symptoms, **supervene**, {{Refs|“Aph.” 5.2, “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 69, +others}}
**II.3**. **befall, come to pass**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.20.}}
**II.4**. **fall** **to** one, **become due**, μισθώσεις ἐ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 36.9; τὰ ἐπιγινόμενα}} **the accruing** **interest**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Pol.” 1280a30, compare “IG” 12.236, +others, “BGU” 8 ii 4 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}
**II.5**. **to be incident to**, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|δόξῃ ἐ. ψεῦδός τε καὶ ἀληθές Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Philebus” 37b}}].
**II.6**. **to be added**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|πρός τι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 297a32}}.

1921:
** ἐπιγιγνώσκω**,
_Ionic dialect_ and later**ἐπιγενν-γῑνώσκω**, **look upon, witness, observe**, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|ἵνα πάντες ἐπιγνώωσι.. μαρναμένους 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.30; τινὰ ὀργιζόμενον Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.33, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Aj.” 18: rarely with _genitive_, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.279.}}]
**II**. **recognize**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|αἴ κέ μ᾽ ἐπιγνώῃ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.217}}; ὅπως μήτηρ σε μὴ 'πιγνώσεται φαιδρῷ προσώπῳ by thy glad face, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “El.” 1296, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Tht.” 192e (variant).}}
**II.2**. of things, **find out, discover**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἔργον Aeschylus Tragicus [{4-5th c.BC}] “Agamemnon” 1598, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.132, etc.; τὰ γεγονότα 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.11.3}}]; ἐπιγνοίης ἂν αὐτὴν [τὴν σοφίαν].. οἰκείαν γενομένη; **would you recognize** when it became your own?, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthd.” 301e; ἐπιγνοὺς ἄνδρα δίκαιον “IG” 9(2).313 (from Tricca); ἐ.ὅτι.. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “HA” 631b11; τὸν πόλεμον ἐ. τίνα φύσιν ἔχει 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.65.6, compare “POxy.” 930.14 (2nd-3rd c.AD); ἐ. εἰ.. Vetus Testamentum Graece redditum LXX.Gen.37.32, “PFay.” 112.14 (1st c.AD):—}}_passive_, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Phylarchus Historicus 10 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}
**II.2.b**. **find out too late**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|ἐπιγνώσῃ τί σπάνις ἐστὶ φίλων “AP” 12.186 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus).}}
**II.3**. **learn to know**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|θεόν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 960}} (Lyric poetry).
**II.4**. **take notice** **of**, {{LXX|LXX.Ruth.2.10.}}
**II.4.b**. **show favour to**, πρόσωπον {{LXX|LXX.Deu.16.19.}}
**III**. **come to a judgement, decide**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|τι περί τινος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.57}}; τὰ πρόσφορα τοῖς {{Refs|οἰχομένοις [prev\. author] 2.65}}; ἐπιγνῶναι μηδέν **come to** no **new resolve**, {{Refs|[prev\. author] 1.70; ἐ. τι εἶναί τινος}} **adjudicate** it as his property, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 11.52.}}
**IV**. **recongnize, acknowledge, approve**, {{NT+4th c.BC+|“NT.1Cor.16.18”; ἐ.σε τῆς ἐπιμελείας Chio Epistolographus [4th c.BC] “Ep.” 6}}.
**IV.2**. **recognize** an obligation, **undertake** to discharge or deliver, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PLips.” 22.14 (4th c.AD), etc.}}

1922:
** ἐπί-γνωσις**, εως, ἡ,
**recognition**, c._genitive_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Lib.” p.49O.; σφραγίδων 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 7.6.7}}; [τινῶν] διὰ βοῆς, δι᾽ ὀμμάτων, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “BJ” 6.2.6; ἐς ἐπίγνωσιν 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Praefatio” 13}}; **recognition** of a mistake, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.114}}; **determination** of a fact, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 24.23 (2nd c.BC).}}
**2**. **knowledge**, [{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|τινός 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.7.6, 3.31.4, 2nd c.BC: Attalus Grammaticus cited in 2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus 1.8.10, compare 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus “Bel.59.2”; τοῦ μέλλοντος 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.222; μουσικῆς Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus [{1-2nd c.AD}] 2.1145a; θεοῦ Vetus Testamentum Graece redditum “Proverbs” 2.5, compare “Ep.” “Rom.” 10.2, etc.; τὸν θεὸν ἔχειν ἐν ἐπιγνώσει [prev. work]1.28, etc.; λαβὼν κανόνας εἰς ἐπίγνωσιν τῆς ἀληθείας 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.20.21, compare 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “M.” 7.259; ἐ. ἐπιστημονική}}] scientific **theory,** {{Refs|Theol.Ar. 17.}}
**II**. **decision**, πρὸς {{Refs 4th c.AD+|-σιν κεκαθίκασιν “SIG” 826 D 16; θεῶν ἐ. 4th c.AD: Himerius Sophista “Or.” 1.17.}}

1923:
** ἐπιγρᾰφ-ή**, ἡ,
**inscription**, στηλῶν on stones, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.43, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mir.” 843b17, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “AJ” 15.8.1; on a vase, “BCH” 6.6 (Delos, 2nd c.BC); on statues, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.9.3.}}
**2**. **title** of a work, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev\. author] 3.9.3, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 30, etc.}}
**3**. **name** of a ship, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“OGI” 447 (1st c.BC).}}
**4**. **ascription** of a deed to its author, **credit** or **honour** of a thing, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|τὴν ἐ. τινος λαβεῖν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.31.4, compare “PSI” 4.424.9 (3rd c.BC); ἀπενέγκασθαι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.50.compare 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Caes.” 322d.}}
**5**. **insertion, interlineation** in a document, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PLond.” 2.178.13 2nd c.AD), “PRyl.” 316.2 (_plural_, 2nd c.AD).}}
**II**. **description** of parties in pleadings,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 4.2.}}
**II.2**. **registration** **of property for taxation**, in _plural_, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 17.41 codices (see. {ἐπιγραφεύς}).}}]
**II.3**. **impost, tax**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 5.510.11 (3rd c.BC)}}; **assessment,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|POxy. 1445.8(2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**II.4**. **requisition**, {{Refs|ὑποζυγίων “BCH” 46.309 (from Teos).}}

1924:
** ἐπιγρᾰφ-ω**,
**mark the surface, graze**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|ὀϊστὸς ἐπέγραψε χρόα φωτός 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.139, compare 13.553, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.179; μιν ἐπιγράψας}} having **put a mark on** the lot, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.187; ἄκροις δακτύλοις ἐ}}. **trifle with** dishes, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 42.—In 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} the word has not the sense of **writing.**
**II**. **write upon, inscribe**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|γράμματα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.88; τάδε [prev\. author] 4.88; ἐ. ὀνομαστὶ τὰς πόλεις 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.132, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.97; ἐπίγραμμα ὃ.. προείλεθ᾽ ἡ πόλις αὐτοῖς ἐπιγράψαι [prev\. author] 18.289}}: _absolutely_, {{Refs|ἐ. τοῖς ἀναθήμασι “IG” 12.76.43}}; especially **write** or **place an epitaph** on a tomb, {{Refs|[prev. work]14.1835, +others, 7.2543.9:}}_middle_, **have inscribed**, ἐπεγράφου [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|τὴν Γοργόνα Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Acharnenses” 1095 (with play on 111.5); ἐλεγεῖον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.132}}]:—_passive_, of the inscription, **to be inscribed upon**, ἐπιγέγραπταί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|οἱ τάδε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.77, compare 7.228; τῶν τῷ χρυσέῳ ἐπιγέγραπται Λακεδαιμονίων [prev\. author] 1.51}}; [ἐπίγραμμα] ὃ Μίδᾳ φασὶν ἐπιγεγράφθαι **over** or **on** **the tomb of** Midas, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phdr.” 264c; ἐπιστολὴ -γεγραμμένη}} **addressed**, of a letter, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.36.4, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cic.” 15}}; also, **to have** something **inscribed** **upon one**, ἐπεγράφοντο ῥόπαλα, ὡς Θηβαῖοι ὄντες **used to bear** clubs **upon their shields**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “HG” 7.5.20; so ἀσπὶς ἐπιγεγραμμένη τὰς ὁμολογίας}} **having** the articles **inscribed upon it**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.58.}}
**II.2**. **entitle**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|τοῦτο τὸ δρᾶμα Καλλίμαχος ἐ. Εὐνοῦχον 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 11.496f}}; αἱ -όμεναι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|Μαιανδρίου ἱστορίαι “Inscription Prien.” 37.104 (2nd c.BC).}}
**II.3**. **sign, append** **a signature to**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|ἄφεσιν “PSI” 4.392.6 (3rd c.BC); ἐ. τὸν Ἀντώνιον}} **sign** Antonius' **name**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “BC” 5.144; αὑτοῦ ποιήματα ἐπέγραψεν (i.e. τοῖς Ἑρμαῖς)}} **inscribed** poems **signed** by himself, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hipparch.” 228d.}}
**II.4**. **write subsequently**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|αἱ ἐπιγραφεῖσαι διαθῆκαι 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 17.9.4}}.
**II.5**. **write** **over** an erasure, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 34.14 (2nd c.AD).}}
**III**. frequently as law-term: {{Refs|1}}. **set down** the penalty or damages **in the title** of an indictment (compare {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἐπίγραμμα 4), τί δῆτά σοι τίμημ᾽ ἐπιγράψω τῇ δίκ; 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pl.” 480; μέχρι πεντήκοντα δραχμῶν καθ᾽ ἕκαστον ἀδίκημα ἐ. Legal cited in 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.38; τὰ ἐπιγεγραμμένα}} the **damages claimed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.8, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lg.” 915a}}; τιμημάτων **-μένων** [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|Isocrates Orator [{5-6th c.BC}] 16.47}}]:—_middle_, Legal cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.16.}}
**III.b**. of a lawgiver, **assign** a punishment, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|τὰ μέγιστα ἐπιτίμια 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.14}}:— _passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 2.12.}}
**III.c**. **make note of, enter**, τὴν πρόφασιν, in inflicting a fine, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ath.” 8.4.}}
**III.2**. **register** the citizens' names and property, with a view to taxes, **lay a public burden upon** one (compare [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|ἐπιγραφή 11.2), ἐμαυτῷ.. τὴν μεγίστην εἰσφοράν Isocrates Orator [{5-6th c.BC}] 17.41, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Oec.” 1351b2; ἐ. δήμοις καὶ δυνάσταις στρατιωτῶν καταλόγους Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus [{1-2nd c.AD}] “Crassus” 17, compare “PHib.” 1.44.3 (3rd c.BC, _passive_), etc.; but ἐ. τινὰ προστίμοις}}] **visit** with penalties, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 12.12 (assuming variant).}}
**III.2.b**. **assess**, {{Refs|τὸ τρίτον μέρος “PEdgar” 38.3}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|τὸ τίμημα τὸ -γεγραμμένον τοῖς χρήμασιν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 17.7}}.
**III.3**. generally, **register** or **enter in a public list**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|ἐπιγράψαι σφᾶς αὐτοὺς ἐπιτρόπους 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.36}}; ἐ. τινὰ εἰς τοὺς πράκτορας **register** his **name** among the πράκτορες, Decrees cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.77 (_passive_):}} —_middle_, ἐπεγράψαντο πολίτας **enrolled fresh** citizens, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.4; ξένην καὶ ξένον γονέας -ψάμενος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.51}}; πῶς οἷόν τε τῷ ἀνδρὶ δύο πατέρας -ψασθα; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 4.4 (later in _active_, ἑαυτῷ τινὰ πατέρα -γράφων _claiming_ as his father, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “BC” 1.32).}}
**III.4**. _middle_, ἐπιγράφεσθαι μάρτυρας **cause to be endorsed on a deposition** as witnesses, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.31; κλητῆρα οὐδ᾽ ὁντινοῦν ἐπιγραψάμενος [prev\. author] 21.87}}; but ἐπιγράφεσθαι τίμημα τῷ κλήρῳ **set one's** valuation on the property, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 3.2.}}
**III.5**. προστάτην ἐπιγράψασθαι **choose** a patron, **and enter his name as such** in the public register (as μέτοικοι at Athens were obliged to do), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 684; so probably ἐπεγράφοντο should be restored for -γραφον in 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Peregr.” 11; ἐπιγράψασθαί τινα κύριον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 43.15}}; οἱ τὸν Πλάτωνα ἐπιγραφόμενοι, i.e. the Platonists, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 14:}} —_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|κύριος ἐπιγεγράφθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 43.15, compare “POxy.” 251.32 (1st c.AD), +others}}
**III.5.b**. _metaphorically_, Ὅμηρον ἐπιγράφεσθαι **attribute** one's fluency to Homer, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Dem.Enc.” 2; πρεσβυτέρους ἐ. χρόνους}} **claim the authority of** greater antiquity, {{Refs|[prev\. author] “Am.” 35.}}
**IV**. ἐπιγράψαι ἐαυτὸν ἐπί τι **claim credit for**, [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.167; ἀλλοτρίοις ἐαυτὸν πόνοις Aelianus [{2-3rd c.AD}] “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 8.2, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pomp.” 31; αὐτὸς ἐ. τὴν νίκην}}] **claim** as his own, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “AJ” 7.7.5:}} —so _middle_ and _passive_, τοιούτων ῥητόρων ἐπὶ τὰς τοῦ δήμου γνώμας ἐπιγραφομένων **inscribing their names** on.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.188; ἐπιγράφεσθαι ἀλλοτρίαις γνώμαις 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.43}}; τὸν [{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|ἐπὶ τοῖς τῆς πόλεως ἀτυχήμασιν ἐπιγεγραμμένον Dinarchus Orator [{4-3rd c.BC}] 1.29}}]; οἱ ἐπιγεγραμμένοι ἢ φυλάττοντες the parties **whose names were endorsed** upon the συνθῆκαι as securities, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rh.” 1376b4; οἱ ἐπιγραφόμενοι τοῖς δόγμασι 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.84}}; ἡμεῖς δ᾽ ἐσμὲν ἐπιγεγραμμένοι we are merely **the** **endorsers**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 482.8.}}
**V**. **ascribe to**, [{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|τοῖς θεοῖς τὸ ἔργον 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 8.9 (but θεὸν τῇ πομπῇ Philostratus Sophista [{2-3rd c.AD}] “Vita Apollonii” 8.12):}}] —_middle_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Φοίβῳ τὰς ἀνίσους χεῖρας “AP” 9.263 (1st c.AD: Antiphilus Epigrammaticus).}}
**V.2**. **claim credit for**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|τὰ ὑπὸ ἄλλων εὑρημένα 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 3.4.2}}; **assume**, προσωνυμίαν Plu {{Refs|“Demetr.” 42; ἐπεγράψατο τὴν ἑαυτοῦ προσηγορίαν [prev\. author] “Tim.” 36}}:—_passive_, of books, **to be ascribed**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|τινί 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.25}}.
**V.3**. **predicate of**, [{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|φυγὴν οὐ φυγόντι Philostratus Sophista [{2-3rd c.AD}] “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.1.12}}].

1926:
** ἐπιδέχομαι**,
_Ionic dialect_ etc. ἐπιδέκομα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus (see. below), “Leg.Gort.” 11.25}}:— **admit besides** or **in addition**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.75.}}
**2**. **receive besides**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 583.}}
**3**. **receive, welcome**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|τινὰς φιλανθρώπως 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.18.3}}; ἐ. εἰς τὰ {{Refs 1st c.AD+|οἰκητήρια “POxy.” 281.9 (1st c.AD).}}
**II**. **take on oneself, undertake**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|πόλεμον 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.31.1; τὴν στεφανηφορίαν “Inscription Prien.” 108.255 (2nd c.BC), compare “POxy.” 498.6 (2nd c.AD)}}, etc.: with _infinitive_, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|[prev. work] 102.7 (4th c.AD): _absolutely_}}, **agree**, **admit liability,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PAmh. 2.31.12 (2nd c.BC).}}
**II.2**. of things, **allow of, admit** **of**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|κατηγορίαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.28; λόγον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 3b2}}; τὸ μᾶλλον καὶ τὸ ἧττον {{Refs|[prev. work] 6a19; ἐναντιότητα [prev. work] 11b1; τὴν μεσότητα [prev\. author] “EN” 1107a8}}; τἀκριβές {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1094b25; δόξαν αἰτίας 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.48, compare 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Aut.” 1.7}}: with _infinitive_, [{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|ὁ χρόνος οὐκ ἐπιδέχεται μακρολογεῖν Dinarchus Orator [{4-3rd c.BC}] 1.31}}].
**II.3**. **expect**, **await**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|βαρεῖαν ἐπιδέγμενοι ἀνάγκαν 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 16.96}}.
**II.4**. **accept** a term **as** **applying to**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|ἐπί τινος 1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 11.2}} (variant{ἐκδ-}).

1927:
** ἐπιδημ-έω**,
**to be at home, live at home**, opposed to ἀποδημέω, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.136, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Tht.” 173e, etc.; παρόντες καὶ ἐπιδημοῦντες 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.46; ἐ. τρία ἔη Andocides Orator [{5-6th c.BC}] 1.132}}]; ἐ. Ἀθήνησι **stay at** **home** at Athens, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.16; so ἐν αὐτῇ (i.e. τῇ πόλει) ἐ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cri.” 52b; opposed to στρατεύομαι, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 9.3.}}
**2**. of diseases, **to be prevalent, epidemic**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Prog..” 25.}}
**II**. **come home**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Mem.” 2.8.1; ἐνθάδε ἐ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Smp.” 172c; εἰ νῦν ἐξ ἀγροῦ ἐνθάδ᾽ ἐπιδημεῖ Menander Comicus [{4-3rd c.BC}] “Γεωργός” 19}}].
**III**. of foreigners, **come to stay in a city, reside in** a place, οἱ -οῦντες ἐν [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|Λακεδαίμονι ξένοι Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Memorabilia” 1.2.61}}]; ἐ. εἰς Μέγαρα **come** to Megara **to** **stay** there, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.37, compare 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.154; ἐκ Κλαζομενῶν Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Parmenides” 126b}}]; ἐ. τοῖς μυστηρίοις **to be present at, attend** them, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.176; τοὺς ἐπιδημήσαντας ἅπαντας τῶν Ἑλλήνων}} all **who were present**[at the festival], {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 217; Φοίβου ἐπιδημήσαντος 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Apollinem” 13}}: laterc. _dative_,[{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|ταῖς Σάρδεσιν Philostratus Sophista [{2-3rd c.AD}] “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.22.4; also ἐν Μέμφει “UPZ” 42i4 (2nd c.BC).}}]
**III.2**. _absolutely_,**stay in a** **place, be in town**, [{{NT+6th c.BC+|ὅσοι ξένοι ἐπιδημοῦσιν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.35, compare “Inscription Prien.” 108.286 (2nd c.BC), NT.Acts.17.21; Πρωταγόρας ἐπιδεδήμηκε; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Prt.” 309d: _metaphorically_, ἐ. θίασος Μουσῶν Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Thesmophoriazusae” 40}}].
**III.3**. ἐ. τινί **visit** a person, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PLond.” 2.416.5 (4th c.AD).}}

1929:
** ἐπιδίδωμι**,
**give besides**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|τινί τι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.559, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121.δ᾽, +others, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 186 (_anapaest meter_), “Ba.” 1128, etc.: _absolutely_, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Op.” 396, etc.}}
**b**. of a physician, **administer**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|ὅσων τῷ κάμνοντι δεῖ 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.253}}; **give afterwards**, τροφὴν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|προδόντες ἐπιδιδόασι τὸν ἐλλέβορον 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.148}}.
**2**. **give in dowry**, ὅσσ᾽ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|οὔ πώ τις ἑῇ ἐπέδωκε θυγατρί 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.148, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 16.10, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lg.” 944a (_passive_), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cyr.” 8.5.19.}}
**2.b**. especially **contribute as a `benevolence**', for the purpose of supplying state necessities, opposed to εἰσφέρειν (which was compulsory), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 5.37; ἐκ τῶν ἰδίων ἐ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.80; τριήρη ἐπέδωκεν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.160; ἐπέδωκα τὰ χρήματα [prev\. author] 18.113; τὸ κοινὸν ἐπέδωκε τῷ θεῷ “SIG” 489.9}} (Delph., 3rd c.BC); but also,
**2.c**. **offer money** as a bribe or consideration, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Ath.” 3.3.}}
**3**. **give freely, bestow**, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.11, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 333; ὑμῖν τῶν ἑαυτοῦ τι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 30.26; ἐ. τοῦ ἑαυτοῦ μέρους Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.5.1}}]; τὸν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|ἑαυτοῦ [ζῆλον] εἰς τὴν φιλοδοξίαν “Inscription Prien.” 114.12 (1st c.BC).}}
**4**. ἐπιδιδόναι ἑαυτόν **give** oneself **up, devote** oneself, τινί to one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Th.” 213, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Peregr.” 13; εἴς τι “SIG” 495.124}} (Olbia, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC), compare 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 3.4.1; εἰς πᾶν τό σοι χρήσιμον ἐμαυτὸν ἐ. “UPZ” 62.9 (2nd c.BC); also (i.e. ἑαυτὸν) ἐπιδιδόναι εἰς τρυφήν 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 12.525e; εἰς ὑπερηφανίαν 3rd c.BC: Nymphis Historicus 15; ἐ. ἑαυτὸν τῇ πνεούσῃ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 28}}: _absolutely_, ἐπιδόντες ἐφερόμεθα ran **before the wind,** {{NT|NT.Acts.27.15.}}
**5**. **give into** another's **hands, deliver**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|ἐπιστολήν τινι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.47}} (uncertain reading), {{LXX+NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Acts.15.30; χρηματισμόν Vetus Testamentum Graece redditum LXX.2Mac.11.17; γραμματεῖον 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Peregr.” 16}}: _absolutely_, of petitions, frequently in Papyrus, {{Refs|“BGU” 45}}, etc.; of reports or returns, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 255.16 (1st c.AD), etc.:}} —_passive_, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“OGI” 515.37 (Mylasa, 3rd c.AD), 6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Nov.” 53.3.1.}}
**6**. ἐ. ψῆφον τοῖς πολίταις **give** them **power to** vote, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Num.” 7.}}
**7**. **dictate**, opposed to γράφειν, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 18.18.}}
**II**. _middle_, **take as one's witness**, θεοὺς ἐπιδώμεθα `**give each other our** gods', {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.254:—in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.463, 3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus read σὲ γὰρ πρώτην.. ἐπιδωσόμεθ᾽, perhaps in the same sense, though 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Biographus and Scholl. explain it by δώροις τιμήσομεν: compare περιδίδωμι.}}
**III**. in Prose, frequently _intransitive_, **increase, advance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἐς ὕψος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.13; καθ᾽ ἡμέραν ἐς τὸ ἀγριώτερον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.60; ἐς τὸ μισεῖσθαι [prev\. author] 8.83}}; ἐπὶ τὸ μεῖζον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 24; ἐπὶ τὸ βέλτιον 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 1.3, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Prt.” 318a; εἰς ὄγκον πρὸς ἀρετήν}} **increase** in virtue [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|[prev\. author] “Lg.” 913b; πρὸς εὐδαιμονίαν Isocrates Orator [{5-6th c.BC}] 3.32}}]: and _absolutely_, **grow**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthd.” 271b}}; **advance, improve**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.72, 7.8; βελτίων ἔσται καὶ ἐ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Prt.” 318c, compare “Cra.” 41c e, “Tht.” 146b, 150d, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.68, etc.; ἐ. πάμπολυ [ἡ μάχη]}} **waxes** great, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Tht.” 179d.}}
**III.2**. ={ἐνδίδωμι} see, **give in, give way**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἐ. ἐπίδοσιν τοῖσι ἕλκουσι 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Art.” 72, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.5, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.103.}}

1932:
** ἐπιείκεια**, ἡ,
**reasonableness**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|ἔχει τινὰ οὗτος ὁ λόγος ἐπιείκειαν 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 31}}.
**2**. **equity**, opposed to strict law, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Top.” 141a16, etc.; κατ᾽ ἐπιείκειαν, opposed to κατὰ τοὺς ὅρκους, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 18.34; ἄκαμπτον εἰς ἐ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Mi.” 4.}}
**3**. of persons, **reasonableness, fairness**, [{{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.40, 48, 5.86, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lg.” 735a, etc.; ἐ. καὶ πρᾳότης Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus [{1-2nd c.AD}] “Pericles” 39, compare “NT.2Cor.10.1”}}]; also, **goodness, virtuousness**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 16.11, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.207, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “EN” 1175b24: _plural_, joined with χάριτες, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.63, compare 15.149.}}
**II**. personified, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Clemency”, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caes.” 57.}}

1933:
** ἐπιεικ-ής**, ές,
**fitting, meet, suitable**, τύμβον οὐ μάλα πολλὸν.., ἀλλ᾽ ἐπιεικέα τοῖον not very large but **meet** in size, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.246; τείσουσι βοῶν ἐπιεικέ᾽ ἀμοιβήν}} **a fair** recompense for them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.382.—Elsewh. 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus has only the _neuter_ ἐπιεικές, either in the phrase ὡς ἐπιεικές}} as is **meet**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.147, 23.537, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.389}}: or c._infinitive_, ὅν κ᾽ ἐπιεικὲς ἀκουέμεν whom it may be **meet** for you to hear, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.547; ὅπλα.. οἷ᾽ ἐπιεικὲς ἔργ᾽ ἔμεν ἀθανάτων}} such as is **meet** they should be, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|19.21, compare 23.50, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.207.}}
**II**. after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}
**II.1**. of statements, rights, etc.,
**II.1.a**. **reasonable**, **specious**, ἡ δὲ τρίτη τῶν ὁδῶν πολλὸν ἐπιεικεστάτη ἐοῦσα μάλιστα [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|ἔψευσται 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.22; ἐ. πρόφασις 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.9; λόγος Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Timaeus” 67d}}]; ἐπιεικῆ {{Refs|λέγειν [prev\. author] “Ap.” 34d}}; ἐ. ὁδός a **tolerable** road, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Crass.” 22.}}
**II.1.b**. opposed to δίκαιος, **fair, equitable, not according to the letter of the law**, ἐπανόρθωμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|νομίμου δικαίου 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1137b11, compare “Rh.” 1374a26; τῶν δικαίων τὰ ἐπιεικέστερα προτιθεῖσι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.53}}; οὔτε τοὐπ. οὔτε τὴν χάριν οἶδεν, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|μόνην δ᾽ ἔστερξε τὴν ἁπλῶς δίκην 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “fragment” 770, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “fragment” 645; συγχωρεῖν τἀπιεικῆ τινι Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Nubes” 1438; ἐπιεικέστερον ἢ δικαιότερον 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.13; ἐ. ὁμολογία 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.4; γνώμη Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Vespae” 1027; τὸ ἐ. καὶ σύγγνωμον Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Leges” 757e}}]; πρὸς τὸ ἐ., ={ἐπιεικῶς} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.19.}}
**II.2**. of persons, **able, capable**, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|παῖς τὰ μὲν ἄλλα ἐ., ἄφωνος δέ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.85; οἱ ἐπιεικέστατοι τῶν τριηράρχων Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.1.30}}]; τίνες.. τῶν νέων ἐπίδοξοι γενέσθαι ἐ. may be expected to turn out **well**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Tht.” 143d, compare “Lg.” 957a; τοὺς ἐ. καὶ τοῦ δήμου καὶ τῶν εὐπόρων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 26.1}}.
**II.2.b**. in moral sense, **reasonable**, **fair, good**, ἐ. τὴν ψυχήν, φύσει, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Smp.” 210b, “R.” 538c: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.93, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.48, NT.Jam.3.17, etc.; ἐ. ἄνδρες, opposed to μοχθηροί, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Po.” 1452b34; ἐ. περὶ τὰ συμβόλαια 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.30}}; τοὐπιεικές **fairness, goodness**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “OC” 1127.}}
**II.2.c**. with social or political connotation, the **upper** or **educated classes**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|λέγω ἀντικεῖσθαι τοὺς ἐ. τῷ πλήθει 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1308b27, compare “Ath.” 28.1.}}
**III**. _adverb_ -κῶς, _Ionic dialect_ -κέως, **fairly, tolerably**, **moderately**, ἐγγλύσσει ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.92; ἐ. δάκνειν, παρρησίαν ἄγειν, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Lib.” pp.13,45 O.; ἐ. ἔχειν}} to be **pretty well**, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Coac.” 368; ἐ. ἐξεπίστασθαι Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Vespae” 1249; ἔστι τὸ χωρίον ἐ. ἰσχυρόν “Hellenica Oxyrhynchia” 13.5; ἐ. ἀναίσθητον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione et Corruptione” 319b20; ἐ. πλατύ [prev\. author] “HA” 495b27, compare 497a23; οἱ πυρετοὶ ἐς τεταρταῖον ἐ. μεθίστανται}}] **about** the fourth day, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Coac.” 140, compare 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 281; ἐ. τὸ τρίτον μέρος}} **pretty nearly, about**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.26.8; ἐ. οἷοί τε ἦσαν κατέχειν}} were **fairly well** able.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phd.” 117c; ἐ. μὲν..}} **perhaps**, {{Refs|[prev\. author] “Grg.” 493c.}}
**III.2**. **probably, reasonably**, {{Refs|[prev\. author] “R.” 431e, etc.: _superlative_ -έστατα, γενέσθαι}} **most suitably**, {{Refs|[prev\. author] “Lg.” 753b.}}
**III.3**. **with moderation**, **mildly, kindly**, [{{Refs 1st c.AD+|οὐκ ἐ. ἐντυγχάνων οὐδὲ πρᾴως Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus [{1-2nd c.AD}] “Pyrrhus” 23}}]; ἐ. [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|ἔχειν πρός τινα Isocrates Orator [{5-6th c.BC}] 15.4}}]: _comparative_ {{Refs|-έστερον, διακείμενοι [prev\. author] 8.61}}.
**III.4**. **generally, usually**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pel.” 18, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Mis.” 348c, 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Or.” 11.19, +others}}

1934:
** ἐπιζητ-έω**,
**seek after, wish for, miss**, τινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.36, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sulla” 19; οὐδὲν ἄλλο χρῆμα οὕτω ἐν βραχέϊ ἐπεζήτησα ὡς.. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.24}}; μηδὲν ἐπιζητείτω **let** her **lack** nothing, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PTeb.” 416.20 (3rd c.AD)}}; ἐ. τὸν ἄν θρωπον**make further search for.. **, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.133}}; τῆς αἰτίας αἰτίαν ἐπι ζητούσης**requiring**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.5.3, compare 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.18: _absolutely_, οἱ ἐπιζητοῦντες}} **the** **beaters** (for game), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.25:}} —_passive_, τὰ ἐπιζητούμενα περὶ τὴν εὐδαιμονίαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1098b22, compare 1172b35, 2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 23}}; οἱ -ούμενοι criminals `**wanted',** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|POxy. 80.15 (3rd c.AD).}}
**b**. **request**, πρός τινας ὁμολογίαν {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“Anatolian Studies” 38, compare “PMasp.” 156.16 (6th c.AD).}}
**2**. **seek for besides**, μηδ᾽ ἕτερ᾽ ἐπιζήτει καλά {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 44.5}}; **inquire further**, περὶ.. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.2, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.490.}}
**3**. _passive_, ἐπιζητεῖται **is matter** **of question**, ἐ. πότερον.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1169b13, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 1.22, “Rh.” 1.194S., +others}}
**4**. **demand, require,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PLille 7.6 (3rd c.BC)}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1194.2 (3rd c.AD).}}

1935:
** ἐπιθᾰνάτ-ιος** [νᾰ], ον,
**condemned to death**, {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 7.35, LXX “Bel” (ό) 31, NT.1Cor.4.9}}; ἐ. μέλος, of {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Arion Lyricus, 12th c.AD: Tzetzes, Joannes Grammaticus “Historiarum variarum chiliades” 1.400.}} _adverb_ -ίως, ἔχειν, = {ἐπιθανάτως ἔχειν}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 13.27.}}
**II**. αἱ ἐ. δᾷδες the **funeral** torches, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Declamationes” 40.15}}; but ἐ. ἐπιστολή **deadly**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 2.28.}}

1936:
** ἐπίθεσις**, εως, ἡ,
**setting on its base**, τοῦ ἀνδριάντος {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 3124 (from Teos).}}
**2**. **laying** or **putting on**, opposed to ἀφαίρεσις, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Juventute” 470a11; τῶν χειρῶν NT.Act.8.18, etc.}}; **application**, περιχρίστων {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.102a, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 13.1 (probably).}}
**3**. **application of epithets**, τὰς ἐ. ποιεῖσθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1405b22.}}
**4**. **imposition of increased burdens**, {{Refs|“Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 7.134.}}
**II**. (from _middle_) **setting upon, attack**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.13; ἐ. γίγνεταί τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.4.22; ἡ Περσῶν ἐ. τοῖς Ἕλλησι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 698b}}; τῶν ἐ. αἱ μὲν ἐπὶ τὸ σῶμα γίγνονται τῶν ἀρχόντων **attempts**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1311a31; ἐ. συστῆσαι ἐπί τινα [prev. work] 1306b35}}; ποιεῖσθαι{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 1312a20; λῃστῶν “PPetr.” 3p.60 (3rd c.BC, probably)}}; κατά τινος {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.7; ἡ διὰ τοῦ πυρὸς ἐ. τοῖς ἔργοις 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.45.2}}; of disease, **aggravated** **attack**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.49 (_plural_).}}
**II.2**. with _genitive_, **attempt to gain**, τῆς τυραννίδος {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.92, etc.}}
**II.3**. **small urn placed on a** σορός, {{Refs|“IGRom.” 4.1284 (from Thyatira).}}
**II.4**. **imposture, deception**, {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aquila LXX.Pro.11.1, +others}}
**II.5**. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus}} name for **two**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

1938:
** ἐπιθῡμ-ητής**, οῖ, ὁ,
**one who longs for** or **desires**, νεωτέρων ἔργων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.6; [δογμάτων] And. 4.6; ἔργων 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.90}}; τιμῆς, σοφίας, {{Refs|Pl. “R.” 475b, etc.}}; φύσει πολέμου ἐ. {{NT+2nd c.AD+|Arist. “Pol.” 1253a6; κακῶν NT.1Cor.10.6; ἀλλοτρίων “BGU” 531 ii 22 (2nd c.AD).}}
**2**. _absolutely_, **lover**, **follower**, {{Refs|X. “Mem.” 1.2.60.}}
**2.b**. **one who lusts**, {{LXX|LXX.Num.11.34.}}

1939:
** ἐπιθῡμ-ία**,
_Ionic dialect_ -ιη, ἡ, **desire, yearning**, ἐ. ἐκτελέσαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32; ἐπιθυμίᾳ **by passion**, opposed to προνοίᾳ, {{Refs|Th. 6.13}}: generally, **appetite**, {{Refs|Pl. “Cra.” 419d, etc.}}; αἱ κατὰ τὸ σῶμα ἐ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phd.” 82c}}; especially **sexual desire, lust**, {{Refs|Democr. 234 (_plural_), Pl. “Phdr.” 232b, etc.}}; αἱ πρὸς τοὺς παῖδας ἐ. {{Refs|X. “Lac.” 2.14.}}
**2**. with _genitive_, **longing after** a thing, **desire of** or **for** it, ὕδατος, τοῦ πιεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Th. 2.52, 7.84, etc.; τοῦ πλέονος 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 224; τῆς τιμωρίας 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.7; τῆς μεθ᾽ ὑμῶν πολιτείας 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.10; τῆς παρθενίας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 406b; εἰς ἐ. τινὸς ἐλθεῖν [prev. author] “Criti.” 113d}}; ἐν ἐ. τινὸς εἶναι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Prt.” 318a, “Tht.” 143e; γεγονέναι [prev. author] “Lg.” 841c}}; εἰς ἐ. τινὸς ἀφικέσθαι θεάσασθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ti.” 19b; ἐ. τινὸς ἐμβαλεῖν τινί 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.1.5}}; ἐ. ἐμποιεῖν ἔς τινα **an inclination** towards.., {{Refs|Th. 4.81.}}
**II**. ={ἐπιθύμημα}, **object of desire**, ἐπιθυμίας τυχεῖν Thalesap.{{Refs|Stob. 3.1.172, cf. Lync. cited in Ath. 7.295a}}; ἀνδρὸς ἐ., of woman, {{Refs|Secund. “Sent.” 8; πενήτων ἐ., of sleep, probably in [prev. work] 13.}}

1940:
** ἐπικαθ-ίζω**,
**set upon**, τινὰ ἐπί τι 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Art.” 78: _absolutely_, (i.e. κλίμακι){{Refs|[prev. passage]}}; τινὰ ἐν ἅρματι Vetus Testamentum Graece redditum **4 Ki..**10.16; ἐν ὅπλοις ἀφανῶς ἐ. τὸ στρατιωτικόν 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 18.3.1:—_middle_, φυλακὴν ἐπεκαθίσαντο **had** a guard **set**, {{Refs|Th. 4.130 (ἐπικαθίσταντο Poppo).}}
**II**. Intr., **sit upon**, πειθώ τις ἐπεκάθιζεν ἐπὶ τοῖς χείλεσι 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 94.5; τοῖς καρχησίοις ἐ. **light upon**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Them.” 12, cf. Thphr. “CP” 6.10.5.}}
**II.2**. **sit** **down against, besiege**, πόλει 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.61.6.

1941:
** ἐπικᾰλέω**,
**summon** a god to a sacrifice or as witness to an oath, etc., **invoke**, θεόν {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.39, 3.8, +others; ἐπὶ δὲ κάλεσον Ἄρτεμιν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1280, cf. NT.Acts.7.59, etc.}}; ἐ. θεόν τινι **invoke** a god **over** one, **to be** **gracious to** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.199}}; or, **watch over** his good faith, {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.65}}:— _middle_,{{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.87, +others, X. “HG” 2.3.55, +others; ἐπικαλεσάμενος τὸν θεόν “OGI” 194.18 (Egypt, 1st c.BC).}}
**b**. **pray for**, πρὸ καιροῦ τὸν θάνατον “PLond.” 5.1676.24 {{Refs 6th c.AD+|(6th c.AD).}}
**2**. **invite**, γέροντας ἐπὶ πλέονας καλέσαντες 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.189:—_middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.187, +others}}
**II**. _middle_, **call in** as a helper or ally, ἐπικαλεῖσθαί τινα σύμμαχον {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.64, cf. Th. 1.101}}: c._infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.87; ἐ. τοὺς κεκμηκότας μὴ γενέσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.59; ἐ. ἐκ Θεσσαλίης ἐπικουρίην 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.63.}}
**II.2**. **call in** as witness, μάρτυρας ἐ. τινάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.30, cf. Pl. “Lg.” 664c}}: c._infinitive_, ἐ. θεοὺς.. καθορᾶν τὰ γιγνόμενα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.55: with _neuter_ _adjective_, ταῦτα ἐ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.62.}}
**II.2.b**. **appeal to**, σύνεσιν καὶ παιδείαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.127 (hence, = Latin _appello_, **provoco**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marc.” 2}}; τὸν δῆμον ἀπὸ τῶν δικαστῶν {{Refs|[prev. author] “TG” 16; Καίσαρα “Act.Ap.” 25.11).}}
**II.3**. **call before one, summon**, of the Ephors, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.39.}}
**II.4**. **challenge**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1.}}
**III**. **call by surname**, Δίων ὃν ἐπεκάλουν Χρυσόστομον 4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Vitae Sophistarum” p.454 B.:—more frequently _passive_, **to be called by surname**, ἐπεκλήθησαν Κεκροπίδαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.44; **to be nicknamed**, Ἀριστόδημον τὸν μικρὸν ἐπικαλούμενον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.4.2 {{LXX|compare “HG” 2.3.31; also τὸ ὄνομα ἐπικέκληταί σοι Vetus Testamentum Graece redditum LXX.Deu.28.10}}; ἐπικληθήσεται ἐν αὐτοῖς τὸ ὄνομάμου{{LXX|LXX.Gen.48.16.}}
**IV**. **bring as an accusation against**, τινί τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.139, 4.133, cf. Isoc. 12.9}}; ἐ. τινί, with _infinitive_, **accuse** one of doing, {{Refs|Th. 2.27, cf. Antipho 3.1.1}}; ἐ. τὴν ἀπόστασιν ὅτι.. ἐποιήσαντο 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.36; ἐ. τινὶ πάντα ὅσα ἠδίκητο 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 37.6; ταῦτ᾽ ἐπικαλεῖ; **is** this **your charge**? {{Refs|Ar. “Pax” 663}}; ἐ. ἀρχαιότητα **objecting to** its obsoleteness, {{Refs|Pl. “Lg.” 657b: _absolutely_, ἐπικαλείτω}} **let** him **bring his action**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 45.17 (Halic., 5th c.BC)}}; ὁ ἐπικαλῶν the **plaintiff**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHal.” 1.216 (3rd c.BC):}} —_passive_, τὰ ἐπικαλεύμενα χρήματα the money **imputed** **to him**, i.e. **which he was charged with having**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.118 (but τὰ ἐπικαλούμενα}} the **sums claimed,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PPetr. 2p.108 (3rd c.BC), and so in}}_active_ λείαν ἐ{{Refs|.[prev. work]3p.185)}}; περὶ δανείου “PGrenf.” 2.31.15 {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|(2nd c.BC).}}
**IV.2**. with _dative_ _person_ only, ἐπικαλεῖν τινί **quarrel, dispute with**, ἀλλήλοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 766e.

1942:
** ἐπικάλυμμα** [κᾰ], ατος, τό,
**cover, veil**, πλοῦτος πολλῶν ἐ. ἐστιν κακῶν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 90.
**II**. in animals, **covering** of any orifice, of the **gills** of fish, {{Refs|Arist. “HA” 505a1, “PA” 696b3}}; of the **opercula** of crabs and other crustacea, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HA” 527b26, 541b26, compare 530a21.}}

1943:
** ἐπικᾰλύπτ-ω**,
**cover over, cover up, shroud**, κακὸν δ᾽ ἐπὶ κῶμα καλύπτει variant in {{Refs|Hes. “Th.” 798}}; of snow **covering** a track, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|X. “Cyn.” 8.1; ἐ. τὴν ἀπορίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 169d; τοὺς ὀφθαλμούς 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.106}}:— _passive_, **to be covered over, veiled**, ἡ ἐπωνυμία ἐπικεκάλυπται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 395b; ἐπικαλύπτεσθαι τὸν νοῦν πάθει ἢ ὕπνῳ **is darkened, obscured**, {{Refs|Arist. de “An.” 429a7.}}
**II**. **put as a covering over**, βλεφάρων φᾶρος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 642 codices (Lyric poetry):}} —_passive_, τῶν βλεφάρων -κεκαλυμμένων when the eyelids **are drawn down**, {{Refs|Arist. “Sens.” 437a25.}}

1945:
** ἐπίκειμαι**,
serving as _passive_ to ἐπιτίθημι, **to be laid upon**, and so,
**I**. of doors, **to be put to** or **closed** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|compare (ἐπιτίθημι 11), θύραι δ᾽ ἐπέκειντο φαειναί 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.19: _metaphorically_, γλώσσῃ θύραι οὐκ ἐπίκεινται 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 421.}}
**I.2**. generally, **to be placed, lie in** or **on**, with _dative_, ἐπισκύνιον ἐπέκειτο προσώπῳ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 24.118; of troops, ὄχθαις Ἴστρου ἐ. Hdn. 2.9.1.}}
**I.3**. of islands, νῆσοι ἐπὶ Λήμνῳ (-ου codices) ἐπικείμεναι **lying off** Lemnos, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.6; so ἐ. τῇ Θρηΐκῃ [prev. work] 185}}; ἐπὶ [τῇ Λακαίνῃ χώρῃ{{Refs|] [prev. work] 235, cf. Th. 4.53: _absolutely_, αἱ νῆσοι αἱ ἐπικείμεναι}} the islands **off the coast**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.14, compare 4.44}}; πάσῃ ἐ. τῇ θαλάσσῃ **lies** right **across** the sea, of Crete, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|Arist. “Pol.” 1271b34; ἡ ἐπικειμένη τινὸς γῆ “PTeb.” 50.6 (2nd c.BC).}}
