**2**. **leave untouched**, ὡς οὔτ᾽ ἂν τῶν ἐμῶν ἐπιλίποιμι οὐδὲν οὔτε τῶν φίλων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310e: c._participle_, μυρία ἐ. λέγων {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phlb.” 26b, compare 52d.}}
**II**. of things, **fail** one, c._accusative_ _person_, ἥβην.., ἥ μ᾽ ἐπιλείπει {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1131; ὕδωρ [μιν] ἐπέλιπε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.21, compare 2.174}}; so τῶν ὄμβρων ἐπιλιπόντων αὐτούς (i.e. τοὺς ποταμούς) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.25; γλαῦκες ὑμᾶς οὔποτ᾽ ἐπιλείψουσι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1106}}; ἐπειδὰν αὐτοὺς ἐπιλίπωσιν ἐλπίδες 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.103: c._infinitive_, [ὁ νόμος] ἐμοὶ μόνῳ ἐπέλιπε μὴ ὠφελῆσαι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.17; ἐπιλίποι ἂν ἡμᾶς ὁ χρόνος time would **fail** me, {{NT|Isoc. 1.11, cf. Lys. 12.1, NT.Heb.11.32}}; τὸ ὕδωρ ἡμᾶς ἐ. {{Refs|Isoc. 15.320}}; ἐπιλείψει με λέγονθ᾽ ἡ ἡμέρα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.296: later, c._dative_{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cic.” 42, Ael. “NA” 8.17.}}
**II.2**. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, frequently of rivers, ἐ. τὸ ῥέεθρον}} **leave** their stream **unfilled**, **run dry**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.43, 58, +others}}; without ῥέεθρον, **fail, run dry**,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 127; τὰς κρήνας καὶ τὰ φρέατ᾽ ἐπιλείπειν πέφυκεν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 14.30.}}
**II.3**. generally, **fail, be wanting**, ἵνα μὴ ἐπιλίπῃ κατεσθιόμενα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.108; σῖτος ἐπιλιπών **a deficiency** of it, {{Refs|Th. 3.20 codices}}; τὰ ἐπιτήδεια ἐ. {{Refs|X. “An.” 4.7.1}}; ὥστε τὸν λόγον μηδέποτε ἐ. {{Refs|Pl. “Prt.” 334e}}; opposed to περιγίγνεσθαι, {{Refs|Ar. “Pl.” 554}}: c._genitive_, **fall short**, σπουδῆς οὐθὲν ἐ. {{Refs|“Michel” 332.9 (from Odessus).}}

1954:
** ἐπίλοιπος**, ον,
**still left, remaining**, μῆνας ἑπτὰ τοὺς ἐπιλοίπους Καμβύσῃ ἐς τὰ ὀκτὼ ἔτεα τῆς πληρώσιος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.67: frequently in _plural_, with _genitive_, αἱ ἐ. τῶν πολίων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.33; τὰ ἐ. τοῦ λόγου [prev. author] 4.154; τἀπ. τῶν λόγων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 24, etc.; τἀπίλοιπ᾽ ἄκουσον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 923, cf. Pl. “Cra.” 397a}}; ἡ 'πίλοιπος ὁδός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 842; τί οὖν ἦν ἐπίλοιπο; And. 1.87.}}
**2**. of Time, **future**, χρόνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.13, Pl. “Lg.” 628a, etc.}}; ἁμέραι ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Pi. “O.” 1.33; βίος 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator “fragment” 67, Lys. 2.71, Pl. “Lg.” 929e.}}

1955:
** ἐπί-λῠσις**, εως, ἡ,
**release from**, ἐ. φόβων δίδου 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 134 (Lyric poetry): _absolutely_, **exemption** from banishment, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 306.51 (Arc., 4th c.BC).}}
**2**. **solution**, σοφισμάτων 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 2.246; **explanation**, {{NT|NT.2Pet.1.20, Vett. Val. 172.3 (_plural_), Hld. 1.18, 4.9, Iamb. “Protr.” 21 (_plural_).}}
**3**. **discharge**, of a debt, δοῦναί τισιν ἐ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PEleph.” 27.23 (3rd c.BC),compare “PGrenf.” 2.26.27 (2nd c.BC).}}
**4**. **spell,** {{Refs|PMag.Leid.W. 25.11, +others}}
**5**. in Medicine texts, **change of** **dressing**, {{Refs|Sor. 1.28 (_plural_), Gal. 18(2).838(_plural_), Paul.Aeg. 4.48.}}

1956:
** ἐπιλύω**,
**loose, untie**, δεσμά 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus “Adon.” 42; ἐ.κύνας **let slip** dogs, {{Refs|X. “Cyn.” 7.8}}: generally, **set free, release**, τοὺς κακούργους τῷ πολέμῳ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Par.” 50:—_middle_, ἐπιλύεσθαί τινα τὸ μὴ οὐχὶ ἀγανακτεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 43c; ἐπιλύεσθαι ἐπιστολάς **open**them, {{Refs|Hdn. 4.12.8}}; Ἐπιλυσαμένη, epithet of various divinities, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**2**. **solve, explain**, πάντα τοῖς μαθηταῖς NT.Mark.4.34:—_passive_,{{Refs|S.E. “P.” 2.246,Scholia Od. 9.106}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 8.6.5, Vett.Val. 259.4, Ath. 10.45o f, +others:}}_perfect_ _participle_ _passive_ ἐπιλελυμένος **lucid**, of writings, {{Refs|Vett.Val. 329.25.}}
**3**. **confute** an accusation, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Bis Acc.” 30.}}
**4**. _middle_, **manumit,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|Delph. 3(2).233 (2nd c.BC).}}
**5**. **release, discharge** a debtor, ἀπὸ τᾶν κοινᾶν ποθόδων ἐπιλυθῆμεν (_infinitive_ _passive_) τοὺς ἐρρυτιασμένους “Schwyzer” 104.7 {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|(Troezen, 2nd c.BC)}}: **pay**, with _dative_ _person_, {{Refs|[prev. work]12}}:—_middle_, **discharge** a debt, δάνειον “PGrenf.” 1.26.2 {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|(2nd c.BC).}}
**II**. _future_ _middle_ in _passive_ sense, **lose strength**, **give in**, {{Refs|Lys. 25.33 (uncertain reading).}}

1957:
** ἐπιμαρτῠρ-έω**,
**bear witness to** a thing, **depose to**, ἐ. ἡμῖν τὰ ὀνόματα μὴ.. κεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 397a; ἐ. τι πρός τινας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lysander” 22; τὰ χρήματα ἅ κα ἐπιμαρτυρήσωντι of which they **admit the possession,** {{Refs|Tab.Heracl. 1.156}}: with _infinitive_, τῶν πραγμάτων -ούντων τὴν δύναμιν αὐξάνεσθαι {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sertorius” 12, cf. NT.1Pet.5.12; ὅτι.. 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 42: _absolutely_, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Nic.” 6}}:— _passive_, **to be confirmed by evidence**, {{Refs|S.E. “M.” 7.211, Polystr. p.31 W.}}
**2**. **bear witness in favour of**, τινί 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ οἰκονομίας” p.57J.
**II**. Astrology texts, **support** **by aspect**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Vett.Val. 111.31, “PMag.Leid.W.” 24.16 (2nd-3rd c.AD).}}
**III**. in _middle_, **adjure**, τισὶ μὴ ποιέειν τι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.93 (as variant for{ἐπιμαρτύρομαι}).

1958:
** ἐπιμέλ-εια**, ἡ,
written **-εα** “IG” 22.483.24 {{Refs 4th c.BC+|(4th c.BC), _Aeolic dialect_ _genitive_ -ηΐας [prev. work]12(2).243 (from Mytilene)}}; _Ionic dialectgenitive_ -λίης Ps.-{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus “Vit.Hom.” 5(assuming variant)}}: —**care bestowed upon** a thing, **attention paid** to it, and _absolutely_, **attention**, **diligence**, Prose word, once in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus (see. below), frequently in Th., X., etc.}}: in _plural_, **pains**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|X. “Cyr.” 1.6.4, etc.: with _genitive_ objecti, ἐ. τοῦ ναυτικοῦ, οἰκείων καὶ πολιτικῶν, Th. 2.39, 40; τῶν ἔργων [prev. author] 3.46; τῶν πραγμάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.13; τῶν κοινῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.25; τῶν καμνόντων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 720d}} (hence, of medical _treatment_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|S.E. “P.” 2.240); πλήθους γεννημάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 740d; also περί τινος τὴν ἐ. ποιεῖσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.56; περὶ τοὺς νέους 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 106}}; πρὸς τοὺς θεούς, πρὸς τὴν πόλιν, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|D. 22.78, Pl. “Lg.” 754b; εἰς τὰ ἀναγκαῖα 2nd-1st c.BC: Posidonius Historicus 8J.}}; ἐπιμέλειάν τινος ποιεῖσθαι, ἔχειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.105, Th. 6.41, Arist. “Pol.” 1330b11, D. 61.43, cf. Pl. “R.” 451d}}; opposed to ἐπιμελείας τυγχάνειν to have **attention paid** to one, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|Isoc. 6.154, compare “POxy.” 58.22 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}; ἐ. παρὰ τοῦ δαιμονίου 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Epitaphius” 43; δι᾽ ἐπιμελείας ἔχειν τινά 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.14; ἐπιμελείᾳ, κατ᾽ ἐπιμέλειαν, **with diligence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|X. “Cyr.” 5.3.47, “HG” 4.4.8; δι᾽ ἐπιμελείας θεοῦ ἡ ἀτυχία γίγνεται 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.8}}; μετὰ πάσης ἐ. {{Refs|X.Eph. 2.10.}}
**2**. **a commission** or **charge**, {{Refs|Aeschin. 3.13, Arist. “Pol.” 1299a20 (_plural_); ἡ περὶ τοὺς θεοὺς ἐ. [prev. work] 1322b18, compare [prev. work] 30 (_plural_)}}; ἡ τῶν ἐφήβων ἐ., a special office at Athens, {{Refs|Din. 3.15}}; so πρὸς τῇ ἐ. τῶν χρηματιστῶν, ={ἐπιμελητὴς τῶν χρ}., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 281.2 (1st c.AD).}}
**3**. **any** **employment** or **pursuit**, interpolated in {{Refs|X. “Cyr.” 1.6.13, etc.: _plural_, ἐ. καὶ σπουδαί}} **pursuits** which demand zeal, {{Refs|Arist. “Rh.” 1370a11, compare “EN” 1138b26.}}

1959:
** ἐπιμελ-έομαι**,
also **ἐπιμελ-μέλομαι** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.98, 2.2, 174, +others, Th. 6.54 (variant in 7.39), Lys. 7.25 (codex M), Pl. “Grg.” 516b, “PEleph.” 13.7 (3rd c.BC), “PCair.” “Zen.” 44.17 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; the _contraction_ form prevails in codices, e.g.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|Ar. “Pl.” 1117, X. “An.” 5.7.10, Pl. “Mx.” 248e, and in _Attic dialect_ Inscrr. from 4th c.BC (older _Attic dialect_ spellings are ambiguous)}}, and is required by the metre in {{Refs|E. “Ph.” 556}}: _future_ ἐπιμελήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.29, Th. 3.25, etc.}}; -μεληθήσομαι variant in {{Refs|X. “Mem.” 2.7.8, Aeschin. 3.27}} (_Doric dialect_ 3rd._person_ etc for 1st._person_, 2nd._person_ _singular_ **-θησεῖ** {{Refs|“IG” 12(3).170.25 (from Astypalaea), _plural_ -θησεῦντι [prev. work]12(1).694 (from Rhodes))}}: _aorist_ ἐπεμελήθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.109, Th. 8.68, Isoc. 4.38, X. “Mem.” 1.3.11, etc. (ἐπεμελησάμην only late, “IGRom.” 4.684.14 (1st c.AD), “SIG” 875 (2nd c.AD), Gal. “Protr.” 9)}}: _perfect_ ἐπιμεμέλημαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.41: _active_ only in {{Refs|“SIG” 1044.31 (Halic.)}}:—**take** **care of, have charge** or **management of**, rare in Poets, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|E. “Ph.” 556, frequently in Prose: with _genitive_ objecti, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.98, 5.29, Ar. “V.” 154, “Pl.” 1117, Th. 3.25, Isoc. 4.38, Pl. “R.” 331d, etc.; περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.7.10}}; [ὑπὲρ] τῆς στρατηγίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.6.12; περί τινα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 248e: later, with _dative_, παιδίῳ “POxy.” 744.6 (1st c.BC),compare “PTeb.” 58.62 (2nd c.BC)}}: c._accusative_ et _infinitive_, **take** **care that.. **, {{Refs|Th. 6.54}}: c._genitive_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs|X. “Oec.” 20.9:followed by ὅπως}} with _indicative_ _future_ or _subjunctive_ _aorist_, Foed. cited in {{Refs|Th. 4.118, X. “Mem.” 2.10.2, etc.}}: with _optative_ after an _aorist_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HG” 6.5.37}}; by ὅπως c._infinitive_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 4.2.37 (assuming variant)}}; by ὡς with _optative_ (after past tenses), {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 1.1.5, etc.}}; also ἐ. τινὸς ὅπως ἔσται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 2d; by ἵνα c._subjunctive_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Inscription Prien.” 44.35 (2nd c.BC)}}: with _neuter_ _adjective_ in _accusative_, **take care with respect to** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Th. 6.41: c._accusative_ et _dative_, τὰ ἄλλα τοῖς πολεμάρχοις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.4, compare “IG” 22.233.20 (in E. “Ph.” 556 the _accusative_ belongs to ἔχοντες)}}: with _accusative_ _cognate_, ἐ. πᾶσαν ἐπιμέλειαν {{Refs|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 325c, compare “IG” 22.1261.5}}: _absolutely_, **give heed, attend**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.2.}}
**2**. of public commissions, **have charge of, be inspector** or **curator of**, τῶν μοριῶν ἐλαῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.29; τῶν δεκαδέων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.14; δρόμου [prev. author] “An.” 4.8.25}}; ὁδῶν (of the Roman _cura viarum)_ {{Refs|“CIG” 4011 (from Ancyra): c._accusative_ _cognate_, Pl. “Lg.” 812e:}} —in _Attic dialect_ Inscrr., ἐπιμεληθῆναι (_infinitive_ = _imperative_) is usual of a definite commission, ποιήσεως “IG” 22.555, etc. (so -ηθέντων{{Refs|[prev. work]12.70); ἐπιμελεῖσθαι, of matters requiring permanent attention, [prev. work] 12.56, etc.}}
**3**. **to be engaged in, cultivate** any pursuit, art, etc., δυοῖν τέχναιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.31; τῆς ἀρετῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.71, compare “Mem.” 4.5.10.}}

1960:
Included with: ** ἐπιμελ-ής**, ές,
**careful** or **anxious about**, ἀγαθῶν {{Refs|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 197d; τῶν φιλῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.6.35, etc.; also ἐ. περὶ τὰ αὑτῶν ἔργα [prev. work] 3.4.9.}}
**2**. _absolutely_, **careful, attentive**, {{Refs|Ar. “Nu.” 501, X. “Mem.” 2.6.38, etc.}}: _comparative_ -εστέρα ψυχή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “fragment” 472, cf. X. “An.” 3.2.30}}: _superlative_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Isoc. 4.142; -εστέραν ἔχειν ἑτέρου θεραπείαν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 223.9.}} _adverb_ -λῶς **carefully**, {{Refs|X. “Mem.” 2.4.2, Pl. “Ti.” 88c, Men. “Pk.” 32, etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_ **-λέως** 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Art.” 55; Cretan dialect **-λίως** {{Refs|“SIG” 685.118 (from Itanos)}}: _comparative_ **-έστερον** 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.5.14; **-έστερα** “SIG” 785.9; **-εστέρως** 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 14.629b: _superlative_ **-έστατα** 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Alc.” 1.104d.
**II**. _passive_, **cared for, an object** **of care**, οἱ τοῦτ᾽ ἦν ἐπιμελές 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.40; οἷς ἁγνεῖαι.. ἐπιμελεῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 909e; τὸ ἐ. τοῦ δρωμένου **the charge** of the execution of orders, {{Refs|Th. 5.66: mostly in _neuter_ ἐπιμελές, with _dative_ _person_, Κύρῳ ἐπιμελὲς ἐγένετο τὰ Κροῖσος εἶπε}} made him **anxious**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.89, compare 5.12, 7.37}}; ἐ. μοι ἦν it was my **business**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.150: c.}}_infinitive_, οὐδενὶ ἐ. ἦν σκοπεῖν it was no one's **business** to see, {{Refs|Antipho 2.4.6}}; οἷς ἐ. εἴη εἰδέναι who made it their **business** to know, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Th. 1.5, cf. D. 18.249; ἐ. πεποίημαι εἰδέναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 172c; τοῖς ἄρχουσιν ἐ. ἔστω μή.. [prev. author] “Lg.” 932d: with _dative_ et _genitive_, οἷς τούτων ἐ. [prev. work] 763e, compare 824; δεῖπερὶ ἀρετῆς ἐ. εἶναι τῇ πόλει 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1280b7.}}
**II.2**. **suitable**, τόπος “POxy.” 1412.11 {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|(3rd c.AD).}}

1961:
** ἐπιμένω**,
**stay on, tarry**, _absolutely_, {{Refs|Il. 19.142, Od. 17.277}}; ἐπιμεῖναι ἐς αὔριον 11.351; ἄγε νῦν ἐπίμεινον, Ἀρήϊα τεύχεα δύω do you **wait**, and I will put on my armour, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Il. 6.340; also ἐ. ἐνὶ μεγάροισιν.. ὄφρα.. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.587; ἐ. ἵνα.. “hymnus ad Cererem” 160, Ar. “Nu.” 196; so ἐ. ἐς τε.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.5.2: after Hom.}}, **remain in** a place, ἐ. ἐν τῇ πόλει 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.75, etc.; ἐπί τῇ στρατιᾷ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.2.1.
**2**. _absolutely_, **remain in place, continue as they are**, of things, {{Refs|Th. 4.4, Pl. “Phd.” 80c, X. “Cyn.” 6.4}}; **keep one's seat**, of a horseman, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.4.8}}; **stay behind** in a place, {{Refs|Str. 10.2.24.}}
**3**. **continue** in a pursuit, ἐπὶ τῇ ζητήσει, ἐπὶ λόγῳ, {{Refs|Pl. “La.” 194a, “Tht.” 179e}}; ἐπὶ τοῖς δοξαζομένοις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 490b; ἐπὶ τοῦ κακουργήματος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.86; ἐπὶ τῆς πολιορκίας 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.77.1}}; c._dative_, **persist in**, τῇ ἀπονοία “PTeb.” 424.4 {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|(3rd c.AD)}}; **continue** treatment, ἐ.βοηθήματι Herod._middle_ in {{Refs|“Rh.Mus.” 58.83}}; **cleave** **to**, μιᾷ γυναικί “PSI” 3.158.26 {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|(3rd c.AD): also with}}_participle_, ἐ. ἐπὶ τῶν ἵππων ὀρθὸς ἑστηκώς {{NT|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 93d, cf. NT.John.8.7}}; **spend time over**, ὑποδείγμασι 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “Synt.” 166.14.
**4**. **abide by**, ταῖς σπονδαῖς uncertain reading in {{Refs|X. “HG.” 3.4.6.}}
**5**. **endure**, τοῖς συμβεβηκόσι 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.3.
**II**. with _accusative_, **await, be** **in store for**, τινά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 624 (Lyric poetry), variant in{{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 223 (Lyric poetry), cf. Pl. “R.” 361d}}: with _aorist_ _infinitive_, ἐ. τι τελεσθῆναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.2; μὴ 'πιμεῖναι τοὐμὸν ὀξῦναι στόμα not **to wait** so as to.., {{Refs|S. “Tr.” 1176}}: with _future_ _infinitive_, {{Refs|Th. 3.26.}}

1962:
** ἐπι-νεύω**,
_future_ **-νεύσω** 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sat.” 4, **-νεύσομαι** 5th c.AD: Aristaenetus Rhetor 2.1:**—nod to**, in token of command or approval, **nod** **assent**, opposed to ἀνανεύω, ἐμῷ δ᾽ ἐπένευσα κάρητι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.75; ἐπ᾽ ὀφρύσι νεῦσε Κρονίων 1.528, etc.; ἐπὶ γλεφάροις νεῦσαν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).49; σὺ.. ἐπένευσας τάδε **did'st approve, sanction** these acts, {{Refs|E. “Or.” 284, cf. D. 18.324}}; ἐπένευσεν ἀληθὲς εἶναι he **nodded in sign that** it was true,{{Refs|Aeschin. 3.59}}; σιγῇ δὲ τὰ ψευδῆ.. ἐπινεύουσι they **indicate** falsehoods without speaking, {{Refs|D. 21.139: _absolutely_, Antipho 2.2.7}}; Ἑλληνικὸν ἐ. **give** a Greek **nod**, {{Refs|Ar. “Ach.” 115}}: with _accusative_, **grant** or **promise**, τινά τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 681 (Lyric poetry); τι [prev. author] “Ba.” 1349; ὑπέρ τινος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.5.3: with _dative_, ἐ. τῇ δεήσει τινός “PGiss.” 1.41 ii 9 (2nd c.AD)}}: with _dative_ _person_, ἐ. τισὶ δεομένοις “SIG” 888.13 {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|(Macedonia, 3rd c.AD)}}: with _dative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, **permit**, κῴδια ἐ. ἡμῖν ἐργάζεσθαι “PPetr.” 2p.108 {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|(3rd c.BC).}}
**2**. **make a sign** to another to do a thing, **order** him to do, with _infinitive_, ἐπ᾽ ὀφρύσι νεῦσε σιωπῇ.. στορέσαι λέχος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.620: _absolutely_, Od. 16.164 (tmesis), “Hymnus in Cererem” 169, 466, X. “Cyr.” 5.5.37.}}
**3**. **nod forwards**, κόρυθι ἐπένευε φαεινῇ he **nodded** with his helmet, i.e.it **nodded**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Il. 22.314; λόφων ἐπένευον ἔθειραι 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.186; ἐ. ἐς τὸ κάταντες 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 25.2}}; πέτραι ἐπινενευκυῖαι **overhanging**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Prom.” 1.}}
**4**. **incline towards**, εἴς τινα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 657.
**5**. **roll down** an inclined plane, {{Refs|Hero “Aut.” 2.1.}}
**6**. _transitive_, **elevate, point upwards**, {{LXX|[prev. author] “Bel.78.8”, 89.14}}:—_passive_, **to be inclined downwards**, opposed to ἐξυπτιάζεσθαι, {{Refs|S.E. “P.” 1.120.}}
**6.b**. **tilt**, [κεράμιον] {{Refs|“Gp.” 7.9.}}
**7**. ἐπινενευκὼς σφυγμός, name coined by Archigenes, {{Refs|Gal. 8.479.}}

1963:
** ἐπίνοια**, ἡ,
**thinking on** or **of** a thing, **thought, notion**, οὐδ᾽ ἐς ἐπίνοιαν ἰέναι τινός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.46; ὡς.. [prev. author] 4.92; οὐδ᾽ ἐπίνοιαν ποιήσασθαί τινος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.20.12; τὰς ἐ. εἴς τι φέρειν 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Epistula ad Pompeium” 1}}; πάσαις ταῖς ἐ. γίγνεσθαι περί τι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.110.10; **conception, idea**, ἐναργὴς τοῦ πράγματος ἐ. {{Refs|Epicur. “fragment” 255, cf. Phld. “D.” 3.8, +others}}; κατ᾽ ἐπίνοιαν in **idea**, opposed to κατὰ περίπτωσιν (which see), {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Stoic.” 2.29; κατ᾽ ἐ. ψιλὴν ὑφεστάναι [prev. work]159; πᾶσαν ἐ. ἀτοπίας ὑπερβάλλειν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1065d.}}
**2**. **power of thought, inventiveness**, οἶνον σὺ τολμᾶς εἰς ἐ. λοιδορεῖ; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Ar. “Eq.” 90, cf. X. “Cyr.” 2.3.19; κατὰ τέχνην καὶ ἐ. γίγνεσθαι 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Odoribus” 7.}}
**3**. **invention, device**, **conceit**, ἐ. ἀστειοτάται {{Refs|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 539; ζητεῖν καινὴν ἐ. [prev. author] “V.” 346}}; θαυμαστὰς ἐξευρίσκων ἐ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Eq.” 1322, etc.; τέχνης ἐπίνοιαι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 399b17; πενία ἐπινοιῶν διδάσκαλος 2nd c.AD: Secundus Gnomologus “Sententiae - FPG” 10.}}
**4**. **purpose, design**, τίν᾽ ἐ. ἔσχεθε; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 408, compare “Medea” 760 (Lyric poetry)}}; τίς ἐ.; {{Refs|Ar. “Th.” 766, compare “Av.” 405 (Lyric poetry); ἥτις ἡ 'πίνοια τῆς ἐγκεντρίδος [prev. author] “V.” 1073, compare “Pl.” 45}}; κατὰ τὴν ἐκφορὰν καὶ τὴν ἐ. {{Refs|“Stoic.” 2.128; ἡ ἐ. τῆς καρδίας “Act.Ap.” 8.22: _plural_, ἐξ οἰκείων ἐ}}., = **sua sponte**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“OGI” 580.7 (Cilicia, 4th c.AD).}}
**II**. **afterthought, second thoughts**, ψεύδει γὰρ ἡ 'πίνοια τὴν γνώμην 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 389.
**III**. **intelligence**, κοινὴ ἐ. {{Refs|Plb. 6.5.2, cf. Longin. cited in Eus. “PE” 15.20.}}
**III.2**. Psychol., **reflection on experience, retrospection**, {{Refs|Plot. 2.9.1, 6.8.7.}}

1964:
** ἐπιορκ-έω**,
also **ἐφιορκέω** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 22.1126.9 (Delph. Amphict.), “OGI” 229.69 (Smyrna, 3rd c.BC)}}, etc.: _future_ **-ήσω** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.188, Ar. “Lys.” 914, etc., -ήσομαι (κατ-) D. 54.40}}: _aorist_ ἐπιώρκησα {{Refs|[prev. author] 49.67}}, _infinitive_ **-ορκῆσαι** [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.68 (variant{ἐφ-})}}]: _perfect_ ἐπιώρκηκα {{Refs|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 948e, X. “An.” 3.1.22, Din. 1.47 (ἐπιόρκηκα is variant in Hdt.[same place])}}:—**swear falsely, forswear oneself**, οὐδ᾽ ἐπιορκήσω πρὸς δαίμονος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.188: also, c._accusative_, of things sworn by, τὰς βασιληΐας ἱστίας ἐπιώρκηκε **has sworn falsely by** the royal hearth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.68; θεάς 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.47}}; [θεούς] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Ar. “Av.” 1609, X. “An.” 2.4.7, D. 49.67, etc.: mostly _absolutely_, Ar. “Eq.” 298, 428, “Nu.” 402, Pl. “Phlb.” 65c, etc.; οὐδὲν ἐφρόντιζ᾽ ἐπιορκῶν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.119: c._accusative_ _cognate_, ἐ. ὅρκους τινί [prev. author] 49.65, cf. Aeschin. 1.115}}; opposed to εὐορκέω, Legal cited in {{Refs|And. 1.98, Cleanth.Stoic. 1.131, Chrysipp.[prev. work] 2.63, who distinguishes between εὐορκεῖν and ἀληθορκεῖν, and between ἐπιορκεῖν and ψευδορκεῖν.}}
**II**. simply, ={ὄμνυμι}, **swear**, {{Refs|Sol. cited in Lys. 10.17.}}

1966:
Related to: ** ἔπειμι**,
(εἶμι
**B** **ibo**) _infinitive_ ἐπιέναι, serving in _Attic dialect_ as _future_ of ἐπέρχομαι: _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _imperfect_ ἐπήϊεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.741}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐπήϊσαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.233, ἐπῇσαν 19.445}}; _Attic dialect_ ἐπῇα, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐπῇσαν: ἐπιείσομαι, -εισαμένη (which see) belong to a different word:
**B.I** **come upon** (in _future_ sense, though this is not so fixed in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus as in _Attic dialect_):}}
**B.I.1** of persons, **come upon, approach,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.42, etc.}}
**B.I.1.b** mostly in hostile sense, **come against, attack,** with _dative_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.482, etc.; τῷ λόφῳ ἐ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.129}}; in Prose also with Preps., ἐ. ἐπὶ τὴν Ἑλλάδα, ἐπὶ τοὺς ἀδικοῦντας, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.157, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.86 (variant{πρός}), etc.}}]; πρὸς τὸ τεῖχος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.4: _absolutely_, Αἰνείαν ἐπιόντα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.477, compare 5.238; ἐπάγοντες ἐπῇσαν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.445}}; οἱ ἐπιόντες the **invaders, assailants,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.11, etc.; ὡς ἐπιών}}**by assault,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.21}}; but ὁ ἐπιών in Trag., ={ὁ τυχών}, the **first comer,** τό γ᾽ αἴνιγμ᾽ οὐχὶ τοὐπιόντος ἦν ἀνδρὸς διειπεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 393, compare “OC” 752.}}
**B.I.1.c** **get on the** βῆμα to speak, variant for{παριέναι} in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.72}}; **come on,** of performers, uncertain reading in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.1.11.}}
**B.I.1.d** **approach, attack** a question, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 186a4.}}
**B.I.2** of events, **come upon** or **over** one, **overtake,** with _accusative_, πρίν μιν καὶ γῆρας ἔπεισιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.29 (but ἔπειμι γῆρας ἔς τε τὸν μόρσιμον αἰῶνα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 7(6).41); οἷός σε χειμὼν καὶ κακῶν τρικυμία ἔπεισι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1016}}: with _dative_, **come near,** ὀρυμαγδὸς ἐπήϊεν ἐρχομένοισιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.741}}; δεινῶν ἐπιόντων πᾶσι Ἕλλησι **threatening** them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.145: _absolutely_, χειμὼν ἐπιών 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 675; νὺξ ἐπῄει 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 378, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.3.14, “An.” 5.7.12}}; τὸ ἐπιόν the (madness) which **threatens** me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 238d.}}
**B.I.2.b** with _dative_ _person_, **come into** one's **head, occur** to one, εἰ καὶ ἐπίοι αὐτῷ λέγειν if it so much as **occurred** to him to say.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 388d, compare 558a; ὅ τι ἂν ἀπὸ ταὐτομάτου ἐπίῃ μοι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.4; ἂν.. ὑμῖν ἐπίῃ σκοπεῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.185: _absolutely_, τὸ ἐπιόν}}**what occurs to one,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 264b.}}
**B.II** of Time, **come on** or **after**: mostly in _participle_ ἐπιών, οῦσα, όν, **following, succeeding, instant,** ἡ ἐπιοῦσα ἡμέρα the **coming** day, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.85, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 105, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 44a; ἡ 'πιοῦσα λαμπὰς θεοῦ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 352}}; ἡ ἐπιοῦσα (i.e. ἡμέρα) {{NT+5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.25.11, LXX.Pro.27.1, NT.Act.16.11; τῆς ἐ. νυκτός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 46a; τῇ ἐ. νυκτί NT.Act.23.11; ὁ ἐ. βίοτος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1659; τοῦ ἐ. χρόνου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 769c; ἐν τῷ ἐ. χρόνῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.23; ἡ ἐ. ὥρα τοῦ ἔτους 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.18; εἰς τὴν ἐ. ἐκκλησίαν [prev. author] 21.162, “IG” 22.717.16; εἰς τὴν ἐ. Πυλαίαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.151}}; τοὐπιόν the **future,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1073.6; τῆς ἐ. ἐλπίδος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 870; περὶ τῶν ἐπιόντων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 4.3}}; τῶν ἐ. ἕνεκα because of the **consequences,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 19.258.}}
**B.II.2** generally, **come after, succeed,** κύματα.. βάντ᾽ ἐπιόντα τε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 115 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὁ ἐπιών the **successor,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OC” 1532; αὐτόματα ἔπεισιν ἐκ τῶν ἔμπροσθεν ἐπιτηδευμάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 427a}}; τὰ ἐπιόντα the words **which follow,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Prt.” 344a, compare “Sph.” 257c.}}
**B.II.3** rarely, **pass, elapse,** ἐπιόντος τοῦ χρόνου {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ti.” 44b.}}
**B.III** **go over** a space, **traverse, visit,** ἀγρόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.359; χώρους 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.74}}; of an officer, ἐ. πύλας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1164; τὸ στράτευμα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.78, etc.}}
**B.III.2** **go over,** i.e. **count over,** φώκας.. ἀριθμήσει καὶ ἔπεισιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.411}}; **think over,** τῇ μνήμῃ ἕκαστα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 1}}; **read,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 2.6.}}

1968:
** ἐπιπίπτω**,
**fall upon** or **over**, ἐπέπιπτον ἀλλήλοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.84; ἐπί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 18.7, cf. Thphr. “CP” 5.4.5: _metaphorically_, ἐπέπεσε μοῖρα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Pae.” 2.64; ἐπί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.89; διαλογισμοὶ ἐπιπίπτουσί τινι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Otho” 9.}}
**2**. of money, **accrue**, τὸ μέρος ὃ εὑρίσκομες ἐπιπῖπτον ἐπὶ τὸ χρέος τὸ ὀφειλόμενον “SIG” 953.66 (Cnidus, 2nd c.BC).
**II**. **fall upon** in hostile sense, **attack, assail**, τινί 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.105 {{Refs|Th. 3.112}}; ἀφυλάκτῳ αὐτῷ ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.116; ἀφάρκτῳ τῷ στρατοπέδῳ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.117; ἀπαρασκεύοις τοῖς ἐναντίοις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.4.3}}; also ἐς τοὺς Ἕλληνας, variant for{ἐσ-}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.210}}; of storms, τοῖσι βαρβάροισι ὁ βορῆς ἐπέπεσε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 189; χειμὼν ἐπιπεσών 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 344d}}; of winds **meeting** one another, {{Refs|Arist. “Mete.” 364b3}}; of diseases, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 3; ἡ νόσος ἐ. τοῖς Ἀθηναίοις 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.87}}; so of grief, misfortunes, etc., οὐχὶ σοὶ μόνᾳ ἐπέπεσον λῦπαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1043 (Lyric poetry), etc.; ἐπέπεσε πολλὰ καὶ χαλεπὰ κατὰ στάσιν ταῖς πόλεσι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.82, etc.
**II.2**. **come on after**, ἐ. ῥῖγος πυρετῷ 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 4.46.
**II.3**. **accumulate**, πλήθη σίτου ἐπιπεπτωκέναι “PPetr.” 2p.62 {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|(3rd c.BC).}}

1969:
** ἐπιπλήσσω**,
_Attic dialect_ **ἐπιπλήττω**, **strike**, τόξῳ ἐπιπλήσσων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.500; ὑπέροισιν 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 952.}}
**II**. **punish, chastise**, especially with words, **rebuke.** **reprove**, c._accusative_ _person_, καί μ᾽ οὔ τινά φημι ἄλλον ἐπιπλήξειν {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.580, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 327a: with _dative_, Ἕκτορ, ἀεὶ μέν πώς μοι ἐπιπλήσσεις 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.211, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.31; ἐμαυτῷ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 805b, cf. NT.1Tim.5.1, etc.}}; ἐπί τινι for a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 286b}}:—_passive_, **to be rebuked**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Grg.” 478e.}}
**II.2**. ἐ. τινί τι **cast** a thing **in** one's **teeth**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.142, 7.136}}; τὴν.. αὐθαδίαν.. μὴ 'πίπλησσέ μοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 80; ἐ. τινὶ τοῦτο, ὅτι.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 319d: with _accusative_ _of things_ only, τί τόδ᾽ ἐπέπληξα; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1730 (Lyric poetry): _absolutely_, [prev. author] “Aj.” 288, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 13.12, etc.}}
**III**. _intransitive_, **fall upon**, ἀρούραις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 1095.}}

1970:
Related to: ** πνίγ-ω** [ῑ],
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 68, +others, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.1.6, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.31, etc.}}: _imperfect_ ἔπνῑγον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1376:}}_future_ πνίξω (ἀπο-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 198, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 171}}; _Doric dialect_ _2nd pers._ _plural_ _future_ πνιξεῖσθε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 155:}}_aorist_ ἔπνιξα, _imperative_ πνῖξον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 27, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.92, Batrachomyomachia 158}}:—_passive_, _future_ πνῐγήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “περὶ φυσικῶν δυνάμεων - Scripta Minora” 1.17, (ἀπο-) 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1504, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 3.16; also ἀπο-πεπνίξομαι 4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Vitae Sophistarum” p.463 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus:}}_aorist_ ἐπνίχθην (ἀπ-) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 1.7}}; but ἐπνίγην [ῐ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Batrachomyomachia 148, 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 1.11, (ἀπ-) 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 159, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 512a, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 32.6, etc.:}}_perfect_ πέπνιγμαι, see #11.—The simple verb is less frequently than the _compound_ ἀποπνίγω:— **choke, throttle, strangle,** Sophr.[same place], etc.; of a doctor, πνίγων.. πικρότατα πόματα διδούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 522a}}; ἂν ὕλη πνίγῃ [τὸν σῖτον] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 17.14: proverbial, ὅταν τὸ ὕδωρ πνίγῃ, τί δεῖ ἐπιπίνει; 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1146a35}}:—_passive_, **to be choked, stifled,** ἐπνιγόμην τὰ σπλάγχνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1036; αἱ ὑστερικῶς πνιγόμεναι 2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 10.19.1.}}
**2** _impersonal_ πνίγει, of great heat, **it is stifling,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 941b4, 944a7.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, **vex, torment,** ἕνα χαλκοῦν ἀποβαλὼν αὑτὸν π. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.37 W.}}; ὃ δὲ μάλιστά με πνίγει variant in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Prom.” 17}}; **oppress** by exactions, 'squeeze', {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Misopogon” 368c.}}
**II** **cook in a close-covered vessel, bake, stew,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.92; δικίδιον.. ἐν λοπάδι πεπνιγμένον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 511; πεπνιγμένος 5th c.BC: Metagenes Comicus 6. 9.}}
**III** **drown** {{Refs|compare (πνῖξις 11)}}, in _passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.7.25: _metaphorically_, of plants, τὰ φυτὰ.. τοῖς πολλοῖς [ὕδασι] πνίγεται 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.9b.}}

1975:
** ἐπιπορ-εύομαι**,
**travel**, ψυχῆς πείρατα οὐκ ἂν ἐξεύροιο πᾶσαν -όμενος ὁδόν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 45}}; **march**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.12.4, +others: with _accusative_, τὴν χώραν}} **traverse**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 30.14: with _dative_, τοῖς ἀγροῖς 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 28 (assuming variant); ἐπὶ τοὺς τόπους “PLille” 3.78 (3rd c.BC).}}
**2**. ={ἐπιπάρειμι} (B) {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|4, of a general, -όμενος τὰ συστήματα παρεκάλει 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 11.12.1, compare 15.10.1, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Timoleon” 12}}; also ἐ. ἐπὶ τὸ πλῆθος **come before** the assembly, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.9.2; πρός τινα NT.Luke.8.4}}: _metaphorically_, **go** or **run through**, τῇ διανοίᾳ, τῇ ὄψει, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.470a.}}
**3**. Astron., **reach**, τινὶ τῶν κέντρων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 99: with _accusative_, ἡλίου τὸν Κριὸν ἐ}}. **begins to traverse** the Ram, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 5.172c.}}
**4**. ἐ. τὸν ἱστόν, ={ἐποίχομαι} (which see), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Ephorus Historicus 5J.}}
**5**. **take legal proceedings against** a person, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.96.10 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}

1976:
** ἐπιρράπτω**,
_aorist 2_ ἐπέρρᾰφον {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 9.3, +others}}:—**sew** or **stitch on**, τι ἐπὶ ἱμάτιον {{NT+4th c.AD+|NT.Mark.2.21: _metaphorically_, δόλον δόλῳ 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 42.315.}}
**2**. **sew up**, in _passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).579.}}

1977:
** ἐπιρρίπτ-ω**
(ἐπῐρίπτω {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.128 (1st c.AD: Automedon Epigrammaticus))}}, **cast at**, ὅτε μοι χαλκήρεα δοῦρα Τρῶες ἐπέρριψαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.310}}; διώκων ἐ. ἑαυτόν **throws** himself **upon** his prey, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 629b20}}; Βρούτῳ τὴν αὑτοῦ φοινικίδα ἐ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 22}}; χεῖρα ἐ., Latin _manum injecit,_ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Anthologia Graeca 9.84 (1st c.AD: Antiphanes Macedo Epigrammaticus): _metaphorically_, ἐ. πλάνας τινί 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 738}}; ψευδεῖς αἰτίας ἐ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.12}}; τὴν μέριμναν ἐπὶ [θεόν] {{NT|NT.1Pet.5.7}}; **inflict**, πολλὰσκληρὰ.. ἐπιρριφήσεται, with _dative_, Nech.in {{Refs|“Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 7.146.}}
**2**. **apply** a plaster or fomentation, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.50 (_passive_), 69; σκεπάσματα 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.88.}}
**3**. _passive_, -όμενα σκιρρώματα **spreading over the surface**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.42.}}
**4**. **requisition**, ἔργα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 5.249 (2nd c.BC); ἱερεῖα τρέφειν [prev. work] 183.}}
**5**. _metaphorically_ in _passive_, **to be imminent**, οὐ βραχὺς ἐπέρριπτο κίνδυνος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.594.}}
**II**. **throw out opinions**, ἀδιορίστως ἐ. περὶ τῶν λοιπῶν, variant for -, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 986a34.}}

1978:
** ἐπί-σημος**,
_Doric dialect_**ἐπί-σᾱμος**, ον, (σῆμἀ **serving to distinguish**, τοῖς δ᾽ ὄνομ᾽ ἄνθρωποι κατέθεντ᾽ ἐ. ἑκάστῳ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 19.3.}}
**II**. **having a mark, inscription** or **device on** it, especially of money, **stamped, coined**, χρυσὸς ἐ., opposed to ἄσημος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.41; ἀργύριον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13; χρυσίον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.40, compare “IG” 12.301, +others}}; so ἀναθήματα οὐκ ἐ. offerings **with** no **inscription on them**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.51}}; ἀσπίδες ἐ., opposed to λεῖαι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 12.280, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 526.}}
**II.2**. of epileptic patients, **bearing the marks of the disease**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἱερῆς νούσου” 8}}; of cattle, **spotted** or **striped**, {{LXX|LXX.Gen.30.42.}}
**II.3**. **notable, remarkable**, μνῆμ᾽ ἐ. a **speaking** remembrance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1258 (_anapaest meter_); ξυμφοραί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 543}}; εὐνή, λέχος, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HF” 68, “Or.” 21; τύχη [prev. author] “Med.” 544; χαρακτήρ [prev. author] “Hec.” 379}}; τάφος ἐπισημότατος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.43; τιμωρία 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 129; τόποι “IG” 12(3).326.42 (Thera, _superlative_)}}; of garments, **fine**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 695.39 (5th c.BC: Magnes Comicus Mae., 2nd c.BC)}}; and of persons, ἐ. σοφίην **notable** for wisdom, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.20; ἐ. ἐν βροτοῖς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 103; ἐ. ξένοι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 543}}: in bad sense, **conspicuous, notorious**, ἐς τὸν ψόγον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 249}}; δέσμιος ἐ. {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Matt.27.16; διὰ δημοκοπίαν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 14; ἐπὶ τῇ μοχθηρίᾳ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Rh.Pr.” 25.}}
**II.4**. **significant**, οὐκ ἐ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 1.59, 3.32.}}
**III**. _adverb_ **-μως**{{LXX+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.39.9, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Symmachus LXX.Psa.73(74).4, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 6.1.8}}: _comparative_ **-ότερον**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 9.762}}; **-οτέρως** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 2.9}}: _superlative_ **-ότατα**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 43.}}

1979:
** ἐπισῑτ-ισμός**, ὁ,
**furnishing oneself with provisions, foraging**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.2.26, “An.” 1.5.9.}}
**2**. **stock** or **store of provisions**,{{NT+4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 7.1.9, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.7, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 3.5.3, NT.Luke.9.12}}; ἔχοντες ἐπισιτις μὸν ἡμερῶν μ Philipp. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.157; ἐ. ἀννώνης “OGI” 200.15 (Axum, 4th c.AD): in _plural_, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 6.7.1.}}

1980:
** ἐπισκέπτ-ομαι**,
={ἐπισκοπέω}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.1, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 710, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 5.89, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.129c, etc.}}
**2**. **pass in review**: hence, **number** a host, {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.15.4.}}

1982:
** ἐπισκῐ-άζω**,
**throw a shade upon, overshadow**, τῇ [πτέρυγι] τὴν Ἀσίην {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.209. compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 780a30, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 2.18.3, NT.Matt.17.5: with _dative_, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sensu” 79, NT.Mark.9.7}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.262, +others}}; opposed to φωτίζειν, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.141:}} —_middle_, -σκιάζεσθαι τὸν ἥλιον **to ward off** the sun's rays, {{Refs|“Geoponica” 5.29.3}}: _metaphorically_, **conceal, obscure**, ἀλήθειαν πλάσμασι μυθικοῖς {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.41, etc.}}; τὰ δεινὰ ἑτέροις ὀνόμασιν ἐ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Juncus Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.50.95}}; τὴν θωπείαν, τὸν βίον, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 11, variant in “Cal.” 1}}:—_passive_, τῇ εὐγενεία {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.10.3}}; λαθραῖον ὄμμ᾽ ἐπεσκιασμένη **keeping a hidden** watch, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 914.}}
**2**. **darken, obscure**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.223 (_passive_)}}: _metaphorically_, ἀφροσύνη ἐ. ψυχήν {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.685, +others}}
**3**. of the Divine presence, **overshadow** for protection, etc., τινί {{LXX|LXX.Psa.90(91).4}}; ἐπὶ τὴν κεφαλήν τινος{{NT|[prev. work] 139(140).8; δύναμις ὑψίστου ἐπισκιάσει σοι NT.Luke.1.35.}}
**4**. _passive_, **to be weak-sighted**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 111.1.}}

1983:
** ἐπισκοπ-έω**,
_future_ -σκέψομαι, later **-σκοπήσω**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 103.8:}}_aorist_ -εσκεψάμην, later **-εσκόπησα**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 44, 59}}: _perfect_ ἐπέσκεμμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 14, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epinomis” 990a}}; also in _passive_ sense, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 299a10, “PA” 692a18}}:—**look upon** or **at, inspect, observe**, ἱστορίας καὶ τἆλλα ἔγγραφα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Milet.” 3.155 (2nd c.BC)}} (also in _middle_, ἐ. τὸ περίχωμα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PLille” 1v 27 (3rd c.BC), etc.)}}; **regard**, τἄμ᾽ ἐ. κακά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 869}}; of tutelary gods, Θηβαΐας ἐπισκοποῦντ᾽ ἀγυιάς, of Bacchus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1136 (Lyric poetry); Ἴλιον.. ἐπισκοπεῖ σεμνὸς Ποσειδῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1414, compare “Ph.” 661 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὦ Δῆμ᾽, ἐναργῶς ἡ θεός σ᾽ ἐπισκοπεῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1173, compare 1186}}; also of a ruler, ἐ. τὴν πολιτείαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 506b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 4.6 (so in _middle_, θαμὰ ἐπεσκοπεῖτο ἡμᾶς}} **observed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 207a): followed by Relat., ἐ. καὶ ἀναμετρήσαντες ὅσῳ ἐλάσσων ὁ χῶρος γέγονε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.109; ἐ. πῶς ἔχει 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 451c; τόδε ἐπίσκεψαι εἴ τι λέγω [prev. author] “Phd.” 87b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.22; πότερον.. ἤ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 518a; τίς εἴη.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.2.4, compare “Smp.” 1.12}}; ἐ. μή.. **take care** lest, {{NT|NT.Heb.12.15.}}
**2**. **visit**, ὦ θάνατε, νῦν μ᾽ ἐπίσκεψαι μολών {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 854}}; **visit** as a friend (ironically), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.12}}; especially **visit** the sick, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.25, “Mem.” 3.11.10}}; of the physician, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 4.2.4}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.56, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.2, 14.633:}} —_passive_, εὐνὴν ὀνείροις οὐκ ἐπισκοπουμένην **visited** not by dreams, i.e. sleepless, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 13.}}
**3**. of a general, **inspect, review**, τὰς τάξεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.2}}; τὰ ὅπλα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 6.3.21, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 296.}}
**4**. **consider, reflect, meditate**, ὅ τι ἂν μέλλῃς ἐρεῖν, πρότερον ἐπισκόπει τῇ γνώμῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.41}}; also ἐ. πρός τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 924d; περί τινος [prev. author] “Prt.” 348d, +others, 1st c.AD: Cebes Philosophus 35.5; ὑπέρ τινος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.15.2; σαυτὸν ἐ. ὅστις εἴης 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.24; ἐ. τίς.., ποία τις.. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1274b32; πότερον.. [prev. work] 1276b16}}:—_middle_, **examine with oneself**, **meditate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 91d; εἰς τὸ ἀληθὲς ἐ. τι [prev. author] “Phlb.” 61e, compare 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 219.8, 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 46:}} —_passive_, _perfect_ (see.above).
**5**. **exercise the office of** ἐπίσκοπος, variant in {{NT|NT.1Pet.5.2.}}

1984:
** ἐπισκοπ-ή**, ἡ,
**watching over, visitation**, of God, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Num.16.29, NT.Luke.19.44.}}
**II**. **office of** ἐπίσκοπος, {{NT|NT.1Tim.3.1, “Codex Justinianus [4th-6th c.AD]” 1.3.35 “Intr.”, etc.}}: generally, **office**, {{LXX|LXX.Psa.108(109).8.}}
**II.2**. ={ἐπίσκεψις}{{LXX|3, LXX.Num.14.29, +others}}

1985:
** ἐπίσκοπ-ος**, ὁ,
**A** (σκοπός 1) **one who watches over, overseer, guardian**, ἦ γὰρ ὄλωλας ἐπίσκοπος, ὅς τέ μιν αὐτὴν ῥύσκευ (i.e. τὴν πόλιν), of Hector, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.729, ἐ... ὁδαίων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.163}}; ἐπίσκοποι ἁρμονιάων **watchers** **over** compacts, of the gods, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.255; νεκροῦ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 217; σῆς ἕδρας [prev. author] “OC” 112}}; ἐ. ὀϊστῶν, of an archer, variant in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 24.107}}; in education, **tutor**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 795d; ἐ. σωφροσύνης καὶ ὕβρεως [prev. work] 849a: with _dative_, ἀγυιαῖς ἔσσῃ καὶ λιμένεσσιν ἐ. 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Dianam” 39}}; especially of tutelary gods (compare ἐπισκοπέὠ, Παλλὰς ἐ. {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 4.3; Δίκη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 872e; Κλειὼ ἐ. χερνίβων 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 45}}; Χάριτες Μινυᾶν ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 14.3; θεοὶ ἐ. ἀγορᾶς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 272}}; πατρῴων δωμάτων ἐ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ch.” 126}}; τὸ δεινὸν.. φρενῶν ἐπίσκοπον **guardian** of the mind, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eu.” 518 (Lyric poetry)}}; νυχίων φθεγμάτων ἐ., of Bacchus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1148 (lyr); Ἐρινύες “IG” 12(9).1179.33 (Euboea, 2nd c.AD)}}; [Χριστὸς] ἐ. τῶν ψυχῶν {{NT+5th c.BC+|NT.1Pet.2.25: rarely with _dative_, πᾶσι γὰρ ἐ. ἐτάχθη.. Νέμεσις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 717d.}}
**A.2**. **scout, watch**, c._dative_, ἐ. Τρώεσσι, νήεσσιν ἡμετέρῃσιν, **one set to watch** them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.38, 342.}}
**A.3**. **supervisor, inspector**, sent by Athens to subject states, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1023, “IG” 12.10,11}}; of **municipal** **officials** at Rhodes, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work]12(1).49.42 (2nd-1st c.BC): generally, “PPetr.” 3 P. 75 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}
**A.4**. **ecclesiastical superintendent**, ἐπίσκοποι καὶ διάκονοι {{NT|NT.Phil.1.1, cf. NT.Act.20.28, NT.1Tim.3.2, etc.}}

1986:
** ἐπι-σπάω**,
**draw** or **drag after** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121.δ}}; ἦγ᾽ ἐπισπάσας κόμης by the hair, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 116, compare “Tr.” 882, “Andr.” 710}}:— _middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.7.14:}} —_passive_, ἐπισπασθῆναι τῇ χειρί with the hand, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.130.}}
**2**. _metaphorically_, **bring on, cause**, τοσόνδε πλῆθος πημάτων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 477.}}
**3**. **pull to**, τὴν θύραν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.4.36}}; compare ἐπισπαστήρ: ἐπισπασθέντος τοῦ βρόχου **being drawn tight**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.139.}}
**4**. **attract**, **gain, win**, πέποιθα τοῦτ᾽ ἐπισπάσειν κλέος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 769}}:—frequently in _middle_, ἐπισπᾶσθαι κέρδος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.72; εὔνοιαν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.98.9; χάριν “SIG” 685.40 (5th c.BC: Magnes Comicus Mae., 2nd c.BC)}}; ἔχθραν {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.340 (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus)}}; **welcome**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.384}}; ἐπισπᾶσθαι πώγωνα **get one** a beard, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “JTr.” 16}}; **induce**, ὕπνον ἐκπώμασιν {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 56.26}}; **attract**, σίδηρον {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 1.}}
**5**. **draw on**, **allure, persuade**, τὴν ψυχήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 420a}}:—_middle_, ὁ λόγος.. ἂν ἐπισπάσαιτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.44, compare 5.111}}; ἐ. ἡ πέρδιξ [τὸν θηρεύοντα] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 613b19}}; θάτερον παρεμπῖπτον ἐπεσπάσατο.. τὸ ἕτερον ἐπινόημα **induced, provoked**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 137}} G.: c._infinitive_, **induce** to do, ἐπισπάσασθαι [ἂν] αὐτοὺς ἡγεῖτο προθυμήσεσθαι he thought it would **induce, invite** them to make the venture, uncertain reading in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.9}}; ἐπισπᾶσθαί τινα ἐμπλησθῆναι δακρύων τὰ ὄμματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.5.10; ἐ. τοὺσπολεμίους ἐφ᾽ ἑαυτόν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Philopoemen” 18, compare “Mar.” 11, 21, 26}}; but τοὺς πολεμίους εἰς τόπους **allure, entice**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.110.2, etc.:}} —_passive_, ἐπισπώμενον εἰς τἀναντία πολλάκις ἅμα though often he **is being drawn** in opposite directionsatonce, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 863e}}; φοβοῦμαι μὴ πάντες.. ἐπισπασθῶσιν πέρα τοῦ συμφέροντος [πολεμῆσαι] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 5.19; πολύ τι μᾶλλον ἐπεσπάσμεθα “OGI” 223.18 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἐπεσπάσθην φιλονεικεῖν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Demetrius Lacon Philosophus “Herc.” 1055.23F.}}
**6**. _middle_, **absorb**, τὰ σιτία -σπᾶται τὴν ὑγρότητα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 868b30; τὰ ἐριναστὰ [σῦκα] ἐ. τὸν ὀπόν 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 2.9.12}}; **quaff**, of a drinker, ἀπνευστὶ ἐ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.500, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 5.4}}; of infants, **suck**, γάλα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.88}}; of cupping instruments, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 22}}; **draw in**, πνεῦμα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3.13}}:—_passive_, of air, **to be sucked** **in**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 931b22.}}
**7**. _middle_, **draw in, call in**, Πύρρον {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.6.5}}; φυλακὴν καὶ βοήθειαν παρά τινος{{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. work] 7.6; μάρτυρας -ᾶται τοὺς μουσικούς 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 5.1425.8}}:—_passive_, **to be called in, forced to work**, εἴς τι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 27.4 (2nd c.BC).}}
**8**. in _passive_, of the sea, ἐπισπωμένη βιαιότερον **returning** **with a rush** after having retired, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.89.}}
**II**. **overturn**: hence _proverbial_, ὅλην τὴν ἅμαξαν ἐπεσπάσω you **have `upset** the apple-cart', {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pseudol.” 32.}}
**III**. _middle_, **draw the prepuce forward, become as** **if uncircumcised**, μὴ ἐπισπάσθω {{NT|NT.1Cor.7.18}}; of the nurse, ἐπισπάσθω τὴν ἀκροποσθίαν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.113.}}

1987:
** ἐπίστᾰμαι**, 2
_person_ **-ασαι**{{Refs 6th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 376, 982, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 629, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 296a, but ἐπίστᾳ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.80, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 86, 581, ἐπίστῃ 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1085, “PCair.Zen.” 41.19 (3rd c.BC), _Ionic dialect_ ἐπίστεαι ἐξ-) 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.135; _imperative_ ἐπίστασο [prev. work] 29, 209, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 840, 967, “PCair.Zen.” 57.4 (3rd c.BC), etc., but ἐπίσταο variant in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.209}}, _contraction_ ἐπίστω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 658, etc.}}; _subjunctive_ _Ionic dialect_ ἐπιστέωμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.134}}, _Attic dialect_ ἐπίστωμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 296a}}: _imperfect_ ἠπιστάμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 267, etc.; without augment ἐπίστατο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.60: 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus has ἐπ- 5.42 (variant ἠπ-), ἠπ- 3.139}}; _Ionic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἠπιστέατο or ἐπιστέατο {{Refs|8.132}}: _future_ ἐπιστήσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.320, etc.}}: _aorist 1_ ἠπιστήθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.15, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 687a.}}
**I**. **know** how to do, **be able** to do, **capable** of doing, c._infinitive_, οὐδέ οἱ ὀστέ᾽ ἐπιστήσονται Ἀχαιοὶ ἀλλέξαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.320, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.207, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 70, etc.: 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus has it both of intellectual power, ὅς τις ἐπίσταιτο ᾗσι φρεσὶν ἄρτια βάζειν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.92; ἐπιστάμεναι σάφα θυμῷ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.730}}; and of artistic skill, ὃς χερσὶν ἐπίστατο δαίδαλα πάντα τεύχειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.60}}: frequently in Trag. and _Attic dialect_, οὔπω σωφρονεῖν ἐπίστασαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 982, compare 1032, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 589; πένεσθαι δ᾽ οὐκ ἐ. δόμος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 962; ἐ... θεοὺς σέβειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 996, cf. Alcaeus566}}; κιθαρίζειν οὐκ ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 989, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 223d, “R.” 420e, +others: without}}_infinitive_, σῷζ ὅπως ἐπίστασαι as best you **can**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 376, compare “Eu.” 581.}}
**I.2**. **to be assured, feel sure** that.., τοῦτον ἐπίστανται πλεῖστα εἰδέναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 57, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.134, 139, 6.139, +others: followed by ὡς, [prev. author] 1.122.}}
**II**. with _accusative_, **understand** a matter, **know, be versed in** or **acquainted with**, πολλὰ δ᾽ ἐπίστατο ἔργα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.705, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.117; Μουσέων δῶρον 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 1; τὴν τέχνην 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.130; τὸ μέλλον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 373; ἐμπειρίᾳ ἐ. τὴν ναυτικήν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.10; τὰς φύσεις ὑμῶν [prev. author] 7.14; πάσας τὰς δημιουργίας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 598c}}; ἔγωγε γράμματ᾽ οὐδ᾽ ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 122}}; τὸ μὴ ἐ. γράμματα illiteracy, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“PRyl.” 73.19 (1st c.BC), etc.}}; ἐ. ἱερατικὰ καὶ Αἰγύπτια γράμματα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PTeb.” 291.41 (2nd c.AD)}}; ἐ. μύθους τοὺς Αἰσώπου **know** them **by heart**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61b, compare “Grg.” 484b: also with an}} _adverb_, Συριστὶ ἐ. **know** Syrian, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.31}}; with _accusative_ and _infinitive_ conjoined, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 276}}; with _infinitive_ to explanation the _accusative_, ἔργον δὲ μοῦνον ἐσθίειν ἐ. {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 7.24, compare 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 65.}}
**II.2**. after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, **know** as a fact, **know** **for certain**, ἐπισταμένοισι εὖ οὐκ ἄν τις λέγοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.8.ά, etc.}}; used convertibly with εἰδέναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 163b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 66b31, “Ph.” 184a10}}; even χάριν ἐ., ={χάριν εἰδέναι}, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 8.246c (but sometimes εἰδέναι is general, ἐπίστασθαι}} being confined to **scientific knowledge** (ἐπιστήμη), διὰ τὸ εἰδέναι τὸ ἐπίστασθαι ἐδίωκον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 982b21): frequently strengthened, εὖ ἐ}}. Hdt.[same place]; σαφῶς ἐ. {{Refs 6th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 840, etc.: most frequently with _accusative_, τὰ διαφέροντα ἐ. 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.19, etc.; also ἐ. περί τινος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.3, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.60; περὶ θεῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 795.4: followed by a dependent clause, τί σφιν χρήσηται ἐ. 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 772}}; ἐ. ὅτι.., or ἐ. τοῦτο, ὅτι.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.3, 156, etc.; ὡς.. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 599; τοῦτ᾽ ἐπίστασ᾽, ὡς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1370; ἐ. αὐτὸν οἷς ψωμίζεται 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 715, etc.}}
**II.3**. rarely, **know** a person, Ἀρίγνωτον γὰρ οὐδεὶς ὅστις οὐκ ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1278, compare 1st c.AD: Musonius Philosophus “fragment” 3p.12H., 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 1}}; τὸν Ἰησοῦν γινώσκω καὶ τὸν Παῦλον ἐ. {{NT|NT.Act.19.15}}; but ὁ παῖς τοὺς τεκόντας οὐκ ἐ. **does** not **know who** they **are**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 51.}}
**III**. with _participle_, in Prose and Trag., **know that** one is, has, etc., εὖ ἐ. αὐτὸς σχήσων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.42; ἐσθλὸς ὢν ἐπίστασο 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1399, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.44}}; also ὡς ὧδ᾽ ἐχόντων τῶνδ᾽ ἐ. σε χρή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 281}}; ὡς φανέν γε τοὔπος ὧδ᾽ ἐ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “OT” 848: with double _accusative_, ἑαυτοὺς Φαυστύλου ἠπιστάμεθα παῖδας (i.e. ὄντας) 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 7}}: c._accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1092, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 53.1.}}
**IV**. _present_ _participle_ ἐπιστάμενος, η, ον, frequently as _adjective_, **knowing, understanding, skilful**, ἀνδρὸς ἐ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.359; χαλεπὸν ἐ. περ ἐόντι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.80}}; καὶ μάλ᾽ ἐ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.313}}; even of a dancer's feet, θρέξασκον ἐπισταμένοισι πόδεσσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.599: also with _genitive_, φόρμιγγος ἐ. καὶ ἀοιδῆς}} **skilled, versed in** them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.406: and with _dative_, ἄκοντι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.282: hence}}
**IV.2**. _adverb_ ἐπιστᾰμένως **skilfully, expertly**,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|7.317, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 87, etc.}}; εὖ καὶ ἐ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.265, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.161, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 107; ἐ. πίνειν 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 212}}; also in Prose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.1.3, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 146.7, Vett. Val. 298.2}}: c._infinitive_, **with knowledge** how to.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 14.4. (Since ἐφίστημι τὸν νοῦν is used in the sense of ἐπίσταμαι}}, **attend, observe**, it is probably that ἐπίσταμαι is merely an old _middle_ form of ἐφίστημι,[{{Refs 4th c.BC+|compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 247b11 τῷ γὰρ ἠρεμῆσαι καὶ στῆναι τὴν διάνοιαν ἐπίστασθαι.. λέγομεν, and see at {ἐπίστασις} II.2.)}}]

1988:
** ἐπιστᾰτ-ης**, ου, ὁ,
(ἐφίσταμαι) **one who stands near** or **by**: hence, like{ἱκέτης}, **suppliant**, οὐ σύ γ᾽ ἂν.. σῷ ἐπιστάτῃ οὐδ᾽ ἅλα δοίης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.455.}}
**2**. in battle-order, **one's rear-rank man**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.50, 8.1.10, +others}}
**2.b**. also, **even numbers** in a λόχος, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 2.3, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 6.6.}}
**II**. **one who stands** or **is mounted upon**, ἁρμάτων ἐ., of a charioteer, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 702, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1147}}; ἐλεφάντων ἐ., of the driver, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.40.11.}}
**II.2**. **one who is set over, chief, commander**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 816 (815); ὅπλων [prev. author] “Pers.” 379}}; ποιμνίων ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 27}}; ἐρετμῶν ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1267}}; θύματος ἐ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hec.” 223}}; but ταύρων πυρπνόων ζεύγλῃσι **mastering** them with.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Med.” 478}}; ἐνόπτρων καὶ μύρων, of the Trojans, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Or.” 1112}}; ἐ. Κολωνοῦ, of a tutelary god, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 889}}; [καιρὸς] ἀνδράσιν μέγιστος ἔργου παντός ἐστ᾽ ἐ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 76}}; also in Prose, ἐ. γενέσθαι τῶν λόγων ἴσους καὶ κοινούς **judges**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.7}}; ποίας ἐργασίας ἐ.; Answ. ἐ. τοῦ ποιῆσαι δεινὸν λέγειν (where it ={ἐπιστήμων}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 312d; πραγμάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.121}}; ἐπιστάται ἄθλων **stewards** of games, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 949a, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 8.4}}; of a pilot, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Oec.” 21.3}}; **supervisor** of training, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 412a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.5.18 (_plural_); ἐ. τῶν παίδων “IG” 12(1).43 (from Rhodes)}}; τῶν ἐφήβων {{NT+1st c.BC+|“Inscription Prien.” 112.73 (1st c.BC): _vocative_ ἐπιστάτα, = “Rabbi, NT.Luke.5.5, +others}}
**III**. **president** of a board or assembly: at Athens, ἐ. τῶν πρυτάνεων **chairman** of βουλή and ἐκκλησία in 5th C, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 44.1}}, later, **keeper** of Treasury or Archives, {{Refs|“IG” 3.841, etc.}}; ἐ. τῶν προέδρων **chairman** of βουλή and ἐκκλησία from 4th C {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.39, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.9, etc.; ἐ. ὁ ἐκ τῶν προέδρων “IG” 22.204.31 (4th c.BC); in other Greek states, [prev. work]12(1).731 (from Rhodes), 12(7).515.116, 125 (from Amorgos), etc.; ἐ. τῶν νομοθετῶν [prev. work]22.222; τῶν δικα[στῶν] “LW” 1539 (from Erythrae).}}
**III.2**. **overseer, superintendent**, in charge of any public building or works, τοῦ νεὼ τοῦ ἐν πόλει, i.e. of the temple of Athena Polias, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 12.372; ἱεροῦ “UPZ” 42.22 (2nd c.BC)}}; ἐ. τῶν ἔργων **clerk** of the works, {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.114, LXX.Exo.1.11 (_plural_); τῶν δημοσίων ἔργων 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.14; τοῦ ναυτικοῦ [prev. work]222; τῆς Ἀκαδημείας 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Dem.fragment” 7; τοῦ Μουσείου “OGI” 104.4 (2nd c.BC)}}; τῶν κοπρώνων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.49.}}
**III.3**. **governor**, **administrator**, τῆς πόλεως {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 254.3 (Babylon, 2nd c.BC),compare “IG” 12(3).320.7 (Thera, 3rd c.BC), “OGI” 479.7 (Dorylaeum, 2nd c.AD)}}; κώμης local **magistrate,** {{Refs|Arch.Papyrus 4.38.}}
**III.4**. ={προστάτης}, Latin _patronus,_ {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 14.1317.}}
**IV**. in [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 437, ={χυτρόπους}, 3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristophanes Byzantinus Philosophus cited in 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1827.45; other explanations, [prev. passage], compareScholia Ar.[same place]: τοὐπιστάτου is from ἐπίστατος, ={πυρίστατος}, Anonymus cited in 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1827.56: uncertain meaning in “BpW” 1892.514}}]; compare ἐπίστατον.

1989:
** ἐπιστέλλω**,
**send to**, γράψας ἐς βυβλίον τάδε ἐπέστειλε ἐς Σάμον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.40; τοῖσι Ἕλλησι [prev. author] 7.239; ἡδίω.. ἂν εἶχον ὑμῖν.. ἐπιστέλλειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.14; ἐ. ἐπιστολάς τινι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.37, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 363b}}; **send a message**, τάδε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 770}}; especially by letter, **write word**, τοιαῦτα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.27}}; περί τινος ὡς ἀδικοῦντος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.38; ἐ. ὅτι.. [prev. work] 50, 99}}; τὰ ἐπισταλέντα ἐκ τῆς Σάμου the **news received** from Samos,{{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. work] 50; τὰ ὑπό τινος ἐπεσταλμένα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Artoxerxes” 21.}}
**2**. **enjoin, command**, τισί τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.37}}; τὸν ἄγγελον ἐπιστείλας ταῦτα ἔπεμψε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.32; τινὶ περί τινος [prev. work] 4.5.34}}: c._infinitive_, ἐ. τινὶ ἀπίστασθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.3; τινὶ ἐκμαθεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 863}}; ὁ Κῦρος αὐτῷ ἐπέστελλε πρὸς Πέρσας λέγειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.26}}; also ἐ. τινὰ ποιεῖν τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 106, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.5.1}}: without any case, **give orders** to do, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 205, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.72, etc.}}; **give orders in writing**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 24.13:}} — _passive_, ἔφη οὐδέν οἱ ἐπεστάλθαι ἄλλο ἢ ἀπαλλάσσεσθαι he **had received** **orders** to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.131; καί μοι ἐκ βασιλέος ὧδε ἐπέσταλται [prev. author] 6.97}}; αἷς ἐπέσταλται τέλος to whom the office **has been committed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 908, compare “Eu.” 743; τἀπεσταλμένα [prev. author] “Ch.” 779; κατὰ τὰ ἐ. ὑπὸ Δημοσθένους 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.8}}; ἀξιῶ ἐπισταλῆναί τισι c._infinitive_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PRyl.” 121.13 (2nd c.AD): with personal construction, ταῦτα ἐπεσταλμένοι}} **having received** these **instructions**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.37}}: in later writers, usually of **orders given in writing**, {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Act.15.20, 21.25, “SIG” 837.14 (2nd c.AD)}}, etc.; of **orders** for payment, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1304 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}

1990:
** ἐπιστήμ-ων**, ον,
_genitive_ ονος, (ἐπίσταμαι) **knowing, wise, prudent**, ἐ. βουλῇ τε νόῳ τε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.374; ἄρχοντες 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 21.5}}; ἐπιστήμων γὰρ εἶ, ={ἐπίστασαι γάρ}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 843.}}
**2**. **acquainted with** a thing, **skilled** or **versed in**, with _genitive_, κακῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 589}}; τῆς θαλάσσης, τοῦ ναυτικοῦ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.142, 8.45}}; τῆς τέχνης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 448b; τῶν τόπων “POxy.” 1469.12 (3rd c.AD)}}; also περί τινος or τι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 599b, “Sis.” 389e}}: with _neuter_ _adjective_, τὰ προσήκοντα ἐπιστήμων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.9, compare “Oec.” 2.16 (_superlative_).}}
**3**. with _infinitive_, **knowing how**, λέγειν τε καὶ σιγᾶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 276a, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 19.16}}: _comparative_ **-ονέστερος**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 174a}}. _adverb_ _comparative_ **-ονέστερον**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 3.14}}: _superlative_ **-ονέστατα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 534d.}}
**II**. **possessed of perfect knowledge**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Plt.” 301b, etc.; opposed to δοξαστής, τινός [prev. author] “Tht.” 208e, in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus}}, **scientifically versed in** a thing, {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 0.74b28, “Cat.” 11a33}}. _adverb_ -ονως, ἔχειν πρός τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 233c: λέγειν}} **with science, with art**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 207b}}; εἰπεῖν use **technical** or **scientific** terminology, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “Orationes” 26(14).97.}}

1992:
** ἐπιστολ-ή**, ἡ,
(ἐπιστέλλω) **anything sent by a messenger, message, order, commission**, whether verbal or in writing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.10, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.45, etc.}}; ἐξ ἐπιστολῆς by **command**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.50}}: used by Trag. always in _plural_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 3, “Pers.” 783, “Supp.” 1012, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 781, “OC” 1601, etc.}}; Πενθέως ἐπιστολαῖς **by** his **commands**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 442}}; τέκνων ἐπιστολὰς ἔγραψεν **commands about** her children, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hipp.” 858.}}
**2**. **letter**, ἐ. διαπέμπειν, ἀποδοῦναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.129, 7.10; λύειν [prev. author] 1.132; ἐ. ἔδωκεν ἀποδοῦναι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.27}}; πέμπειν τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 589 (_plural_)}}: in _plural_ of one letter, like{γράμματα}, Latin _litterae_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “IA” 111, 314, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.132, etc.}}; ὁ ἐπὶ τῶν ἐπιστολῶν.. τοῦ Ὄθωνος, Latin _ab epistulis Othoni_, his **secretary**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Otho” 9}}; νομογραφικὴ ἐ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1135.7 (1st c.BC).}}

1993:
** ἐπιστομ-ίζω**,
_Attic dialect_ _future_ **-ῐῶ**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 7.33}}: (στόμα):—**bridle, curb**, ἵππον conjecture in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.85}}; [δελφῖνας] {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 2.18}}: _metaphorically_, **curb, bridle**, τοὺς ἐχθρούς {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 845, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 7.33, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.110, NT.Tit.1.11; τὴν Ἰουδαίων νεωτεροποιίαν 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 17.10.1}}; **silence** a speaker, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.30, compare 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.191; οἷον ἐ. καὶ χαλινοῦντες τὸ φιλόφωνον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.967b}}:—_passive_, ἐπεστομίσθη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 482e.}}
**II**. of flute-players, ἐ. ἑαυτὸν φορβειᾷ καὶ αὐλοῖς **put on the mouthpiece** and flutes, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.713d}}; but ὁ αὐλὸς ἐ. {{Refs|[prev. author] Alcaeus2}}: hence, **gag**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc.” “Cond.” 7.}}
**III**. **throw on his face**, τινά {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pr.Im.” 10, “Cal.” 12.}}

1994:
** ἐπι-στρέφω**,
_perfect_ ἐπέστροφα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Diogenes Cynicus Philosophus (see. below 1.2a)}}:—**turn about, turn round**, νῶτον Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.141; δεῦρ᾽ ἐ. κάρα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 942, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 10.12; στροφεῖς 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Automatopoetica” 23.3}}; ἐ. τὰς ναῦς **tack** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (ἐπιστροφή 11.1), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.90}}; also, **put** an enemy **to flight**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.4.9}}; **wheel about**, τοὺς ἱππεῖς {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sulla” 19}}; **wheel through a right angle**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 10.5 (_active_ and _passive_), etc.; _intransitive_, [prev. work]12.11, etc.}}
**b**. _intransitive_, **turn about, turn round**, ἕλκε δ᾽ ἐπιστρέψας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.370}}; here only in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, and perhaps _transitive_, **whirl**, but see {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.103, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 566; ἀλλὰ πᾶς ἐπίστρεφε δεῦρο 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 422}}; of ships, **put about**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.47.8, 50.5}}; of a wild boar, **turn upon** the hunter, ἐπί τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 10.15}}; **return**, ἀπὸ τῆς στρατείας Epist. Philipp. in {{NT|“IG” 9(2).517.37 (from Larissa), cf. NT.Matt.12.44, etc.}}; of an illness, **recur**, _falsa lectio_ for{ὑπο-}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 124}}: as Hebraism, with _infinitive_, as periphrasis of πάλιν, ἐπιστρέψει.. εὐφρανθῆναι {{LXX|LXX.Deu.30.9, cf. LXX.2Esd.9.14, +others}}; so with καί and finite Verb, ἐπέστρεψεν καὶ ᾠκοδόμησεν{{LXX|LXX.2Ch.33.3, cf. LXX.Mal.1.4, +others}}
**2**. **turn towards**, νόημα {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1083; ἦθος κατά τινα [prev. author] 213}}; ἐ. τινά **turn** his **attention towards** one, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 11}}; τινὰ πρός τι, εἰς ἑαυτόν, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.21c,69f, compare 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 5.3.8}}; οἱ τὴν Ἑλλάδα ἐπεστροφότες ἐπὶ σοφίαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Diogenes Cynicus Philosophus “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 34.1}}; ἐ. πίστιν **press** a pledge **upon** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1182}}; ἐ. τὴν φάλαγγα **bring** it **into action**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 42: hence}}
**2.b**. _intransitive_, **turn** (oneself) **towards**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 8.12, etc.}}; ἐ. εἰς or πρὸς ἑαυτόν, of νοῦς, **reflect**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.3.1, 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 15}}; τὸ ἐπιστρέφον βαθρικόν the steps **leading to** the sarcophagus, Judeich **Altertümervon Hierapolis** {{Refs|152.}}
**3**. **turn** or **convert** from an error, **correct**, **cause to repent**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 5, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 16; πλημμελοῦντας [prev. author] “Cat.Mi.” 14}}; **warn**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.7.1}}; **coerce,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-6th c.AD: Codex Justinianus 4.20.15.1.}}
**3.b**. _passive_, **to be converted, return**, ἐπὶ Κύριον {{LXX|LXX.Deu.30.2}}; _intransitive_, **repent**,{{LXX+NT|LXX.Judg.5.19, +others, NT.Matt.13.15, NT.Luke.22.32, etc.}}
**3.c**. Philos., **cause** **to return** to the source of Being, τινὰς εἰς τὰ ἐναντία καὶ τὰ πρῶτα {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.1.1; τι πρὸς τἀγαθόν 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 144}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.2.4, 5.2.1}}; τὸ προϊὸν ἀπό τινος -στρέφεται πρὸς ἐκεῖνο ἀφ᾽ οὗ πρόεισιν {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 31; πρὸς τὸ ἕν 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 27}}:—also _intransitive_ in _active_, ἐ. εἰς ἑαυτόν {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.3.6}}; τὸ γεννηθὲν φύσει πρὸς τὸ γεννῆσαν ἐ. {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Sententiae ad intelligibilia ducentes” 13}}; οὐδὲν τῶν σωμάτων πρὸς ἑαυτὸ πέφυκεν ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 15.}}
**4**. **curve, twist**, ὀδύνη σε περὶ τὰ σπλάγχν᾽ ἔοικ᾽ ἐπιστρέφειν variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1131; ἐ. ἐπισκύνιον “Anthologia Graeca” 11.376.8 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus)}}:—_passive_, **to be distorted**, ἢν τράχηλος ἐπιστραφῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 4.35}}; of hair, **curl**, οἷς ἐπέστραπται τὸ τρίχιον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 963b10}}; ἐπεστραμμένος, of a tree, **crooked**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.8.4}}; of fir-needles, **bent**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 3.9.6.}}
**II**. _middle_ and _passive_, especially in _aorist 2_ _passive_ ἐπεστράφην [ᾰ], also ἐπεστρέφθην {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 4.179}}: _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _future_ _passive_ **-στραφησεῖται**{{Refs|“GDI” 3089.27 (from Callatis)}}:—**turn oneself round, turn about**, ἤϊε ἐπιστρεφόμενος **constantly turning**, as if to look behind one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.156: and with _accusative_, πολλὰ θάλαμον ἐξιοῦσ᾽ ἐπεστράφη}} **turned to gaze** **on** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 187}}; so of a lion retreating, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 629b15}}; δι᾽ οὗ πάσας ἐπιστρέφεσθαι τὰς περιφοράς by which all the revolving spheres **are turned**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 616c}}; δόξα τῇδ᾽ ἐπεστράφη thus **turned about, changed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1111.}}
**II.2**. **go back-and forwards**, πάντῃ {{Refs|“Hymni Homerici” 27.10}}; κατ᾽ ἄλσος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 508: with _accusative_, γαῖαν ἐπιστρέφεται}} **wanders over** the earth, with collateral sense of **observing, studying** it, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 753, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 648; so ἐ. ὀρέων κορυφάς 6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 2.4}}: also with _accusative_ loci, **turn to** a place, πόθεν γῆς τῆσδ᾽ ἐπεστράφης πέδο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 83, compare 89, 768, “Ion” 352 (also εἰς χώρας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 4.13): c._accusative_ _cognate_, [διεξόδους] ἐπιστρέφεσθαι}} **walk in.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 247a}}; of the sun, **revolve**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 584.}}
**II.3**. **turn the mind towards, pay attention to, regard** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|compare (ἐπιστροφή 11.3), τινός 6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 96, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 599, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.15 O., “Anthologia Graeca” 5.47 (2nd-3rd c.AD: Rufinus Epigrammaticus)}}; τῶν ἰδίων οὐδὲν ἐ. {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 440; εἴς τι 3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “in librum de sensu commentarium - pars” 57.18}}: _absolutely_, **return to oneself, pay attention**, ἐπιστραφείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.88; οὐκ ἦλθες,.. οὐδ᾽ ἐπεστράφης 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 400}}; οὐκ ἐπεστράφη, ={οὐκ ἐφρόντισε} (just above), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.136, compare 10.9, “Anthologia Graeca” 11.319 (1st c.AD: Automedon Epigrammaticus).}}
**II.3.b**. **conduct oneself, behave**, ἀξίως τᾶς τιμᾶς {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 539 A 22 (Decrees Amphict., 3rd c.BC).}}
**II.4**. c._accusative_, θεοῦ νιν κέλευσμ᾽ ἐπεστράφη **turned against** her, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 101 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.5**. _perfect_ _participle_ _passive_ ἐπεστραμμένος, ={ἐπιστρεφής}, **earnest, vehement**, λέγειν ἐπεστραμμένα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.62; ἀφέλεια -στραμμένη 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.7.1.}}

1995:
** ἐπιστροφ-ή**,ἡ,
**turning about**, τῆς τοῦ ἀτράκτου δίνης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 620e}}; **twisting**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.13.3}}; of strands, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 58.15; τῶν σχοινίων 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 25 (_plural_); ἡ εἴσω ἐ. τῶν δακτύλων 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 1.23.}}
**2**. **bending** of a bow, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.5.22.}}
**3**. **curve, winding** of a bay, {{Refs|[prev. work] 33}}; of a river, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Alm.” 8.1.}}
**II**. _intransitive_, **turning** or **wheeling about**, δαΐων ἀνδρῶν ἐπιστροφαί, i.e. hostile men **turning to bay**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1045 (Lyric poetry)}}; **tossing**, of a restless patient, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 7.83 (_plural_)}}; μυρίων ἐπιστροφαὶ κακῶν **renewed assaults** of ills unnumbered, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 537 (Lyric poetry), compare 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 7.17.5; especiallyin military evolutions, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.23.3, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Philopoemen” 7}}; **wheeling** **through a right angle**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 10.4, etc. (but, as a general term, αἱ ἐ. τῶν ἵππων [prev. work]7.2,compare 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 16.7)}}; of ships, **putting** **about, tacking**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.90, 91}}; ἐξ ἐπιστροφῆς by **a sudden wheel**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.76.5, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Timoleon” 27}}; but ἐξ ἐπιστροφῆς παθεῖν to have **a relapse**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 251.}}
**II.2**. **turn** of affairs, **reaction, counter-revolution**, μή τις ἐ. γένηται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.71}}; **result, end**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.32.15 (uncertain reading).}}
**II.3**. **attention** **paid to** a person or thing (ἐπιστρέφω {{Refs|11.3), ξενοτίμους δωμάτων ἐ}}. **respect** for guests, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 548}}; πρὸ τοῦ θανόντος τήνδ᾽ ἔθεσθ᾽ ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 134}}; ὧν ἐ. τις ἦν to whom any **regard** was due, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 671}}; so ἐπιστροφῆς ἄξιον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.9; παραμυθέεσθαι μετ᾽ ἐπιστροφῆς καὶ ὑποδέξιος 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ εὐσχημοσύνης” 16}}; ἐ. ποιεῖσθαι Philipp. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 12.1, compare 19.306, etc.}}; ἐ. ἔχειν τινός {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 836; περί τινος 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 3.187, etc.}}; ἐπιστροφῆς τυγχάνειν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.4.4, etc.}}
**II.3.b**. Philos., **turning towards**, πρὸς τὰ τῇδε {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.3.4}}; ψυχὴ καταδεῖται πρὸς τὸ σῶμα τῇ ἐ. τῇ πρὸς τὰ πάθη τὰ ἀπ᾽ αὐτοῦ {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Sententiae ad intelligibilia ducentes” 7.}}
**II.4**. **moving up and down in** a place, mostly in _plural_, πατρῴων δωμάτων ἐπιστροφαί **the range** of them, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 648}}; οἷσιν οὐκ ἐπιστροφαί men who have no **business here**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 440}}; βούνομοι ἐ. **haunts** of the grazing herds, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 249; so Κίλιξ δὲ χώρα καὶ Σύρων ἐπιστροφαί (conjecture for Σηρῶν ἐνστροφαί) [prev. work] 271.}}
**II.5** **intentness, vehemence,** ἐπιστροφὴν εἶχεν ὁ λόγος καὶ ἔρρωτο {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.21.5; θρασυτέρᾳ τῇ ἐ. χρήσασθαι [prev. work] 2.5.2.}}
**II.5.b** **gravity** of deportment, ἡ ἐ. τοῦ εἴδους {{Refs|[prev. author] “Im.” 2.16.}}
**II.6** **correction, reproof,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.55b.}}
**II.7** **conversion,** {{NT+5th c.AD+|NT.Acts.15.3; ἡ πρὸς θεὸν ἐ. 5th c.AD: Hierocles Platonicus Philosophus “in Carmen Aureum - FPG” 24p.473M.}}
**II.8** in Philos., **return** to the source of Being, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.2.4; ἡ ἐ. πρὸς αὑτόν [prev. author] 5.3.6, compare 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 31}}; [ἡ ἐ.] τοῦ προελθόντος ἐπάνοδος εἰς τὸ γεννῆσαν {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 75; ἡ ἐ. τῆς ἐκστάσεώς ἐστιν ἐπανόρθωσις [prev. work] 61.}}
**II.9** in Logic, **conversion** of a proposition, ἡ σὺν ἀντιθέσει ἐ. **the contraposition,** Suppl.ad {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Parmenidem commentarii” p.1004S.}}

1996:
** ἐπισυν-άγω** [ᾰ],
**collect and bring to** a place, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.75.2 (_passive_), 5.97.3, Wilcken “Chr.” 11 A 5 (2nd c.BC)}}; **gather together,** {{LXX+NT|LXX.Gen.6.16, +others, NT.Matt.23.37, etc.}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 90.23 (Rosetta, 2nd c.BC), “Placita Philosophorum” 3.4.1, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.338; οἱ -συνηγμένοι ἐν Ξόει Βοιωτοί “Supp.epigram” 2.871 (Egypt, 2nd c.BC)}}; **to be combined,** τὰ ἐκ τῶν πληθυντικῶν εἰς τὰ ἑνικὰ -όμενα {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 24.1}}; ἐπισυναχθέντες τόκοι **accumulated** interest, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PGrenf.” 2.72.8 (3rd-4th c.AD),compare “PFlor.” 1.46.14 (2nd c.AD)}}; ἐπισυναγόμενος ἀριθμός **counted up,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 43.}}
**II** **bring in,** in a discussion, περιττὸν-ειν καὶ ταύτας {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “Academicorum Index” 28.}}
**II.2** Astrology texts, ={ἐπισυμφέρω}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 288.29.}}
**III** **conclude, infer,** συλλογιζόμενοι τὸν μεταξὺ χρόνον ἐπισυνάγουσιν ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Hypotyposis astronomicarum positionum” 5.54.}}

